//www.worldlabs.ai/blog/amd-announcement"}, "items": [{"insight": "AMD backed World Labs via AMD Ventures in 2024 and again in the 2026 $1B round, then agreed to acquire it in Sept 2026: strategic investor turned acquirer.", "type": "exit", "evidence_quote": "World Labs has signed a definitive agreement to join AMD.", "source_url": "https://www.worldlabs.ai/blog/amd-announcement", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}]}, {"slug": "chonghao-huang", "name": "Chonghao Huang", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 12.0, "n": 1, "nk": 1, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Huang went from Vice President (2025-07 to 2026-04 archived team pages) to Partner, Seed on the rebranded wndr.vc site by 2026-11", "q": "Chonghao Huang | Vice President", "u": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260415id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team"}, "items": [{"insight": "Huang went from Vice President (2025-07 to 2026-04 archived team pages) to Partner, Seed on the rebranded wndr.vc site by 2026-11", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Chonghao Huang | Vice President", "source_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260415id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}]}, {"slug": "kanjun-qiu", "name": "Kanjun Qiu", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 12.0, "n": 1, "nk": 1, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Imbue CEO Kanjun Qiu was a WndrCo venture partner from at least mid-2024 until between 2025-11 and 2026-04", "q": "Kanjun Qiu | Venture Partner", "u": "https://web.archive.org/web/20251115id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team"}, "items": [{"insight": "Imbue CEO Kanjun Qiu was a WndrCo venture partner from at least mid-2024 until between 2025-11 and 2026-04", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Kanjun Qiu | Venture Partner", "source_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20251115id_/https://www.wndrco.com/team", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}]}, {"slug": "lux-capital", "name": "Lux Capital", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Flapping Airplanes in one line: Lux bought into a lab it did not seed. It co-led the $1B Series A in Oct 2026 with Index, about nine months after a seed led by GV, Sequoia and Index. Brandon Reeves is the only Lux name on it, and he made an efficiency argument: getting \"more out of what we have\". Lux now wins late-stage lab allocations with Fund IX dry powder, not only founding checks. Why they win: Lux is often the first institutional check into science-first teams before the AI consensus forms. Examples: Hugging Face in 2019, three years before Sequoia's first check in the 2022 Series C; Together AI's seed in 2023; and Sakana AI's founding round in 2024. Every non-founder investor holds the same \"Partner\" title, and the AI work is spread across a young bench (Isford, Reeves, Jiang) rather than run by the co-founders alone. The AI repeat-pair: Grace Isford and Brandon Reeves co-sign Lux's AI lab and agent deals: Sakana (Jan 2024), Phonic (Apr 2025) and Crosby (Mar 2026). Portfolio leverage: Lux brings its own portfolio CEOs into new seeds. In Phonic's seed, the co-investors were Qasar Younis (Applied Intuition), Clem Delangue (Hugging Face) and Erik Bernhardsson (Modal). Delangue also wrote an angel check into Sakana. Constraint: Fund IX is $1.5B, the largest in Lux's history, and was \"significantly oversubscribed\". Its stated scope runs across AI, advanced manufacturing, energy, biotech and defense. So AI competes for capital with defense and roll-up bets like Onodrim's reported $300M Series A (single source,). In mega-rounds for labs, Lux tends to participate rather than lead: it followed Thrive in Physical Intelligence's seed and CapitalG in its Series B. What they'd never do (inference, not sourced): back a consensus app-layer company without a science or IP edge. Grace Isford's stated filter requires \"true technology\" with IP or a differentiated approach.", "score": 11.67, "n": 27, "nk": 2, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Index's own Series A post claims the lead and never mentions Lux. Lux's news page also has no Flapping Airplanes post, and Lux has no company page for it (404). Lux was the second co-lead, without a board seat as far as reported.", "q": "we're thrilled to double down and lead their $1B million Series A", "u": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Index's own Series A post claims the lead and never mentions Lux. Lux's news page also has no Flapping Airplanes post, and Lux has no company page for it (404). Lux was the second co-lead, without a board seat as far as reported.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "we're thrilled to double down and lead their $1B million Series A", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lux builds its own new labs: Flourish was incubated in-house and raised about $500M from inception. Lux was first money by construction, the way it got into Valthos.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "incubated inside Lux and raised half a billion from inception", "source_url": "https://www.luxcapital.com/news/lux-q1-2026-report", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "WashU found Lux bottom-up, through an overlapping position in an early automated-manufacturing company, not through fund marketing. The company is unnamed.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "that's how we originally found Lux", "source_url": "https://blog.riskgaming.com/p/shredding-the-lp-asset-management-playbook-with-scott-wilson-cio-of-washington-university-in-st-louis", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ryan-kim", "name": "Ryan Kim", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He sits at the junction of two BCV motions: AI-native services seeds, where BCV leads and is often first. Crosby was incubated through a BCV EIR. He also led the Adaptive and Corridor seeds. Allocation into hot research-team rounds: Simile at the A, Flapping Airplanes at the A. His memo voice is thesis-heavy and history-minded: Baumol's cost disease and the 1880s electric-motor story (Adaptive) \"regulatory arbitrage\" (his bio) the \"diffusion gap\" (Corridor) What constrains him. On the frontier-lab side he is a late, non-lead allocator. Flapping: BCV came in at the $1B A, about 9 months after Index, GV and Sequoia co-led the seed (companies/flapping-airplanes.md). Simile: BCV entered at the Index-led A, after A co-led the seed. He has no lab or semiconductor seed of his own on record. Five of his seven CMS records carry a co-signer, mostly Melas-Kyriazi. Only Flapping and Corridor are solo, so his independent authority is recent. What he'd never do (by inference from the memos). Back a copilot sold into an incumbent workflow. His services memos argue the opposite: become the provider. \"you cannot re-pour concrete from the outside\".", "score": 11.67, "n": 24, "nk": 2, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "BCV attributes Flapping Airplanes to Ryan Kim alone, filed as an Infrastructure deal; his only infra name.", "q": "Improving AI efficiency with radical new techniques", "u": "https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/flapping-airplanes/"}, "items": [{"insight": "BCV attributes Flapping Airplanes to Ryan Kim alone, filed as an Infrastructure deal; his only infra name.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Improving AI efficiency with radical new techniques", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/portfolio/flapping-airplanes/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Kim is titled Partner in BCV metadata while the bio still calls him an investor: a quiet, unannounced promotion of the junior who did Flapping.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Ryan Kim is an investor at BCV.", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/team/ryan-kim/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Third-party writer credits Kim personally with incubating Crosby (founder was BCV's NY EIR), so BCV was first in by construction.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "a partner at Bain Capital Ventures who incubated Crosby", "source_url": "https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "eric-vishria", "name": "Eric Vishria", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he backs teams early, in the first institutional round, and holds the board seat for a decade. Cerebras is the proof. It was \"five founders and a deck\" and Benchmark's first hardware deal in ten years. He took the meeting about 18 months into being a VC and was won over by the team slide and Feldman's argument that GPUs are the wrong tool. He was still on the board at the 2026 IPO (TechCrunch). What constrains him: Benchmark's model. A five-or-so person equal partnership with no sector specialists, where the binding constraint is partner time with founders. He says plainly that this \"doesn't scale\" (Uncapped Ep. 41, 20VC). What he'd never do: act as a sector specialist or a passive check-writer. He calls the founder relationship \"a sparring partner and a sounding board,\" not advice, and Benchmark always takes a board seat (Uncapped Ep. 41). He also does not believe in foundation models as a durable store of value (20VC, 2024-09).", "score": 11.67, "n": 24, "nk": 2, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Vishria admits not following the Cerebras pitch; founding partner Bruce Dunlevie was brought in because the partnership lacked hardware expertise", "q": "Most of that meeting was like a dog watching TV for me", "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Vishria admits not following the Cerebras pitch; founding partner Bruce Dunlevie was brought in because the partnership lacked hardware expertise", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Most of that meeting was like a dog watching TV for me", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The hardest Cerebras moment was the 2022 raise with little traction (not 2016)", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "You don't have a lot of traction on the company yet", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Vishria nearly skipped the first Cerebras meeting and later asked his assistant why it was booked; the deal that made Benchmark billions started almost by accident.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Why did you let me take this meeting?", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/14/cerebras-ipo-makes-billions-for-benchmark-but-vc-eric-vishria-almost-didnt-take-the-meeting/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "mamoon-hamid", "name": "Mamoon Hamid", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he rebuilt Kleiner into a small partnership that is measured like a sales team. Kleiner tracks whether it saw every seed, A and B done by 40–50 peer firms each Monday. It targets 60–70% coverage and a 100% win rate on deals it wants. About 80% of investments come through founder referrals (, Venture Unlocked, 2023-11-01). His first Kleiner deal, leading Figma's $25M Series B, is now roughly 90x (, 2026-01-31). Constraint / tension: in Nov 2023 he called the growth mega-round asset class something that \"just didn't totally need to exist\" and asked why a software business would raise \"half a billion, a billion dollars\". By 2026 he was co-leading a $200M seed (Mirendil), joining Etched's $700M round at $21B, and is listed on Flapping Airplanes, which raised a $1B Series A. The read: his capital discipline applies to software, and labs and chips are a separate bucket for him. No source has him explaining that switch yet. What he'd never do (sourced): geographic funds, biotech and a standalone growth practice were cut in the 2017–18 reboot (\"We don't have geographical funds\"). He also won't make judgment an OKR, because it isn't real-time enough. Edge in your lane: he started as a Xilinx FPGA engineer and joined USVP for its semiconductor reputation. His page carries AI chips and compute: Alif (2019), Akeana (2021), Together.ai (2023), Nexthop AI (2025) and Etched (2026).", "score": 11.67, "n": 24, "nk": 2, "nn": 8, "key": {"t": "In 2023 he argued software companies do not need $500M-$1B and the Tiger/Coatue growth class need not exist; by 2026 he co-led a $200M seed and joined $700M/$1B lab rounds, so labs are a separate bucket for him.", "q": "If you're a software business, why do you need to raise half a billion", "u": "https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf"}, "items": [{"insight": "In 2023 he argued software companies do not need $500M-$1B and the Tiger/Coatue growth class need not exist; by 2026 he co-led a $200M seed and joined $700M/$1B lab rounds, so labs are a separate bucket for him.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "If you're a software business, why do you need to raise half a billion", "source_url": "https://docs.google.com/document/d/13DDaPC__rpC6OByKZMehUlXADBNBam2I/export?format=pdf", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Neyshabur and Mehta's partnership predates any investor by about seven years. It started with a cold email at Google, so the team was a proven pair before the raise.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Behnam and Harsh met for the first time in Google through a cold email", "source_url": "https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/mirendil-building-the-system-that-builds-systems/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Kleiner attributes Flapping Airplanes to Hamid as an early-stage 2026 investment; no other investing partner page lists it.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Foundational AI research lab", "source_url": "https://www.kleinerperkins.com/people/mamoon-hamid/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "brian-schechter", "name": "Brian Schechter", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: He had the thesis before he had the founder. He was quoted in the WSJ saying transformers opened a slot for new chips, and met Gavin Uberti \"That same day\". He replaced the missing in-house semis expertise with outside experts: ASIC-startup veterans and incumbent engineers read Uberti's white paper and took calls with him. He underwrites the founder and the market size, not the chip. The founder lens: He is a two-time founder and former teacher (team page). He was a Primary-backed founder before he was a Primary partner: \"Primary was the only one that felt like a team member\" (Vintage, 2025-07). His team page says he backs outsider founders who take \"first principles\" approaches. Constraint: Seed-only check capacity. Primary leads pre-seed and seed, and its later rounds come from the separate Select vehicles (TechCrunch 2021). On Etched he could co-lead a $120M A, but from the B onward he was a follower and could not price. His diligence bench is also thin. His own call for startups names the \"thin bench of diligence partners\" as the reason seed funds avoid compute (, 2026-11-09). What he'd never do (inference from his writing): Back a compute team without seasoned silicon operators next to the first-principles founder, or lead a growth round.", "score": 11.67, "n": 23, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Thesis preceded founder: Schechter met Uberti the same day his WSJ quote on AI-chip openings ran (Feb 2023).", "q": "That same day, I met Gavin Uberti", "u": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond"}, "items": [{"insight": "Thesis preceded founder: Schechter met Uberti the same day his WSJ quote on AI-chip openings ran (Feb 2023).", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "That same day, I met Gavin Uberti", "source_url": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Quantified the gap: since 2015 compute had ~15% of software-infra M&A deal count yet more value created ($705B vs $587B).", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "the power law on top of the power law", "source_url": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/our-compute-thesis-etched-and-beyond", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Schechter was a Primary-backed founder before becoming a Primary partner; Primary converted a portfolio founder into its compute GP-level partner.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Primary was the only one that felt like a team member.", "source_url": "https://vintage-ip.com/interviews/vc-insights-interview-with-brian-schechter-partner-at-primary-venture-partners/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "erik-nordlander", "name": "Erik Nordlander", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: He is still a working engineer, so he builds conviction on technology before revenue exists. In his words, \"many of us at GV can speak that language\". He follows his companies for 8 to 10 years and keeps backing the same founders. Alphabet is GV's only LP and lets him stay in \"every single round\" of a company. What constrains him: He does not take hard lead-alone positions in the biggest rounds. Lightmatter's C-2 was a co-lead with Viking, and GV only participated in the Series B and Series D. The board seat at Lightmatter went to another GV partner, Tyson Clark, when GV led in 2019. He says checks under about $1M don't make sense for a fund GV's size. What he'd never do: Push portfolio companies onto Google products: \"I'd feel awful\" doing that, he says. Back things that are \"ethically not aligned with us\". Treat early revenue as the main signal. He is wary of companies that could turn revenue on \"two days later\", because others could copy them.", "score": 11.67, "n": 18, "nk": 2, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Nordlander was present at Lightmatter's launch at the 2017 MIT $100K (secondary source says he judged it), ~2 years before GV led its $22M A extension.", "q": "I was lucky enough to be there as they launched in an entrepreneurship competition", "u": "https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt"}, "items": [{"insight": "Nordlander was present at Lightmatter's launch at the 2017 MIT $100K (secondary source says he judged it), ~2 years before GV led its $22M A extension.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I was lucky enough to be there as they launched in an entrepreneurship competition", "source_url": "https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "0:51:02", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Stage preference: as early as possible, often at inception, typically a multi-million seed.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "my joke is I'll go as early as they'll let me.", "source_url": "https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SED1707-Google-Ventures.txt", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "0:10:26", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Repeat-founder bet: GV/Lux co-led Reardon's CTRL-labs A (2018); after its Facebook exit and 6 years at Meta, Reardon co-founded Flourish, again backed by GV and Lux (2026). Link inferred from Wired's founder description.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Fundamentally, the company is looking for the algorithms underlying intelligence,", "source_url": "https://hn.jakechampion.name/reader/https://www.wired.com/story/jeff-bezos-is-funding-a-wild-hunt-for-the-brains-core-algorithm/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jack-altman", "name": "Jack Altman", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: access. That comes from a Lattice founder's credibility (YC W16, $3B valuation), the Altman name, and the Uncapped podcast. The podcast doubles as a channel for founders, LPs and firms: they hosted two of their own Fund II LPs on it, and aired a full-partnership Benchmark episode 13 days before joining Benchmark. They raised Fund II ($275M) in about a week. How they work: they back founders, then take board seats and do the work. They publicly attack the \"no board seat\" pitch. Constraints: By their own account they lack the \"oddities\" of great investors. At Benchmark, everything runs through a five-person flat partnership. They name neolabs and heavy robotics as categories that don't start with a normal seed check, so these sit outside a seed-fund playbook. Not first in on the headline deals: at Legora and Monaco, they came in behind the firm that was already there (Benchmark at Legora, Founders Fund at Monaco). What they'd never do: sell capital as \"no board seat,\" or run venture as a dollars-first factory.", "score": 11.67, "n": 18, "nk": 2, "nn": 7, "key": {"t": "Names the categories Benchmark-sized seed checks can't reach, neolabs and heavy robotics, while asking A* about fund-size limits. This marks where their lens stops relative to large lab seeds.", "q": "I’m thinking of neolabs, or heavy robotics projects, or things like that", "u": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing"}, "items": [{"insight": "Names the categories Benchmark-sized seed checks can't reach, neolabs and heavy robotics, while asking A* about fund-size limits. This marks where their lens stops relative to large lab seeds.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "I’m thinking of neolabs, or heavy robotics projects, or things like that", "source_url": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/how-ai-is-rewriting-seed-stage-investing", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Used the podcast to host their own LPs: TrueBridge's Mel Williams (#34, 2025-11) and UMich's Dan Feder (#14, 2025-06, three months before the Fund II close). Both are Alt Capital II LPs per WSJ. The #34 notes name Alt Capital among TrueBridge's managers.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Mel’s team has backed firms like Thrive, Founders Fund, Sequoia, and Alt Capital", "source_url": "https://anchor.fm/s/1156f1c04/podcast/rss", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "show notes, Uncapped #34", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Admits they are not themselves an unusual personality and wonders whether great investors must be. They frame their edge as spotting oddity, not having it.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "I don’t think I’ve got the oddities that sometimes I wish I had", "source_url": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/the-benchmark-partnership-peter-fenton", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "kevin-zhang", "name": "Kevin Zhang", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: he meets technical founders very early and stays with them for years before writing the check. He met Will Falcon of Lightning AI over lunch \"long before he incorporated.\" BCV then co-led the seed in late 2019. He knew Sam Kececi of Sentience for \"the past couple of years\" before leading the 2026 seed. BCV incubated Magical through an EIR, then co-led its seed. What constrains him: his record is mostly product-led SaaS and fintech from 2019 to 2022 (Alloy, Hightouch, Relay, Column Tax, Playbook). He has never been named on a frontier-lab or AI-chip round. His AI deals are applied AI or infrastructure seeds of $5–10M, Solcoa aside. The firm's lab and chip exposure sits with Slater Stich, Saanya Ojha and Merritt Hummer. What he'd never do: chase hype. He says \"I try to avoid the latest hype\" and favours \"founders with independent conviction\". He has also written skeptically about human-data vendors that train AI on expert labour, a contrarian view in 2026 (see below). Seniority signal: he co-authored the Fund XI announcement (2026-09-16) with Slater Stich and Christina Melas-Kyriazi. He also wrote Alysaa Co's partner-promotion post. That places him in the firm's leadership voice.", "score": 11.67, "n": 18, "nk": 2, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Contrarian on expert-data vendors: calls hourly-rate perpetual licences of human expertise Faustian. (Personal Ghost blog; mapped to substack.)", "q": "something Faustian in the transactions that training data companies are making", "u": "https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Contrarian on expert-data vendors: calls hourly-rate perpetual licences of human expertise Faustian. (Personal Ghost blog; mapped to substack.)", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "something Faustian in the transactions that training data companies are making", "source_url": "https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Thesis: general LLMs are a composite voice; value is in scaling an individual's judgment (Sentience, and by extension Hyde's own-your-weights pitch).", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "a composite voice and judgment of millions of people", "source_url": "https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Sentience credited internally to Ryan Kim as collaborator, though BCV CMS lists only Kevin as partner.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "in collaboration with my partner Ryan Kim", "source_url": "https://www.efficiencyfrontier.org/your-best-ai-assistant-is-you/", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sanjana-basu", "name": "Sanjana Basu", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why she wins: She has been at Radical for seven years and has a long public paper trail on a few theses: healthcare AI (2020–22), AI for science (2024–25), voice agents (2024–26) and tabular/causal foundation models (2026). She writes about a space, names the founders in it, and then leads their seed. The clearest case is Attuned Intelligence. Her August 2024 voice-AI essay credited Domenic Donato as \"CTO of Assembly AI building something new\" about 14 months before Radical co-led his $13M seed. What constrains her: Every check she is publicly named on is under $10M of round size, or a $13M co-lead. Radical's large frontier deals (Cohere, World Labs, Discovery Loop, Prime Intellect) belong to Jacobs, Toews, Rosenberg and Katz. Science deals she writes about, Nabla Bio and Orbital, carry other partners' bylines: Toews on Nabla Bio. She also splits foundational and applied explicitly. For foundational tech, investors want \"the top five to ten people on earth\"; for applied AI, she evaluates a founder's \"slope\". That is a summary of her view by Radical editorial staff, not a direct quote. What she would not do: Fund non-AI businesses (\"you should be building an AI business\"). She also will not decide in an exploding window: \"We're not just going to throw money anywhere.\"", "score": 11.67, "n": 16, "nk": 2, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Named Attuned CEO Domenic Donato in her Aug 2024 voice-AI essay while he was in stealth, ~14 months before Radical co-led his $13M seed: thesis-writing doubles as sourcing", "q": "ex-DeepMind research engineer & CTO of Assembly AI building something new", "u": "https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging"}, "items": [{"insight": "Named Attuned CEO Domenic Donato in her Aug 2024 voice-AI essay while he was in stealth, ~14 months before Radical co-led his $13M seed: thesis-writing doubles as sourcing", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "ex-DeepMind research engineer & CTO of Assembly AI building something new", "source_url": "https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/voice-ai-agents-evolution-to-emerging", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "2026 thesis on tabular and causal foundation models, co-written with U of T prof Rahul G. Krishnan (CausalPFN); names Prior Labs, Fundamental, Kumo", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "including myself (Rahul G. Krishnan) and Vahid Balazadeh-Meresht", "source_url": "https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/tabular-foundation-models-enterprise", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Co-wrote with Radical talent head that next iconic AI companies may come from product builders, not labs; unusual for a researcher-founder firm", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "The next iconic companies may not come from labs.", "source_url": "https://sanjanabasu.substack.com/p/the-new-ai-talent-wars-what-matters", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sonya-huang", "name": "Sonya Huang", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "She wins on access to research founders, not on price. She is Sequoia's public AI voice: the yearly essays, AI Ascent keynotes and the Training Data podcast. That platform doubles as a relationship channel with top researchers. David Silver was the first guest she emailed when the show started, about two years before Sequoia co-led his $1.1B seed. Her filter is \"hard to build\": she is openly hostile to thin wrappers and \"vibe revenue\". Since mid-2026 she has argued that companies must own their own models (\"not your weights, your products\"). The constraint is that she sits on a growth team at a mega-fund. Her deals are big, priced rounds she co-leads with other large firms (Lightspeed, Spark), and her public essays put her calls on record, so reversals are visible. Act o1 (Oct 2024) said to avoid models and infra. She has since co-led two labs and an inference-chip round.", "score": 11.67, "n": 14, "nk": 2, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Training Data doubles as a sourcing channel: David Silver was the first guest she reached out to when the show launched (2024-06), about two years before Sequoia co-led his $1.1B Ineffable seed (2026-04). Whether he appeared before founding Ineffable is unconfirmed.", "q": "the first person I called — emailed — to come on Training Data", "u": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience"}, "items": [{"insight": "Training Data doubles as a sourcing channel: David Silver was the first guest she reached out to when the show launched (2024-06), about two years before Sequoia co-led his $1.1B Ineffable seed (2026-04). Whether he appeared before founding Ineffable is unconfirmed.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "the first person I called — emailed — to come on Training Data", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Sequoia had already backed an AlphaGo-team alum (Ioannis Antonoglou, Reflection AI; Sequoia and CRV co-led the $25M seed, announced 2025-03) before Ineffable, so Silver's lab was Sequoia's second DeepMind-RL bet. Her personal role on Reflection is unconfirmed.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "We backed Ioannis [Antonoglou], who was on your original AlphaGo team", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Aug 2026 thesis: app companies must own their models ('not your weights, your products'); she sits on the Fireworks board and calls owning intelligence an existential, strategic imperative. This is a de facto update of Act o1's 2024 'avoid models' stance, framed as app companies building labs.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "I'm on the board of Fireworks.", "source_url": "https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "naveen-rao", "name": "Naveen Rao", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he has done the AI-hardware-plus-software cycle twice. Nervana (AI accelerators) sold to Intel in 2016. MosaicML (efficient training) sold to Databricks for about $1.3B in 2023. That record let him raise a $475M seed at a $4.5B valuation for a company about two months old (; Bloomberg). What constrains him: his bet is a new physical computing substrate (analog and dynamical systems), not a better GPU. It depends on energy becoming the binding constraint within about 3 years, and on the field accepting porting at the model layer rather than the operations layer (All-In transcript, 2026-09). What he'd never do again: sell early. He says he sold Nervana to Intel \"way too early\", and Intel later killed Nervana's training chip in favour of Habana (Synced, 2020-02-26). So expect him to keep Unconventional independent and raise in tranches rather than look for an early exit.", "score": 11.67, "n": 13, "nk": 2, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Repeat-backer circle (synthesis of 4 announcements): Lux and DCVC in all three Rao companies since 2015; Jurvetson (DFJ then Future Ventures) since 2014; a16z and Lightspeed are new. Entry to his next company runs through his current one.", "q": "with participation from Sequoia, Lux Capital, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos", "u": "https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Repeat-backer circle (synthesis of 4 announcements): Lux and DCVC in all three Rao companies since 2015; Jurvetson (DFJ then Future Ventures) since 2014; a16z and Lightspeed are new. Entry to his next company runs through his current one.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "with participation from Sequoia, Lux Capital, DCVC, Future Ventures, Jeff Bezos", "source_url": "https://unconv.ai/blog/introducing-unconventional-ai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Founding pattern: cold-emails academics whose papers he likes; MosaicML began with a 2020 cold email to MIT's Frankle and Carbin; Carbin is now an Unconventional cofounder.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "emailed Frankle and Carbin out of the blue", "source_url": "https://news.mit.edu/2024/mosaicml-helps-nonexperts-build-advanced-generative-ai-models-0621", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Admits selling Nervana to Intel too early; Intel later dropped Nervana's training chip for Habana. Signals he will not take an early exit at Unconventional. Auto-generated transcript; wording may vary.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "I think I sold the company way too early to Intel.", "source_url": "https://podcasts.happyscribe.com/all-in-with-chamath-jason-sacks-friedberg/naveen-rao-4d-computing-ai-s-energy-wall-beating-biology", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "pieter-abbeel", "name": "Pieter Abbeel", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: He gets early access to talent. His bio says his students co-founded over a dozen AI companies, including OpenAI, Perplexity, Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, EvolutionaryScale, Ideogram, Genmo, xdof and Inception. Founders also list him as a credential. Etched lists him as \"Pieter Abbeel (Covariant)\" in its angel wall, and Engram names him alongside Karpathy. How he invests: He never leads. In every round found, he is a named angel inside a round someone else leads: Index (Cohere, Hebbia), Gil/Friedman (Perplexity), Eclipse (Wayve), a16z/Index (Ideogram), Samsung (Config), and GC/KP/Sequoia (Engram). What constrains him: Since 2025-12-17 he has had a full-time job inside a frontier lab: he leads Amazon's frontier model research team. He is also no longer listed on the AIX Ventures team page. He still wrote personal checks after the Amazon appointment (Config 2026-05, Engram 2026-06). What he would never do: Lead, or price a round. Nothing found shows him taking a board seat or writing a check above aggregator-reported angel size. How he converts his network: Selectively. Of the student-founded companies on his bio, he is confirmed on the cap table of only two (Perplexity, Ideogram). He is not named in the funding announcements of the others that I checked: Skild, Physical Intelligence, Reflection, xdof and Inception. Genmo lists him as an advisor, not an investor.", "score": 11.67, "n": 13, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Still writing personal angel checks after becoming head of Amazon's frontier model research team (Dec 2025): named angel in Config's $27M seed in May 2026.", "q": "angel investor Pieter Abbeel (co-founder of Covariant and a UC Berkeley professor)", "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Still writing personal angel checks after becoming head of Amazon's frontier model research team (Dec 2025): named angel in Config's $27M seed in May 2026.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "angel investor Pieter Abbeel (co-founder of Covariant and a UC Berkeley professor)", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/11/koreas-biggest-manufacturers-back-config-the-tsmc-of-robot-data/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "In Engram's $98M first round ($600M post) alongside Karpathy and Wiz CEO Assaf Rappaport, six months into his Amazon frontier-team role; Amazon employment has not stopped him backing a lab-like research company.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "AI/robotics pioneer Pieter Abbeel", "source_url": "https://dealroom.co/news/135964-engram-emerges-from-stealth-with-98m-to-build-a-learned-memory-layer-for/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Was named an AIX Ventures Fund I investing partner at launch, but the current aixventures.com/team page no longer lists him; aggregators still show him at AIX '2021-present', so they are stale.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "Fund I Investing Partners include Pieter Abbeel, Anthony Goldbloom, Chris Manning, and myself.", "source_url": "https://www.aixventures.com/newsroom/introducing-aix-ventures", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ryan-shannon", "name": "Ryan Shannon", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: He brings PE-style underwriting to AI. His essays are spreadsheet arguments about burn, dilution, ARR per FTE and procurement budgets, and he publishes a thesis first and then announces the deal that fits it. The August 2024 piece on AI helping small businesses was followed in September 2024 by OffDeal, \"our first core bet within this theme\". His growth role sits on top of Radical's early book: the companies he is named on (Cohere, Writer, Twelve Labs, Hebbia, V7) are mostly ones where Radical kept investing at later stages. What constrains him: Only one deal (OffDeal) is clearly his own lead. Radical's founding Cohere, Covariant and Promise Robotics checks (2019–2020) were made before he joined full-time in September 2021, so his credit on those is for later involvement, not origination. No board seats are sourced. What he'd never do (inference from his writing): fund a company in the middle of his \"barbell\", that is, one with neither deep research nor extreme shipping speed. He calls that zone \"Death Valley\". As of February 2025 he would not back a new lab trying to push the frontier against Google, OpenAI and Anthropic.", "score": 11.67, "n": 13, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Shannon says pushing the frontier belongs to Google, OpenAI/Microsoft and Anthropic/Amazon only. That contrasts with Radical's 2026 neolab bets (Discovery Loop, Periodic Labs), on none of which he is named.", "q": "pushing the model frontier forward is big tech’s game to lose", "u": "https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups"}, "items": [{"insight": "Shannon says pushing the frontier belongs to Google, OpenAI/Microsoft and Anthropic/Amazon only. That contrasts with Radical's 2026 neolab bets (Discovery Loop, Periodic Labs), on none of which he is named.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "pushing the model frontier forward is big tech’s game to lose", "source_url": "https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/what-are-the-implications-for-startups", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "He calls services-heavy AI companies (forward-deployed engineers, implementation) mispriced: enterprises pay for FDEs but refuse outcome pricing.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "If you’re investing in this market, I’d argue this is the most mispriced thing", "source_url": "https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "An unnamed Radical portfolio company has an 'order of magnitude better' enterprise product but still faces six-month sales cycles because buyers can't budget for it.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "the deals are still taking six months", "source_url": "https://ryanshannon.substack.com/p/the-legibility-tax", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "philip-clark", "name": "Philip Clark", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "(Added 2026-11-09 from the full YouTube auto-caption transcript of the Sourcery episode, uploaded 2025-11-24.) Clark wins by courting founders for about a year before any money moves. He \"simulates\" being a partner well before Thrive is on the cap table: he met Cursor on pivot day in late 2022 and invested in May 2024, and he worked on Wiz from 2022 until early 2024. When conviction arrives, Thrive's concentration lets him put a lot of capital behind it. His physicist and chip-Substack background makes him Thrive's \"hardware guy,\" and he says the hardware opportunity set is the largest in 20 years. He is constrained by Thrive's concentration model, which allows only a small number of \"n of 1\" founders at large check sizes, and by working under Kushner (Kushner plus Clark on Wiz). He says he'd never invest on momentum, meaning a deal where one bad quarter would shake his conviction, and that Thrive doesn't define itself against other investors (\"you play against yourself\").", "score": 11.67, "n": 12, "nk": 2, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Clark says OpenAI's ~$29B round got only two term sheets, Thrive's being one. It was a belief-driven bet, not a competitive auction, and the memo never mentioned ChatGPT. Contrarian timing on the frontier lab, not momentum.", "q": "we were one of two uh firms that gave the company a term sheet", "u": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU"}, "items": [{"insight": "Clark says OpenAI's ~$29B round got only two term sheets, Thrive's being one. It was a belief-driven bet, not a competitive auction, and the memo never mentioned ChatGPT. Contrarian timing on the frontier lab, not momentum.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we were one of two uh firms that gave the company a term sheet", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:40:39", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "His Dec 2022 diligence checklist for foundation-model startups explicitly scores how much 'research DNA' a team has. That is an early talent-centric screen for AI companies.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "How much research DNA should your team have?", "source_url": "https://philipjclark.substack.com/p/ais-eigenquestions", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Thrive led a seed in Mesh Optical in early 2025 (Clark says 'beginning of this year' in an episode recorded ~late Oct 2025). That is about a year before Bloomberg's Feb 2026 report of a $50M Thrive-led round, so Thrive was in well before the public round. The year is inferred from the recording date.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we led the seed for that company at the beginning of this year", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=niosgDC-QHU", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:05:34", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "shaun-maguire", "name": "Shaun Maguire", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. They are a physicist-founder (Caltech PhD, co-founded Escape Dynamics and Qadium/Expanse, which Palo Alto Networks bought for over $1B) with personal ties to Patrick Collison and the Musk orbit (, aggregator;). Technical founders treat them as a peer: Factory's founder cold-emailed them as \"a fellow former hep-th physicist\" and got a reply in under an hour. How they work. They are the senior name, paired with a junior or regional partner who owns the local relationship: Dean Meyer in Israel, Liam Corrigan on Valar, Andrew Reed on SSI and Harmonic, and Sonya Huang and James Flynn on Corma. Their filter is founder intensity over pedigree. Their bio says they want someone \"irrationally motivated\". What constrains them. Sequoia's scout program fed them founders years early (Mithril). The 2026 memo shut Arc and Scouts, so that option-on-founders pipeline is gone. Their public political posting (the July 2025 Mamdani post, a December 2025 Brown shooting misidentification) brought founder petitions and press. Sequoia's managing partner defended them, saying the firm needs \"spiky\" people, and conceded \"does it come with trade-offs? Yes, it does\". Some founders will self-select out of a Maguire-led cap table. What they'd never do. No sourced statement. Their record is all hard tech (labs, compute, security, defense, nuclear, space). I found no consumer-social or thin AI wrapper deal on their page. That is my read, not their statement.", "score": 11.67, "n": 12, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Sequoia used the Scout program as a multi-year option on Mithril founder Jared Quincy Davis (intro by Andrew Reed 2021), then led the seed as first money.", "q": "we immediately decided to make Jared a Sequoia Scout", "u": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Sequoia used the Scout program as a multi-year option on Mithril founder Jared Quincy Davis (intro by Andrew Reed 2021), then led the seed as first money.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "we immediately decided to make Jared a Sequoia Scout", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-mithril-ai-compute-on-demand/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Factory came from a cold email by a fellow high-energy-physics theorist; Maguire replied within an hour and walked with him days later.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I wrote back in less than an hour.", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-factory-autonomous-ai-for-all/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Dean Meyer converted a planned 1:1 lunch with Eon founder Ofir Ehrlich into a three-way with Maguire (Nov 2023); Israel pipeline runs through Meyer.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Dean had invited Ofir to join what was originally planned as a one-on-one lunch", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "atreides-management", "name": "Atreides Management, LP", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: compute-stack conviction backed by public-market capital, plus Musk-ecosystem access. The largest 13F line at 2026-06-30 was SpaceX at $4.67B. The rest of the top 15 is mostly AI infrastructure: Micron, Cerebras, Astera Labs, Ciena, Credo, Coherent, NVIDIA, CoreWeave. Baker made his first SpaceX investment during 18 years at Fidelity. A lab whose pitch is \"more performance per chip\" fits that book. So does a compute-ownership company like Oxide. The Valor tie is structural, not just a co-investment habit. Valor Atreides AI I L.P. is co-managed by Valor Management and Atreides Management. Its related persons include both Antonio Gracias and Gavin Baker. Valor and Atreides sit next to each other in Flapping's Series A investor lists,. Hypothesis, unconfirmed: the Flapping cheque was made jointly or through this vehicle. Constraint: the private capital is raised deal by deal or through wealth platforms, not from a classic VC LP base. The Valor Atreides AI fund drew 664 investors with a $50k minimum, sold through Jefferies, iCapital, Morgan Stanley and UBS. That favours brand-name, later-stage AI rounds that wealth clients recognise. It works against $5M pre-product seeds. What they would likely never do: lead a tiny pre-seed or run board-heavy company building. I found no Atreides lead or board seat at seed in any source (an absence of evidence, not proof).", "score": 11.67, "n": 11, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Atreides and Valor run a joint AI fund: Valor Atreides AI I is co-managed, with Gracias and Baker both promoters. Explains Valor+Atreides appearing side by side in AI rounds like Flapping.", "q": "CIO of Co-Investment Manager", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Atreides and Valor run a joint AI fund: Valor Atreides AI I is co-managed, with Gracias and Baker both promoters. Explains Valor+Atreides appearing side by side in AI rounds like Flapping.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "CIO of Co-Investment Manager", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Atreides and Valor went from Series B participants (Nov 2025) to Series C co-leads 10 months later; Gavin Baker took a board seat only at the C.", "type": "insider-doubled-down", "evidence_quote": "alongside Atreides Management and Valor Equity Partners", "source_url": "https://capitalg.com/insights/doubling-down-on-celero/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The joint AI fund is wealth-channel money: $758.7M from 664 investors at a $50k minimum, placed by Jefferies, iCapital, Morgan Stanley, UBS.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "664", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2033590/000101297526000109/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "noetive", "name": "Noetive", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why it matters to THESIS.md. It is the closest thing at Eclipse to the AI-native automation the owner likes (Serval). The product is an AI layer plus sensing pods that learns how a plant or logistics operation runs and then automates planning. The seed is large enough to take a ~$20M check. It also brands itself in the new-lab register (\"NeoLab\"), which puts it near the frontier-lab seeds in the thesis. The catch. This is a firm-built company: Eclipse was in before it existed, so outsiders can get in only at the syndicated seed. Eclipse Venture Equity does only a few builds a year. It is applied AI for industry, not pre-training or chips. Traction claim. The first named customer is Steuben Foods. Its CEO says planning that \"took a week\" monthly now runs daily in minutes. Other design partners are in manufacturing, logistics, energy and data centers.", "score": 11.67, "n": 11, "nk": 2, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Eclipse's Chief AI Officer is also Noetive's CEO and co-founder: the CAIO hire on 2026-04-28 was in practice a founder hire, not just an advisory seat.", "q": "CEO and co-founder of Noetive", "u": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed"}, "items": [{"insight": "Eclipse's Chief AI Officer is also Noetive's CEO and co-founder: the CAIO hire on 2026-04-28 was in practice a founder hire, not just an advisory seat.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "CEO and co-founder of Noetive", "source_url": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Decart AI's three co-founders are angels, a frontier-model founder team backing an Eclipse-built company.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Decart AI co-founders Dean Leitersdorf", "source_url": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The company's own team page lists Frenkel as Co-Founder & CEO and Dan Barak as Co-Founder & CPO; Eclipse's post and the release name no other co-founder.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Co-Founder & CPO", "source_url": "https://www.noetive.ai/team", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "a-star", "name": "A* (A-Star; filings use \"A-Star Capital\" / \"A-Star Partners\" / \"A-Star Fund\")", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: relationship-led, \"pseudo proprietary\" seeds, and an early lean into teenage and research founders. Siegel says more than half of A's seeds come from prior relationships. Hartz says that half has returned disproportionately. Mapping talent, not markets: Siegel says \"We generally map talent not markets\". The 2025–26 pattern shows it: Stanford research spinouts: Simile, from Joon Sung Park, Percy Liang and Michael Bernstein. MIT engineers: Andera. Teenage founders: about 20% of recent deals, per Hartz to TechCrunch (2025-10-18) as reported by Yahoo. What constrains them: fund size. Fund III is $450M with $3–5M average checks and at least 30 companies. Siegel says a lot of the multi-stage money now goes into big \"seed\" rounds, and that \"we can't really do those rounds\". That rules out leading a $180M lab seed like Flapping Airplanes'. Reserves: A is reserve-heavy. It always takes pro rata in the A, and more than 50% of dollars go in after seed. What they'd never do: lead a mega-seed for a frontier lab, or chase markets without a talent edge. Hartz also thinks AI is heading to \"the mother of all bubbles\".", "score": 11.67, "n": 10, "nk": 2, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Fund III Form D shows $451M sold to 59 investors with first sale 2026-04-03, i.e. an average LP ticket of ~$7.6M (my arithmetic) from an endowment/foundation base.", "q": "<totalNumberAlreadyInvested>59</totalNumberAlreadyInvested>", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2119865/000211986526000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Fund III Form D shows $451M sold to 59 investors with first sale 2026-04-03, i.e. an average LP ticket of ~$7.6M (my arithmetic) from an endowment/foundation base.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "<totalNumberAlreadyInvested>59</totalNumberAlreadyInvested>", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2119865/000211986526000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "A* sourced the seed through a campus coffee with Park after tracking the generative-agents research. Park was then weighing a company with his advisors.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "first met him over coffee at Bytes Cafe on Stanford's campus", "source_url": "https://www.a-star.co/news/simile", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "A* says it cannot do the $10-100M 'seed' rounds now absorbing multi-stage money, so frontier-lab mega-seeds are structurally outside its lane; its Flapping Airplanes exposure is a non-lead A participation.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "we can't really do those rounds", "source_url": "https://ceointerviews.ai/interview/903381/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "35:12", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "benchmark", "name": "Benchmark", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win: equal ownership and pay, no junior or senior partners, no CEO role). The founder gets a GP, not an associate, and the GP joins the board. Returns support the brand: the first eight funds (1995–2019) returned 7.5x net and the 2020 fund is reported at 10x (Wikipedia citing NYT/Bloomberg)). What constrains them: (1) A tiny partnership. 2025 departures (Tavel to venture partner in April, Lazarte leaving in July) left three full-time GPs. (2) The ownership model is under pressure from AI pricing. The Information's Oct 2025 headline reads \"High Prices, Smaller Stakes—and a Poached Star\" (paywalled; body not read). (3) Geopolitics: the Manus Series B drew a US Treasury outbound-investment review (May 2025), and China later blocked Meta's acquisition of Manus). How they are adapting: two new GPs (Randle, Oct 2025; Altman, Feb 2026). Altman brought Alt Capital teammates, the firm's first departure from a GP-only roster. Benchmark also now leads Series B rounds (Gumloop, Monaco), and the $1.25B growth fund reportedly plans five to six large checks. What they would not do (by history): run a platform or associate pyramid, or write mega-seeds into new labs. No Benchmark seed in a new frontier lab was found. Their AI bets are chips/compute (Cerebras, Starcloud), infra (Fireworks, LangChain) and applications (Mercor, HeyGen, Manus, Gumloop, Sierra).", "score": 11.67, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "WashU CIO Scott Wilson named Benchmark as the fund he wishes WashU were in; signal of top-endowment demand for access", "q": "benchmark probably", "u": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026"}, "items": [{"insight": "WashU CIO Scott Wilson named Benchmark as the fund he wishes WashU were in; signal of top-endowment demand for access", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "benchmark probably", "source_url": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:51:00", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The 2026 $750M and $1.25B Form D totals include GP commitments, so third-party LP money is below the headline. The prior flagships were $425M from 38-39 investors (2020/2024 Form Ds).", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Totals include commitments of all partners.", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138630/000213863026000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The 2026 Form Ds list six managing members: Altman, Fenton, Hu, Puttagunta, Randle and Vishria. Tavel and Lazarte have dropped off, and no Alt Capital staff are listed.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Managing Member of the General Partner", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2138636/000213863626000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ben-spector", "name": "Ben Spector (Benjamin F. Spector)", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he sits at the intersection of two talent graphs that frontier-AI money chases. One is the Stanford Hazy Research GPU-kernel scene (ThunderKittens, megakernels, with Chris Ré). The other is the young-founder network of Prod, which Index says he co-founded and led and which played a role in the early shaping of Cursor, Mercor, Etched and Decart. What investors are buying: Index's own description is \"a firehose of ambitious ideas that can sound outlandish at first\". Brother Asher is cast as the filter on those ideas (same source). Constraint: no commercial product a year in, and the public thesis moved from data efficiency at seed to performance per chip (megakernels) at the A (see companies/flapping-airplanes.md). What he'd never do (inferred from his own writing, not stated): build a big, specialized, bureaucratic lab. His 2022 essay argues \"specialization is a tool of the bureaucrat\" and favours small, intense pairs. He told the WSJ (via the company's X post) that small teams of brilliant young people are \"the kinds of organizations that actually win\".", "score": 11.67, "n": 8, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Ben's tie to Cursor CEO Michael Truell predates Prod: the two built Two Sigma's Halite AI competition together as summer 2016 interns. Repo files route the Cursor link through Prod; the primary source shows a decade-old personal relationship.", "q": "primarily created by Ben Spector and Michael Truell for Two Sigma", "u": "https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md"}, "items": [{"insight": "Ben's tie to Cursor CEO Michael Truell predates Prod: the two built Two Sigma's Halite AI competition together as summer 2016 interns. Repo files route the Cursor link through Prod; the primary source shows a decade-old personal relationship.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "primarily created by Ben Spector and Michael Truell for Two Sigma", "source_url": "https://github.com/HaliteChallenge/Halite/blob/master/README.md", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "In March 2023 Ben argued foundation models have fab-like economics (costly, fast-depreciating, compute concentrated at Google and NVIDIA). Flapping's Series A pitch of getting more work from each chip follows from this view (inference).", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "the economics of FMs will be just like semiconductor fabs", "source_url": "http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/ae", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Ben's 2022 essay favours the 'hive mind' (two friends collaborating on nearly everything) over specialization, which he calls bureaucratic. It prefigures a lab run by him, his brother and a close friend.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "the hive mind is an underutilized tool of project management", "source_url": "http://benjaminfspector.com/writing/sathm", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sabrina-hahn", "name": "Sabrina Hahn", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.67, "n": 6, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "She held Flapping Airplanes before the Series A: Forbes listed it at the seed valuation in May 2026, five months before the $1B A. The A check was likely a follow-on.", "q": "Flapping Airplanes, valued at $1.5 billion after a 2026 seed round", "u": "https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/"}, "items": [{"insight": "She held Flapping Airplanes before the Series A: Forbes listed it at the seed valuation in May 2026, five months before the $1B A. The A check was likely a follow-on.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Flapping Airplanes, valued at $1.5 billion after a 2026 seed round", "source_url": "https://www.forbes.com/profile/sabrina-hahn/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Big-platform LPs back her small fund (Sequoia, Insight). This is a plausible allocation channel into rounds Sequoia co-leads, such as Flapping's seed (inference).", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "manages a VC fund whose LPs include Sequoia, Insight Partners", "source_url": "https://www.sabrinahahn.com/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The solo predecessor fund, SH Fund LP, filed in Feb 2022 from Alpine NJ. That dates her institutional track record to about four years before Absolute.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Managing Director of the General Partner of the Issuer", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1912867/000092542122000003/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "washington-state-investment-board", "name": "Washington State Investment Board", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.67, "n": 5, "nk": 2, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Staff explicitly cited Menlo's Anthropic tie and the Anthology Fund as the reason to keep re-upping. Proximity to a frontier lab is what sells an AI-era re-up to a pension board.", "q": "Menlo's partnership with Anthropic, one of the leaders in foundational AI models", "u": "https://sib.wa.gov/docs/meetings/private/25_0904final.pdf"}, "items": [{"insight": "Staff explicitly cited Menlo's Anthropic tie and the Anthology Fund as the reason to keep re-upping. Proximity to a frontier lab is what sells an AI-era re-up to a pension board.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Menlo's partnership with Anthropic, one of the leaders in foundational AI models", "source_url": "https://sib.wa.gov/docs/meetings/private/25_0904final.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The consultant (Albourne) flagged Menlo's AI focus as a concentration risk while still recommending it. An AI-only manager will face the same objection at this board.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "introduces some concentration risk", "source_url": "https://sib.wa.gov/docs/meetings/private/25_0904final.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "WSIB's biggest venture bet is a 45-year single-manager relationship: $1.8B+ across 17 Menlo funds since 1981. Menlo XVII ($175M) is an AI seed/Series A fund, so WSIB's frontier-AI exposure runs almost entirely through Menlo and its Anthropic position.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Menlo Ventures is the WSIB's longest-standing private equity relationship", "source_url": "https://sib.wa.gov/docs/meetings/private/25_0904final.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sarah-catanzaro", "name": "Sarah Catanzaro", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.67, "n": 3, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Datology was sourced via a portfolio-adjacent researcher intro: Mosaic's Jonathan Frankle connected Amplify to Ari Morcos in April 2023.", "q": "Jonathan Frankle, the Chief Scientist of Mosaic, sent us a well-timed message", "u": "https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/datology"}, "items": [{"insight": "Datology was sourced via a portfolio-adjacent researcher intro: Mosaic's Jonathan Frankle connected Amplify to Ari Morcos in April 2023.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Jonathan Frankle, the Chief Scientist of Mosaic, sent us a well-timed message", "source_url": "https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/datology", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Runway originated from a 2018 coffee with a GAN chair designer who then introduced Catanzaro to Cristobal Valenzuela at NYU ITP.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Philipp introduced me to Cristobal Valenzuela", "source_url": "https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/our-series-a-investment-in-runway", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Catanzaro considered incubating Taste herself before co-leading its $18.5M seed: thesis-first, founder-second sourcing.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "I even considered incubating a version of the company myself", "source_url": "https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/kill-the-slop-announcing-our-investment-in-taste", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "absolute-ventures", "name": "Absolute Ventures (most likely Absolute Partners, Sabrina and Evan Hahn's firm; see the identity note)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win: Sabrina Hahn gets small allocations in the most competitive frontier-AI rounds: neolabs such as Flapping Airplanes and Mirendil, Periodic, and AI infrastructure such as fal and Together. That track record put her at #23 on Forbes' 2026 Midas Seed List,. The list is built on SH Fund's results. Non-traditional edge: she has no operator or big-fund background. She was an angel and a Pace Gallery director (the gallery's youngest) and wrote a children's art-book series. My inference: access runs through personal and LP networks (Sequoia as LP) rather than through a brand. Constraint: the firm is very small and newly institutionalized. The 2026 Form Ds report $0 sold at filing and an indefinite offering size. She almost certainly isn't leading the rounds she lists. No source shows her leading or pricing a deal; she shows up in long participant lists, as on Flapping. What they'd never do (inference, unsourced): lead a $1B round. Absolute is a participant-check firm.", "score": 11.5, "n": 2, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "The Absolute vehicles were only formed in mid-2026. Both Form Ds show an indefinite offering with no sales yet at filing, so fund size is undisclosed.", "q": "Manager of the General Partner of the Issuer", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "The Absolute vehicles were only formed in mid-2026. Both Form Ds show an indefinite offering with no sales yet at filing, so fund size is undisclosed.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Manager of the General Partner of the Issuer", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2140744/000090514826003382/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The 'Absolute Ventures' in Flapping's Series A has no handle or link. The only frontier-AI investor matching the name is Sabrina Hahn's Absolute Partners, whose site lists Flapping first.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Hummingbird, Hof, Absolute Ventures, A*, CRV", "source_url": "https://x.com/flappyairplanes/status/2112958916871225736", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "hemant-taneja", "name": "Hemant Taneja", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: he runs GC as a capital platform rather than a fund. Creation (incubation), the Customer Value Fund, HATCo (which reaches about 15% of US healthcare operations), the GC Institute, and SMAs mean he can offer a founder distribution and every stage of capital. He co-founds instead of waiting for deal flow: Livongo, Commure, Hippocratic AI, Transcarent and Percepta. What constrains him: scale and a mandate for applied AI. His own bio centres on \"applied AI as the engine for abundance and resilience\". His Q2-2026 review argues that outcomes are concentrating and that venture must innovate on capital itself. The result is a bias toward platform plays (a GC-owned Percepta, a hospital, roll-ups) over picking. What he'd never do: stay a passive minority holder. Every frontier name shows him re-upping: Mistral seed, then A, then a co-led B; Hippocratic seed, A and C; Applied Intuition B lead, then E; Substrate seed, then the 2025 round.", "score": 11.33, "n": 24, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "The River AI relationship predates River: in the launch interview Taneja says he and Igor Babuschkin met around the time Babuschkin was starting xAI, so GC's $1.1B co-lead of a two-month-old lab rested on a ~3-year relationship. The Babuschkin profile had the intro path as 'nothing found'.", "q": "when we met, and what got you excited about starting xAI", "u": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience"}, "items": [{"insight": "The River AI relationship predates River: in the launch interview Taneja says he and Igor Babuschkin met around the time Babuschkin was starting xAI, so GC's $1.1B co-lead of a two-month-old lab rested on a ~3-year relationship. The Babuschkin profile had the intro path as 'nothing found'.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "when we met, and what got you excited about starting xAI", "source_url": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Taneja personally took the Chai Discovery board seat on GC's $130M Series B co-lead, yet GC's own Chai portfolio page does not credit him (it lists Viboch, Momeni, Neupane, Pai). GC's pages under-count his AI involvement.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Hemant Taneja from General Catalyst will be joining the board", "source_url": "https://www.oakhcft.com/news/chai-discovery-announces-130-million-series-b-to-transform-molecular-discovery", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Same LP letter admits GC overpaid on some deals in the 2021 frenzy.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "we, too, made a handful of investments that were paying far ahead", "source_url": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/hemants-year-end-letter-to-lps", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "low", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "aaref-hilaly", "name": "Aaref Hilaly", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: he is a two-time founder (one flop, one $410M exit) and an ex-Sequoia investor, so he sells hands-on company building. His own bio says he spends an afternoon at Decagon \"every couple of weeks\" on recruiting, org design and strategy. He also gets in through long, informal relationships: he backed Decagon co-founder Ashwin Sreenivas's prior company Helia and advised Decagon informally for about a year before BCV led its Series B. What constrains him: he is selective by design, and says BCV partners with \"a small number of new companies each year\". His 2024 to 2026 frontier exposure is mostly as a follower in rounds led by Founders Fund, Coatue, Index and Greenoaks (see Portfolio). His view of the market: he thinks first-generation GenAI categories are \"over-funded and intensely competitive\", so winners must out-execute on product and go-to-market. What he'd never do (inferred, not stated): chase a thin \"AI wrapper\". His posts keep asking for proprietary data, full-workflow capture and real reasoning, as with EvenUp and Actively.", "score": 11.33, "n": 22, "nk": 1, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Sourced Contextual AI via its research papers circulating among enterprise CTOs.", "q": "when we found it on the desks of CTOs building on LLMs", "u": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Sourced Contextual AI via its research papers circulating among enterprise CTOs.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "when we found it on the desks of CTOs building on LLMs", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/bringing-context-to-enterprise-ai-why-we-invested-in-contextual-ai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Decagon came from a prior bet: he backed Ashwin Sreenivas's Helia (acq. Scale), met Jesse Zhang through him, and BCV advised informally ~1 year before leading the Series B.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I backed Ashwin’s prior company, Helia (acquired by Scale)", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "BCV informally advised Decagon for about a year before investing — relationship-first sourcing.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we’ve been informally advising Decagon over the past year or so", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/why-we-backed-decagon-ai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "cendana-capital", "name": "Cendana Capital", "kind": "lp", "fit": "low", "crux": "Cendana believes fund size determines strategy. Its rule: back small funds whose GPs lead rounds and take large initial ownership relative to fund size, and become one of their largest LPs with a $10–20M Core commitment. The constraints are its mandate (sub-$100M funds in Cendana 6, average portfolio fund $55M, average Core commitment $15M;) and a fee stack its LPs must accept: Cendana's 1% and 10% carry sit on top of the GP's fees. Things it would never do: take a cut of a GP's management company, which it reads as a negative signal, or back GPs who don't lead or don't keep ownership.", "score": 11.33, "n": 21, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Kim cites Casey Caruso (Topology Ventures) living in hacker houses as the model of founder access, versus an older GP in Woodside.", "q": "live in hacker houses", "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/18/our-funds-are-20-years-old-a-look-inside-the-liquidity-crisis-reshaping-venture-capital/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Kim cites Casey Caruso (Topology Ventures) living in hacker houses as the model of founder access, versus an older GP in Woodside.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "live in hacker houses", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/18/our-funds-are-20-years-old-a-look-inside-the-liquidity-crisis-reshaping-venture-capital/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Cendana 6 raised $200M in two managed accounts for groups beyond its normal mandate; the likely route for bigger funds such as Alt Capital II ($275M), though that link is unconfirmed.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "a bit outside of the Cendana aperture", "source_url": "https://medium.com/@CendanaCapital/announcing-cendana-6-our-new-chapter-in-very-early-stage-venture-0ffb923ea677", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Refuses GP-stake deals: never asks for a cut of the management company and reads an offer of one as a red flag.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "for fund managers who offer that, it's actually a negative signal", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2023/09/08/exclusive-cendana-capital-closes-on-470m-more-to-back-seed-stage-fund-managers/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "dean-meyer", "name": "Dean Meyer", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: proximity and speed in the Israeli Unit 8200 / Talpiot founder pool. They get to founders before there is a company. On AIR, Sequoia shook hands on the seed two days after a founder left military service. Origin of the edge: Sequoia hired them in March 2024 after co-investing with them in Eon's seed while they ran a US seed fund's Tel Aviv office. Constraint: they are a node, not a solo decision-maker. Every deal is co-signed with a senior partner (Maguire on Decart, Irregular, Eon and Kela; Balkansky on Cymphony and AIR; Cahn on Kela and AIR). Their own thesis surface is narrow: Israeli security, defense and infrastructure. What they'd likely never do (inference, unsourced): back a first-time founder without deep, long-term domain experience. Their stated filter is \"real experience in a particular problem space\".", "score": 11.33, "n": 20, "nk": 1, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Sequoia hired Dean Meyer after co-investing with him in Eon's seed while he ran a US seed fund's Tel Aviv office. Co-investing worked as a hiring audition.", "q": "in March, we happily welcomed Dean to the Sequoia team", "u": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Sequoia hired Dean Meyer after co-investing with him in Eon's seed while he ran a US seed fund's Tel Aviv office. Co-investing worked as a hiring audition.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "in March, we happily welcomed Dean to the Sequoia team", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Sequoia hired Meyer after they co-invested in Eon's seed from a US seed fund's Tel Aviv office. The co-investment worked as a hiring audition.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "in March, we happily welcomed Dean to the Sequoia team", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-eon-cloud-backup-reinvented/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "AIR timing: Sequoia committed to the seed before the company had a name or product. The handshake came two days after the founder left Unit 8200 service.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Yair finished his service on a Thursday, and we shook hands", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-air-securing-the-ai-supply-chain/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "eclipse", "name": "Eclipse (formerly Eclipse Ventures)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. Technically fluent operators, plus elder networks in semis and manufacturing, plus patience. Eclipse held Cerebras through about 18 months without a working chip at ~$8M a month of burn. Then it manufactured demand through the G42 and OpenAI introductions. Company creation makes Eclipse first money on its best seeds. Constraint. The scope is explicitly physical; Susan says \"We're not disrupting consumer sectors and knowledge work\". So pure-software frontier labs are outside the mandate. The AI exposure is compute hardware (Cerebras, Efficient, Oxide), embodied-AI labs (Genesis AI, Wayve) and AI-native industrial automation (Noetive, Bedrock). The bench is partners only, so deal flow concentrates in about a dozen people. AUM of ~$10B pulls toward growth checks. What they'd never do (inferred, not stated). Lead a pure LLM or chatbot lab with no physical or compute layer.", "score": 11.33, "n": 20, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "WashU reached Cerebras, about half of its FY26 IPO-driven return, via an Eclipse LP commitment rather than direct.", "q": "We got Cerebras through a fund called Eclipse", "u": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026"}, "items": [{"insight": "WashU reached Cerebras, about half of its FY26 IPO-driven return, via an Eclipse LP commitment rather than direct.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "We got Cerebras through a fund called Eclipse", "source_url": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:51:00", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Cerebras came via Pierre Lamond's prior relationship: he had backed Andrew Feldman at SeaMicro and was among the first people Feldman called. Lamond also introduced Cerebras to TSMC's CEO.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Pierre was technical. Some investors are not", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Cerebras burned heavily in 2017-2019. Lior Susan stayed through it and made the G42 and OpenAI introductions.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "We needed someone bold enough to take the risk", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "lightspeed-venture-partners", "name": "Lightspeed Venture Partners", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "A multi-stage platform that missed the 2021-22 lab wave, then bought its way back in with big-check leads (Anthropic Series E as the round's biggest investor, Mistral's seed, Ineffable's $1.1B seed co-lead, Unconventional AI's $475M seed co-lead). It now pairs those headline lab checks with a fast-growing pile of $10-300M AI-infrastructure leads run by Guru Chahal and James Alcorn. Crux: it wins by showing up with size and the co-founder (Ravi Mhatre) in the room, and by re-upping hard. Constraint: it often arrives a round late (Anthropic D, SSI's second round, Reflection after the CRV and Sequoia seed), so it pays up rather than getting in first. Lens fit: high. It co-leads the large new-lab seeds the owner wants to join.", "score": 11.33, "n": 19, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Lightspeed knew the Andera founders from company formation but did not lead seed (A* did); it waited and led the Series A ~20 months later.", "q": "We have known the co-founders, Aryo and Tinah, since the formation of Andera.", "u": "https://lsvp.com/stories/audits-moment-has-arrived-why-we-invested-in-andera/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Lightspeed knew the Andera founders from company formation but did not lead seed (A* did); it waited and led the Series A ~20 months later.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "We have known the co-founders, Aryo and Tinah, since the formation of Andera.", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/stories/audits-moment-has-arrived-why-we-invested-in-andera/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Form D shows Lightspeed Select VI had sold ~$1.745B to 204 investors by Dec 2025 (first sale 2024-12-13); XV-A $978M and XV-B $1.195B each to 207 investors, i.e. the same ~200-LP base across vehicles.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "totalNumberAlreadyInvested 204", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2044417/000095010325016144/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Lightspeed met founders March 2022 but only entered at the Series C in 2023, then re-upped twice", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "When we first met Rami, Saam, and Ray in March 2022", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/stories/our-investment-in-evenup-the-ai-platform-shaping-the-future-of-legal-services/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ryan-gibson", "name": "Ryan Gibson", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins. He wins on domain immersion before incorporation. He sources, recruits and vets technical co-founders himself, and opens doors through an operator network. For Bedrock that meant 50+ construction-industry conversations, many through his Kojo contacts, before the company was formed. Eclipse says the practice has built nearly 20 companies during his tenure, about 20% of them unicorns, collectively worth over $5B. These figures are firm-reported. He is the scoper, not the sourcer. On Bedrock, the founders came in through other partners. Boris Sofman connected with Aidan Madigan-Curtis, and Kevin Peterson had worked with Seth Winterroth. Gibson's job was scoping the company and opening the industry. On Noetive, the technical lead was Amir Frenkel, Eclipse's Chief AI Officer, who became Noetive's CEO. What constrains him. The work does not scale. Eclipse says it takes on only a few builds a year. His network comes from construction tech and data-center hardware, so his edge is in construction, logistics, energy and manufacturing. It is unproven in frontier-model or compute companies. What he would not do. This is inferred, not stated: he would not back a company from the outside in a contested round. Every sourced deal is an Eclipse formation where the firm is in before the founders incorporate. He and Lior Susan also reject top-down enterprise selling into industrial companies (see below).", "score": 11.33, "n": 18, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Noetive's angel syndicate is Meta-heavy (Bosworth, Al-Dahle, Schroepfer, Sheikh), tracking CEO Frenkel's Meta tenure; Decart AI co-founders also in.", "q": "Andrew 'Boz' Bosworth, CTO of Meta", "u": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed"}, "items": [{"insight": "Noetive's angel syndicate is Meta-heavy (Bosworth, Al-Dahle, Schroepfer, Sheikh), tracking CEO Frenkel's Meta tenure; Decart AI co-founders also in.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Andrew 'Boz' Bosworth, CTO of Meta", "source_url": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Bedrock intro path ran through other partners: Sofman reached Madigan-Curtis, Peterson knew Winterroth. Gibson was the scoper, not the sourcer.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Boris connected with Aidan as he began exploring what to build next", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Portfolio-founder loop: Bedrock CEO Boris Sofman is an angel in Noetive's seed, Gibson's next published build.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "co-founder and CEO of Bedrock Robotics, Boris Sofman", "source_url": "https://www.noetive.ai/news/noetive-emerges-from-stealth-with-41-million-seed", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "founders-fund", "name": "Founders Fund", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: brand, plus serial conviction. When they find a breakout (Anduril, Cognition, Netic), they lead round after round and absorb the step-ups. Partners also operate their own frontier companies (Anduril, Varda, General Matter), which gives them technical credibility with deep-tech founders. What constrains them: capital has moved to growth. The live venture vehicle (Fund IX) is ~$972M, against $6.02B in Growth IV, and ~80% of growth goes to follow-ons. The two big lab positions (OpenAI, Anthropic) entered through growth, not venture. Every meaningful check still goes through Thiel. The real engine: a cheap seed, then concentrated growth-fund doubling (Crusoe went from Series B participant to Series D lead to F participant). The returns come from the follow-ons, not from winning the seed. What they'd never do (inferred, not sourced): follow consensus at small size, or write broad index-style seed checks. Luttig's view that talent wars are \"a net-consolidating force\" suggests skepticism toward undercapitalized new labs.", "score": 11.33, "n": 18, "nk": 1, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "OpenAI entered FF through the growth fund, not venture. FF did not back the labs at seed; it bought in late.", "q": "No, that was first in growth.", "u": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan"}, "items": [{"insight": "OpenAI entered FF through the growth fund, not venture. FF did not back the labs at seed; it bought in late.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "No, that was first in growth.", "source_url": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Primary filings show the live venture fund (Fund IX) is ~$972M against $6.02B in Growth IV. It was filed in 2023 at a $675M target, not launched in Oct 2025 as press implied.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Founders Fund IX, LP", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1971631/000123191925000327/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Stephens: the 2021 venture fund was oversized, so they split it in half and run the halves back to back.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "we realized that we raised it, and it was too big.", "source_url": "https://uncappedpod.com/p/founders-fund-on-truth-seeking-taiwan", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "hunter-somerville", "name": "Hunter Somerville", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He sits on a platform that does primaries, small-manager programs (VC Micro, Micro Growth Equity), secondaries and co-invest under one team. A manager relationship he opens can feed all four. He co-led the $3.3B VC Secondaries VI, the largest VC secondaries fund at close. What constrains him. The flagship VC Global Partners XII had raised $833.7M from 131 investors Form D. Pacing that much capital pulls most dollars toward established franchises. Emerging managers come in through micro programs. What he'd never do (his stated filter). He won't treat venture as an index. He discounts track records built on tiny angel checks or on deals someone \"sourced\" but didn't cover. He won't back a spinout on a standalone basis podcast. The transcript labels all speakers \"Host\", so attribution is likely but not certain. What he backs in practice. For Seven Stars he underwrote a first-time GP's relationships with AI companies, not a track record.", "score": 11.33, "n": 17, "nk": 1, "nn": 9, "key": {"t": "A new StepStone VC Micro-K II vehicle filed in June 2026 with no sales yet; Micro programs are the door for small emerging managers", "q": "StepStone VC Micro-K II LP", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133412/000213341226000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "A new StepStone VC Micro-K II vehicle filed in June 2026 with no sales yet; Micro programs are the door for small emerging managers", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "StepStone VC Micro-K II LP", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2133412/000213341226000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Discounts track records built from tiny angel checks; wants evidence the person can execute at fund scale (auto-transcript labels every speaker \"Host\"; attribution to Somerville likely but unconfirmed)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "very tiny angel checks that are just not indicative of their ability to execute", "source_url": "https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "2:03", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Flags the 'sourced but didn't cover' problem: spinouts who claim deals they found but didn't lead or sit on (auto-transcript labels every speaker \"Host\"; attribution to Somerville likely but unconfirmed)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "a body of work that maybe they sourced, but they didn't cover", "source_url": "https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "2:03", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "rob-toews", "name": "Rob Toews", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win: They are the Bay Area face of an AI-only firm whose founders built Toronto's AI scene (Hinton, Fei-Fei Li). They can bring luminary angels into their rounds: Radical introduced Fei-Fei Li to Twelve Labs and she invested, and Jeff Dean and Fei-Fei Li joined their Yutori seed. The Forbes column (yearly \"10 AI predictions\" since end of 2020) is a sourcing magnet and a public record of their theses. What constrains them: Radical's lead partners share deals; most of their named companies are co-credited (Shannon on Hebbia, Writer and Delphina; Welch on Generalist; Katz on Crusoe; Basu on Nabla). In several big rounds they followed someone else's lead (Hebbia A by Index, DatologyAI A by Felicis, Ricursive A by Lightspeed). They are not the pre-company incubator; that is Jordan Jacobs' role (World Labs). What they'd never do (inference): Non-AI deals at Radical. Their only non-AI-looking deal, Muon Space, sits under their \"AI data centers in space\" thesis.", "score": 11.33, "n": 17, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Their December predictions double as a deal map: they called web agents the next consumer killer app in Dec 2024, then led Yutori's $15M seed (a web-agent company) three months later.", "q": "Web agents will go mainstream, becoming the next major killer application in consumer AI.", "u": "https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Their December predictions double as a deal map: they called web agents the next consumer killer app in Dec 2024, then led Yutori's $15M seed (a web-agent company) three months later.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Web agents will go mainstream, becoming the next major killer application in consumer AI.", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/10-ai-predictions-for-2025/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Backs AI compute (Crusoe, Emerald AI) while openly questioning hyperscaler GPU accounting; implies they expect compute to be repriced and favours efficiency plays.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "whether it's still justifiable for these companies to use five-plus-year depreciation schedules", "source_url": "https://hgcapital.com/insights/orbit-podcast/predicting-ai-rob-toews-scorecard-from-the-frontier", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Reka was a Radical-originated lab: Radical led the founding seed and took a board seat at inception, then co-led the $58M Series A with DST.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Radical was Reka's founding investor, leading the company's seed round", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/custom-ai-models-for-the-enterprise/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "andreessen-horowitz", "name": "Andreessen Horowitz (a16z)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. They scale a deal through a dedicated team per category, and they say so. The 2024 raise letter argues that model founders \"need an entirely different set of networks\" than biotech founders, which is why each practice has its own fund (, 2024-04-16). Bylines on the investing posts show the result. A typical AI deal has 2 to 5 named partners: a GP lead plus junior co-signers who source and diligence (examples: Cursor, Exa, TypeSafe). Where the money is. In the January 2026 raise, Growth got $6.75B against $1.7B each for Infra and Apps (, 2026-01-09). Most AI dollars are later-stage. Early AI checks come from the $1.7B Infra fund, speedrun and Bio. What constrains them. Size and conflicts. They are in several directly competing coding companies: Cursor (Series A lead), Cognition (Series E co-lead, Sept 2026), Replit (since 2018), Sourcegraph, Relace. So founders in hot categories can find a16z already on a rival's board. The lab bets that put them early (Mistral, BFL, Periodic) depended on one partner who has since started AMP. AMP then took the next rounds of BFL and Periodic itself. What they would never do (inferred). Stay out of a hot category on principle, or pass on a big lab round because the price is high. The AI-hardware Machine Age Fund (Aug 2026) shows they will create a new vehicle rather than sit a cycle out. Notable absences from the portfolio page (checked 2026-11-10): Anthropic (held only through Midha's AMP PBC), Etched (the ledger shows a16z in the 2026 Series C and growth rounds per Etched), Physical Intelligence, Skild, Figure, Poolside, Crusoe and Lambda. Either the page omits some holdings, or a16z has no position. For humanoid and robot-foundation-model labs, they have Mind Robotics, Eka, Tutor Intelligence and Atoms instead.", "score": 11.33, "n": 15, "nk": 2, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Hardware went from a sliver of a16z deal flow to over 20% in a couple of years, which is the stated reason for the Machine Age Fund.", "q": "hardware startups grow from a small amount of deal flow to now over 20%", "u": "https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Hardware went from a sliver of a16z deal flow to over 20% in a couple of years, which is the stated reason for the Machine Age Fund.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "hardware startups grow from a small amount of deal flow to now over 20%", "source_url": "https://a16z.com/the-machine-age-fund/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Naval Ravikant introduced Sigil Wen to Chris Dixon in 2021; a16z led Conway first round five years later (Oct 2026).", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "We first met Sigil when Naval Ravikant brought him to an event", "source_url": "https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-conway-the-creator-of-underdog/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "a16z won the Krea A within the same week: Marc Andreessen personally flew in to close over dinner in Palo Alto.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Marc Andreessen flew from Malibu to close the deal over dinner", "source_url": "https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "hannah-seal", "name": "Hannah Seal", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why she wins: She leads seeds big enough to be an early A, either $34M for Wonderful or about $10M for Arondite. Then she has Index lead the next round within months: Wonderful's A came four months after the seed, and nexos.ai's A came nine months after an Index-led seed. Thesis: Enterprise AI fails on execution, not on model quality. So she backs companies with forward-deployed teams, local general managers and agents running in production. Constraints: Her stated remit is B2B software and enterprise AI applications. She has no named pre-training, lab or compute deals. Index's Flapping Airplanes A was linked from the Wonderful page, but no source names her on it. What she'd never do, from her own words: back founders who aren't in it \"for the long run\" or chase quick flips.", "score": 11.33, "n": 15, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Frames enterprise-agent go-to-market as a consumer-style land grab", "q": "A consumer-style land grab, but in the enterprise.", "u": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Frames enterprise-agent go-to-market as a consumer-style land grab", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "A consumer-style land grab, but in the enterprise.", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/from-assistive-ai-to-authoritative-systems-how-wonderful-is-taking-enterprise-ai-out-of-pilot-mode/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Founder screen in regulated/vertical markets: deep, specific market insight", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "I look for founders with a deep and specific insight of their market.", "source_url": "https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Index Origin II prioritises co-investing with seed funds, solo GPs and angels: a path for a small manager to co-invest", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "co-investing with seed funds, solo general partners, and angel investors", "source_url": "https://formventures.substack.com/i/101904558/form-updates", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "menlo-ventures", "name": "Menlo Ventures", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. Anthropic access, plus a fund (Anthology) that founders can take without running a formal raise. Menlo says Anthology gives founders \"access to a multi-stage capital fund without having to formally fundraise\". Demo days with Daniela Amodei and Mike Krieger, plus $25–30K in Claude credits, give it a sourcing funnel of \"thousands\" of applications. The double-down rule. \"In the case of a smaller initial investment, we usually aim to lead the next round\". Goodfire went from a seed in Aug 2024 to a Menlo-led $50M A in Apr 2025. OpenRouter went from a seed in Mar 2025 to a Menlo-led $40M A in May 2025 and then a B. Numeric went from a seed in early 2024 to a led $28M A in Oct 2024. Axiom went from a non-lead seed in Oct 2025 to a led $200M A in Mar 2026. Lovable went from a co-led B to a led C. Operator-partners. The AI bench is ex-builders: Tully (Splunk CTO), Redfern (Atlassian CPO), Kraning (Expanse CTO, Stanford ML PhD) and Das (Glean founding engineer). Founders get architecture-level engagement. Constraints. Anthology is strategically tied to Anthropic. Menlo says companies need \"some key alignment with Anthropic\", though not to build on Claude. That makes it awkward for labs competing head-on with Anthropic. Its frontier-lab checks have mostly been non-lead. Economic control of the GP entities sits with three managing members: Ganesan, Carolan and Murphy (Form D, 2025-08-28). What they'd never do (inferred). Lead a direct Anthropic competitor's mega-round. No such lead appears in the portfolio. Elorian (multimodal) and Nous (open-source) are its frontier-lab exposures outside Anthropic, and neither is on the public portfolio page.", "score": 11.33, "n": 15, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Anthropic made its first-ever direct investment in another company alongside Menlo in Goodfire's $50M Series A, so Anthology doubles as Anthropic's de facto corporate-venture channel.", "q": "making their first direct investment into another company", "u": "https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Anthropic made its first-ever direct investment in another company alongside Menlo in Goodfire's $50M Series A, so Anthology doubles as Anthropic's de facto corporate-venture channel.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "making their first direct investment into another company", "source_url": "https://menlovc.com/perspective/leading-goodfires-50m-series-a-to-interpret-how-ai-models-think/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Inflection IV is sold through the wealth channel: an iCapital feeder ('Menlo Inflection IV RCM Access Fund') reported $102M sold to 369 investors (first sale 2026-03-27).", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "iCapital - Menlo Inflection IV RCM Access Fund", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2126520/000212652026000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Fund XVII (Seed to Series A) and Inflection IV (Series B+) are the two flagships. The Form Ds name only Venky Ganesan, Shawn Carolan and Matt Murphy as managing members of the GP entities, which shows where economic control sits.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "A Managing Member of the General Partner", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2079450/000110465925084691/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "rak-garg", "name": "Rak Garg", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he runs an incubation machine that gets BCV in before the first financing. BCV Labs says it shapes ideas with founders \"before any funds are raised\". Contextual AI and Echelon AI were both incubated there, and BCV led Contextual's $20M seed. Second edge: a dense Atlassian-alumni and security-buyer network. Prophet Security and Index both trace to people he worked with at Atlassian (see Deal stories). What constrains him: his own portfolio is mostly enterprise AI applications and security, such as Norm Ai, Prophet, Straiker, Nebulock and Cogent. These are not labs. On the one lab in his portfolio, poolside, BCV followed in at the seed and only took a lead role at the Series B. He shares credit on every BCV CMS record with another partner (Enrique Salem, Aaref Hilaly or Slater Stich). What he'd never do: back a founder with a \"butts in seats\" hiring mindset. In his words, every such startup \"has failed\".", "score": 11.33, "n": 14, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "In Feb 2023 he predicted new chip architectures for foundation models, citing model parallelism on large on-chip SRAM (the Cerebras/Groq design), years before the inference-chip wave; no chip deal on record.", "q": "New hardware architectures emerge for foundation models.", "u": "https://rakgarg.substack.com/p/five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023"}, "items": [{"insight": "In Feb 2023 he predicted new chip architectures for foundation models, citing model parallelism on large on-chip SRAM (the Cerebras/Groq design), years before the inference-chip wave; no chip deal on record.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "New hardware architectures emerge for foundation models.", "source_url": "https://rakgarg.substack.com/p/five-predictions-for-ai-in-2023", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "His BCV CMS short bio (updated 2026-09-16) moved his mandate to cyber, space, defense and industrial capacity, while the SEO text still says 'AI incubations'; consistent with 2026 deals Mind Robotics and Oratomic.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "frontier of cyber-security, space, defense, industrial capacity", "source_url": "https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=%2A%5B_type%3D%3D%22team%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22rak-garg%22%5D%7Bshort_bio%2CseoMetadata%2C_updatedAt%7D", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Passed on leading the seed of a team BCV had known for years over scaling-friction worries; openly expects to pay a premium for the A instead.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "In the end, we passed on leading the seed.", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/vc-insights-2025-ai-trends-startup-growth-and-2026-predictions/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jane-street", "name": "Jane Street", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: they are a sophisticated end user of the thing they invest in. Jane Street's homepage says \"We believe deep learning is the future of quantitative trading\". Its ML page cites \"Tens of Thousands of high-end GPUs\" and a new Texas datacenter with 4,032 liquid-cooled GPUs. That lets it do something VCs can't: test hardware in production and then price the round. Etched says the $700M round \"was led by Jane Street after testing our hardware\". Structure (the key to everything else): private holdings flow through trading P&L. Bloomberg reports that investments in private companies and funds \"lifted its third-quarter trading revenue by about $830 million\" (Q3 2025), mostly from Anthropic. So there is no fund vehicle, no named deal partners, and deals are papered by outside counsel (Sidley's Michael Podolny and Graeme Waller on Etched). Two different plays, not one: 1. Compute-customer / strategic: Etched (bought the rack, then led), CoreWeave (~$6B cloud commitment plus a $1B equity purchase at $109/share, same announcement, 2026-04-15), and existing positions in Oxide (rack-scale compute) and Firmus (AI factories). 2. Capital-markets / balance-sheet: Anthropic, entered via the FTX estate secondary in March 2024 (~$100M, second-biggest buyer), then followed in Series E through H (per existing repo rows citing anthropic.com). Also Magic (Aug 2024) and the Thinking Machines seed (Jul 2025). Constraints: a trading firm's priorities come first. It is culturally closed; a WSJ-quoted trader who works on AI investments says \"We are culturally insular\" (WSJ 2026-06-19, via repost). No sourced instance of it being first institutional money in an AI company. What they'd never do (inferred, unsourced): take outside LP money, back an external venture manager, or lead a pre-product seed. Every deal found is either compute it can run or a late, priced round.", "score": 11.33, "n": 14, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Jane Street entered Etched at the unannounced $5B Series B (Dec 2025, Stripes lead) as part of a quant cohort with HRT and Two Sigma, seven months before Sequoia priced it at $10.3B. The 'Sequoia turned consensus' story skips the quants who were already in.", "q": "also included an investment from Jane Street, Hudson River Trading, Two Sigma", "u": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested"}, "items": [{"insight": "Jane Street entered Etched at the unannounced $5B Series B (Dec 2025, Stripes lead) as part of a quant cohort with HRT and Two Sigma, seven months before Sequoia priced it at $10.3B. The 'Sequoia turned consensus' story skips the quants who were already in.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "also included an investment from Jane Street, Hudson River Trading, Two Sigma", "source_url": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Private holdings are marked into trading revenue, not run as a fund: about $830M of Q3 2025 trading revenue came from private stakes, mostly Anthropic. That explains why there is no venture team, partner or LP base.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "lifted its third-quarter trading revenue by about $830 million", "source_url": "https://news.bgov.com/new-york-brief/jane-streets-trading-haul-juiced-by-surging-bet-on-anthropic", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Jane Street's latency needs (nanosecond packet turnaround, small fast models) explain why inference-specific silicon matters to it as a user.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "sometimes we care about turning around a packet in literally 100 nanos", "source_url": "https://signalsandthreads.com/the-uncertain-art-of-accelerating-ml-models/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "vinod-khosla", "name": "Vinod Khosla", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he moves first on contrarian technical bets and then doubles down fast. Examples: OpenAI in 2019 (Fortune), a $2M cheque to Symbolica before anyone else (Fortune), and the seed and A at Scaled Cognition. His brand as \"first VC in OpenAI\" also pulls in founders. What constrains him: KV fund cheques top out around $30M, so he uses SPVs for anything bigger (NJ memo). KV also refuses to credit deals to individuals, so his personal list is undercounted. He has now publicly attacked a KV portfolio company his own partner sits on (Factory), which shows his loyalties can override firm consensus (TechCrunch). What he'd never do: slow down AI for safety-regulation reasons. He calls China the real risk (essay). He also wouldn't avoid a bet because it is likely to fail.", "score": 11.33, "n": 14, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "He admits he doesn't follow Symbolica's category theory and uses researcher migration toward an idea as his diligence proxy.", "q": "when these really smart people gravitate to an idea, it's an important idea", "u": "https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/"}, "items": [{"insight": "He admits he doesn't follow Symbolica's category theory and uses researcher migration toward an idea as his diligence proxy.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "when these really smart people gravitate to an idea, it's an important idea", "source_url": "https://fortune.com/2024/04/09/vinod-khosla-former-tesla-autopilot-engineer-ai-models/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Intra-firm conflict: Vinod publicly attacked Factory (KV-led Series C, Rabois on its board) on X to defend Cognition, another KV company. The founder's personal loyalty can override a partner's board position.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "struggling second tier competitor", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/factory-ceo-just-accused-his-vc-board-advisor-of-spying-for-cognition/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Sagence (ex-Analog Inference) is a 2018 KV-led AI-chip seed where Vinod personally advises; an AI-inference-efficiency bet made five years before the LLM boom.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "is now acting as an advisor to Analog Inference", "source_url": "https://www.av.vc/blog/analog-inference-lower-power-higher-performance", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "molly-welch", "name": "Molly Welch", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why she wins: She worked on Google Brain \"collaborating with research leadership on commercial initiatives\". That background shows in who she backs. P-1 AI combines a DeepMind researcher (Aleksa Gordić) with ex-Airbus CTO Paul Eremenko, and its seed angels include Jeff Dean, Peter Welinder and Zak Stone. She also writes on her own Substack and hosts Radical Talks, which puts her in conversation with researchers such as Jim Fan and Ross Taylor. What constrains her: Radical splits deals by partner. Pre-training labs and compute sit mainly with Jordan Jacobs, Rob Toews, David Katz and Aaron Rosenberg, and the growth deals she is named on are co-credited. Generalist is co-credited to Toews, who also gave the quote, and Outset is co-authored with Richa Mehta. Her solo, bylined leads are mid-size checks into applications and engineering AI ($15–30M rounds). She is credited on Periodic Labs and World Labs, but Radical did not lead the Periodic round and Jacobs incubated World Labs, so she rides along on the frontier-lab names rather than originating them (inference from bylines and team pages). What she would not do (inference): Back a non-AI company. Lead an ownership-maximizing solo seed into a frontier lab: her own lab exposure came through rounds led by others (a16z on Periodic Labs; Jacobs on World Labs). Pattern: She leads the seed and takes the board seat, then lets a bigger firm price the A. NEA led P-1 AI's $50M Series A (2026-07-29) with no Radical named among the A investors, while she stayed on the board.", "score": 11.33, "n": 14, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Holds the P-1 AI board seat after leading the $23M seed; Radical not named in NEA's $50M Series A", "q": "joins Radical Ventures partner Molly Welch", "u": "https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Holds the P-1 AI board seat after leading the $23M seed; Radical not named in NEA's $50M Series A", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "joins Radical Ventures partner Molly Welch", "source_url": "https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026072916/engineering-ai-startup-p-1-ai-announces-its-series-a-financing-led-by-nea-adding-jeff-immelt-to-the-company-s-board/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Radical bio rewrote her Google role from policy/marketing (2024) to Google Brain research commercial work (2025)", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Google Research in public policy and marketing roles", "source_url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20240416012647/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Archived bio credited her on Datology and Solver; current bio drops both and adds Generalist, Outset, Periodic Labs", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Datology, Muon Space, P-1, Solver, Spara, World Labs", "source_url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20251012044858/https://radical.vc/team/molly-welch/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "stripes", "name": "Stripes (formerly Stripes Group; fund vehicles SG Growth Partners I-IV, Stripes V-VII)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Ken Fox's New York growth firm, historically consumer brands plus SaaS. It led Etched's $500M Series B at $5B post in December 2025. That was the first growth-style price on the company, about seven months before Sequoia's $10.3B Series C, which the market later credited as the moment Etched turned consensus. Stripes' own site names the deal team as Ken Fox, Bryan Gertzog and Charlie Carter. This answers the repo's open question of who the Stripes partner on Etched was (it had been needs-id in etched-leads.csv). Stripes is not a co-investor type for THESIS.md (growth, no seed). It matters as a price-setter: it ran a systematic sweep of the compute layer and then made one concentrated bet before the brand-name firms did.", "score": 11.33, "n": 14, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Correction: Etched's $5B price was not secret until June 2026. Bloomberg reported it around 2026-01-13, still ~6 months before Sequoia.", "q": "Bloomberg previously reported that Etched raised a $500 million round", "u": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested"}, "items": [{"insight": "Correction: Etched's $5B price was not secret until June 2026. Bloomberg reported it around 2026-01-13, still ~6 months before Sequoia.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Bloomberg previously reported that Etched raised a $500 million round", "source_url": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-etched-says-jane-street-tsmc-linked-vc-invested", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Stripes names its Etched deal team as Ken Fox, Bryan Gertzog and Charlie Carter, resolving the repo's unidentified \"Stripes partner\"; who championed it is not stated.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Ken Fox Partner Bryan Gertzog Partner Charlie Carter Investor", "source_url": "https://www.stripes.co/investments/etched", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Style drift: in 2012 Stripes required $15M+ revenue and $3M+ pre-tax earnings; in 2025 it led a pre-volume AI chip company at $5B.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "minimum of $15 million of revenue and a minimum of $3 million", "source_url": "https://regents.umich.edu/files/meetings/03-12/2012-03-IX-3.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jan-hammer", "name": "Jan Hammer", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. Long, trust-heavy founder relationships that compound over a decade. Robinhood ran from a $500K seed in 2013 to a board seat until 2021. Those relationships turn into the next deal: Tenev's next company is Harmonic. The Alan portfolio visit is how Index met Mistral. Rohlik founder Tomas Cupr's next company is Duvo. Hammer also has a dense Czech and CEE network: Resistant AI, Rohlik, Productboard, Credo Ventures and Daniel Dines. Constraints. They are not a lab-sourcing specialist. On both frontier labs they followed someone else's lead: Lightspeed on Mistral and Sequoia on Harmonic. In mid-2024 they were reported as sceptical that new model companies could take on incumbents directly. What they'd never do. The record suggests they won't seek a lab cold, outside a founder relationship. Their frontier-AI exposure comes through people they already backed.", "score": 11.33, "n": 13, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Hammer holds the Harmonic observer seat; Harmonic co-founder Vlad Tenev is the Robinhood CEO whose board Hammer sat on ~2013-2021 (intro path is inference).", "q": "Index Ventures partner Jan Hammer, who will join as an observer.", "u": "https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Hammer holds the Harmonic observer seat; Harmonic co-founder Vlad Tenev is the Robinhood CEO whose board Hammer sat on ~2013-2021 (intro path is inference).", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Index Ventures partner Jan Hammer, who will join as an observer.", "source_url": "https://www.harmonic.fun/news/series-a/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "In July 2024, a year after the Mistral seed, Hammer was reported as sceptical that new model labs can take on incumbents head-on (FT quotes via aggregator).", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "we don't see any new model companies competing on their own turf", "source_url": "https://eulerpool.com/en/news/ai/index-ventures-bets-on-ai-23-billion-us-dollars", "source_type": "aggregator", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Views the model layer as capital-intensive; implies Index co-invests in labs behind others rather than leading.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "This is a pretty capital-intensive game.", "source_url": "https://eulerpool.com/en/news/ai/index-ventures-bets-on-ai-23-billion-us-dollars", "source_type": "aggregator", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "openai", "name": "OpenAI", "kind": "company", "fit": "medium", "crux": "OpenAI's money came in this order. First, founder-adjacent donors (2015). Then a small first cheque from Khosla, a Hoffman foundation and, it turns out, the University of Michigan endowment, all before Microsoft (2019). Growth funds came in through employee tenders (2021–2024), and crossover, PE, sovereign and strategic money set the price from 2025.", "score": 11.33, "n": 13, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "WashU's Scott Wilson uses a hypothetical OpenAI SPV syndication call as his example of negative selection; he prefers tenders and direct cap-table access.", "q": "$500 million of OpenAI that we're syndicating out", "u": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026"}, "items": [{"insight": "WashU's Scott Wilson uses a hypothetical OpenAI SPV syndication call as his example of negative selection; he prefers tenders and direct cap-table access.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "$500 million of OpenAI that we're syndicating out", "source_url": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:25:30", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "A university endowment (Michigan) held a $20M direct OpenAI position from before Microsoft's 2019 $1B; it surfaced only via Musk v. Altman exhibits and is reportedly worth ~$2B.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "The university made a $20 million investment in the early days of Open AI", "source_url": "https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The first-cheque group was sized at about $50M across Khosla Ventures and an entity called the Aphorism Foundation, per lawsuit exhibits reported by Business Insider via GovTech.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "with $50 million coming from Khosla Ventures and the Aphorism Foundation", "source_url": "https://www.govtech.com/education/higher-ed/university-of-michigan-expects-2b-windfall-from-ai-investment", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ravi-mhatre", "name": "Ravi Mhatre", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He co-founded Lightspeed in 2000 (Wikipedia, secondary) and has about 25 years of enterprise relationships. Those relationships turn into repeat-founder deals. Glean is the cleanest example: Lightspeed had known Arvind Jain \"for nearly 20 years\", through Riverbed and then Rubrik, before co-leading Glean's 2019 Series A (lsvp 2021, lsvp 2024). The 2023 reset. In April 2023 he and Bejul Somaia told partners worldwide to clear their calendars, \"Even board meetings.\" The trigger was that Lightspeed had no early OpenAI stake and had missed ElevenLabs and Hugging Face (Newcomer, 2026-10-08). Since then the firm's frontier bets carry his name. Lightspeed first engaged with Anthropic in January 2023 (lsvp), invested at the Series D in 2024 (lsvp), and led the Series E. What constrains him. On labs he is almost never first. Anthropic came in at the D. On Thinking Machines a16z led the seed (TechCrunch). On Ineffable, Sequoia was first reported as sole lead and Lightspeed became co-lead only at close (see Deal stories). His edge is conviction and fund size: Opportunity Fund III is $3.3B and Select VI is $1.8B (press release, 2025-12-15). Proprietary access to pre-founding researchers is not where he wins. Correction to the task premise: Mistral is not his deal. Lightspeed's Mistral page (seed, 2023) lists Paul Murphy, Antoine Moyroud and James Ephrati, not Mhatre (lsvp). European AI routes through Moyroud and Murphy. Mhatre's name appears on Europe only where the deal is very large or defense-related: Ineffable (with Moyroud) and Helsing (lsvp). What he'd never do. He would not back an app built for today's model. His founder filter is \"thinking in future tense\", which means designing for where model capability will be in six months (Lightwork, 2026-06-15; see Differential insight). No source states a hard \"never\".", "score": 11.33, "n": 13, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "Mistral is not his deal: Lightspeed Mistral page lists Paul Murphy, Antoine Moyroud, James Ephrati; Europe AI routes through Moyroud/Murphy.", "q": "Paul Murphy - Venture Partner", "u": "https://lsvp.com/company/mistralai/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Mistral is not his deal: Lightspeed Mistral page lists Paul Murphy, Antoine Moyroud, James Ephrati; Europe AI routes through Moyroud/Murphy.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "Paul Murphy - Venture Partner", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/company/mistralai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Core founder filter is \"future tense\": design for model capability 6-12 months out; he credits the phrase to Dario Amodei.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "we talk about founders who really are thinking in future tense", "source_url": "https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~42:05", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Says he borrowed the future-tense frame from watching Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei; lab proximity shapes his app-layer filter.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "I sort of borrowed that phrase", "source_url": "https://podcasters.spotify.com/pod/show/generative-now--ai-builde/episodes/The-SpaceX-IPO--Apple-Rebuilds-Siri--Anthropic-Releases-Fable-5-e3kqhr5", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~42:08", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "brandon-reeves", "name": "Brandon Reeves", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Two modes. Reeves is first in on compute, infrastructure and new-lab seeds that Lux leads: Hugging Face in 2019, the Together seed in 2023, Sakana's founding round in 2024, Phonic in 2025 and SkyPilot in 2026. They are a fast follower writing large co-led checks into frontier names that are already consensus. On Cognition, Lux came in after Founders Fund. On Flapping Airplanes, Index, GV and Sequoia did the $180M seed in January 2026, and Lux arrived at the $1B A about nine months later, at roughly 4x the seed price. What links the two modes is compute efficiency. Both SkyPilot and Flapping Airplanes are bets on getting more out of existing chips. On Flapping Airplanes, Reeves says \"getting more out of what we have\" matters for \"the next frontier\". The flywheel. Reeves recycles the portfolio. Clem Delangue (Hugging Face) angels into Sakana and Phonic. Qasar Younis (Applied Intuition, a 2017 Lux deal) and Erik Bernhardsson (Modal) angel into Phonic. Crosby was diligenced through demand from Lux portfolio companies. Constraints. They work inside Lux's multi-stage process. Many deals are co-signed with Grace Isford, so personal attribution is often unclear. Lux's own site does not yet list Flapping Airplanes on their page, and the Lux company page returns 404. Their deal role there rests on one press quote. Their public output is thin: no podcast, Substack or readable X content found. What they'd never do (inferred, not stated): I found no sourced statement. Their AI deal pattern shows no consumer AI apps; every name is infrastructure, a lab or an agent.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Lux was not first into Cognition: Founders Fund led the March 2024 Series A, and Lux co-led a later round with 8VC (Reeves is the only Lux partner on the Cognition page)", "q": "Lux Capital and 8VC (who jointly led our previous round)", "u": "https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents"}, "items": [{"insight": "Lux was not first into Cognition: Founders Fund led the March 2024 Series A, and Lux co-led a later round with 8VC (Reeves is the only Lux partner on the Cognition page)", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Lux Capital and 8VC (who jointly led our previous round)", "source_url": "https://cognition.com/blog/funding-growth-and-the-next-frontier-of-ai-coding-agents", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lux was not in the Flapping Airplanes seed (Index, GV, Sequoia; Jan 2026); it surfaced as co-lead of A talks ~6 months later and closed the $1B A ~9 months after the seed at ~4x the seed valuation. Same fast-follower pattern as Cognition (after Founders Fund)", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Index Ventures and Lux Capital are leading talks", "source_url": "https://runtimewire.com/article/flapping-airplanes-5-billion-valuation-funding-talks", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Reeves's attribution on Flapping Airplanes rests on one press quote: Index's own A post names neither Lux nor Reeves, and Lux's site lacks the company (404) as of 2026-11-10", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "The winners in AI will be the companies that get the most", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/toward-more-efficient-ai-leading-flapping-airplanes-1b-series-a/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bucky-moore", "name": "Bucky Moore", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: a long run as a developer-infrastructure investor (Teleport, Labelbox, Browserbase, Bun, Together AI) gives him credibility with technical founders. Lightspeed hired him laterally to co-lead enterprise investing in AI, infrastructure and cybersecurity (, 2025-05-05). Constraint: at both firms his biggest AI names are shared credits. Mamoon Hamid bylined Kleiner's Together AI post. At Lightspeed, Ravi Mhatre is co-listed on every one of his four AI company pages. He has no clean solo frontier-lab lead. What he'd never do: nothing found. No source states a stage, valuation or sector line he won't cross. Status risk: as of 2026-11-10, his Lightspeed team-member URL returns 404 and the team page doesn't list him. Company pages still credit him. That pattern usually means a departure.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Cartesia's A was Kleiner-led while Moore was there; Lightspeed's page dates its own entry to 2024, so Lightspeed credit to Moore is inherited.", "q": "We're thrilled to announce our $64 million Series A led by Kleiner Perkins", "u": "https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a"}, "items": [{"insight": "Cartesia's A was Kleiner-led while Moore was there; Lightspeed's page dates its own entry to 2024, so Lightspeed credit to Moore is inherited.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "We're thrilled to announce our $64 million Series A led by Kleiner Perkins", "source_url": "https://cartesia.ai/blog/series-a", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lightspeed removed his team-member profile (404) and team-page listing by 2026-11-10 while company pages still credit him: likely departure, destination unknown.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "Bucky Moore joins us after eleven years as a venture investor", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Moore led Cartesia and backed Nexthop at Kleiner while co-investing with Lightspeed, then joined Lightspeed in 2025: deals followed the partner.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "co-invested with Lightspeed on multiple occasions, including Nexthop, Cartesia", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/stories/welcoming-enterprise-partner-bucky-moore-to-lightspeed/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jaya-gupta", "name": "Jaya Gupta", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. They write the frame and then back the founders who fit it. The context-graphs essay with Ashu Garg named Maximor and PlayerZero as examples, then brought in \"100s of pitches\". Their sourcing is personal and early. They pushed for a meeting with a founder who wasn't raising (Docket), and they backed a former McKinsey colleague before Fulcrum had customers. Constraints. Every visible deal is co-signed by a GP (Ashu Garg or Joanne Chen). Their lane is Foundation's enterprise application layer. No pre-training, lab or chip deal is attributed to them. What they wouldn't do. Their own thesis says not to build where model providers will \"eat everything\". They favour workflow-owning apps with proprietary feedback loops over thin wrappers. That is an inference from their writing, not a stated rule.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Jaya sourced Docket by pushing for a meeting with a founder who was not raising.", "q": "I insisted on setting up a meeting", "u": "https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days"}, "items": [{"insight": "Jaya sourced Docket by pushing for a meeting with a founder who was not raising.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I insisted on setting up a meeting", "source_url": "https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/docket-from-idea-to-investment-in-20-days", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Fulcrum came through Jaya's McKinsey network.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "I first met Arjun Mangla when we worked together at McKinsey.", "source_url": "https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/building-the-context-graph-for-insurance-why-we’re-doubling-down-on-fulcrum", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Maximor rebranded to Hyphenate and raised a $45M A within about a year of the Foundation-co-led $9M seed; Foundation followed on.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Hyphenate recently closed a $45M Series A", "source_url": "https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/always-audit-ready-how-hyphenate-s-founders-are-teaching-ai-to-run-finance", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "mario-gabriele", "name": "Mario Gabriele", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "How they win: access, not capital. Founders sit for long-form interviews before they are famous, and in 2026 The Generalist interviewed lab founders. They turned the publication into a partner seat. In 2023 Mario profiled Hummingbird from the outside; by 2026 they are listed as a Partner (team). Constraint 1: no confirmed deal authority. No deal is attributed to them at Hummingbird, and their own vehicle, Generalist Capital, has only one sourced position (Privy). Constraint 2: the independent-fund route met resistance. In 2022 at least one fund-of-funds, Nomads, declined Generalist Capital. What they'd never do: claim to explain founders with a formula. Their founder-formation series disclaims statistical generalization. That fits Hummingbird's \"outlier person\" thesis rather than a checklist approach.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Admits underestimating the lag between AI-for-science discovery and usefulness", "q": "I underestimated the gap between discovery and utility", "u": "https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory"}, "items": [{"insight": "Admits underestimating the lag between AI-for-science discovery and usefulness", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "I underestimated the gap between discovery and utility", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Nomads FoF passed on Gabriele's fund; Gabriele later became a Hummingbird partner", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Nomads declined to invest in Generalist Capital.", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "LinkedIn post: met Flapping Airplanes CEO in person at the SF office earlier in 2026, before the $1B Series A; only sourced Hummingbird-person touchpoint on the deal", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "sit down with Benjamin Spector at this office in SF earlier this year", "source_url": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/mariogabriele/", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jeff-herbst", "name": "Jeff Herbst", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: the network. He ran NVIDIA business development for 20 years, founded its venture and acquisitions programs (\"more than 40 global investments and 20 acquisitions valued over $8B\") and led Inception (GFT bio). He says roughly 15,000 startups have been through Inception, which gives him reach among founders, VCs, bankers and lawyers (GCV 2023-09-21). GFT also brings in corporates (SK Hynix, Naver and others) as LPs and uses them to test and later sell to portfolio companies. Constraint: fund size and stage. Fund I closed at $140M (GCV 2023). First checks are $3M to $4M for 10-15% ownership, and he requires a built product and \"first customer traction\". As of Q1 2025 about half of committed capital was still undeployed (GFT Q1 2025). No Fund II appears in public sources (searched 2026-11-10). What he'd never do (sourced): back pre-product companies or chase hot rounds. His stated sweet spot is traction, and he rejects \"deal-chasing VCs\" (GCV 2023). He insists on \"reasonable valuations\" (GFT Q2 2024). A pre-product $600M lab seed fails both tests.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Robotics pivot: after years of not finding robotics deals that met criteria, GFT made a small April 2024 check into K-Scale Labs (garage-stage humanoid team), then led Anyware Robotics' Seed+ in Q1 2025.", "q": "we made a small investment in K-Scale Labs", "u": "https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update"}, "items": [{"insight": "Robotics pivot: after years of not finding robotics deals that met criteria, GFT made a small April 2024 check into K-Scale Labs (garage-stage humanoid team), then led Anyware Robotics' Seed+ in Q1 2025.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we made a small investment in K-Scale Labs", "source_url": "https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q2-2024-update", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Corporate LPs (~15-20% of Fund I, at least four, some with CVCs) are used to pressure-test startup business models before GFT invests, then as customers/partners afterward.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "We will test some of the business models on them before we invest", "source_url": "https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/gft-ventures-launches-with-140m/?format=md", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Slow deployment: a 2021-founded fund still had about half its committed capital undeployed in Q1 2025, with no Fund II found; GFT is mid-cycle and capacity-limited.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "approximately 50% of our committed capital remaining available for deployment", "source_url": "https://gftventures.substack.com/p/gft-ventures-q1-2025-update", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "kleiner-perkins", "name": "Kleiner Perkins", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win now: they have fresh, large capital plus a new flexible vehicle. Three Form Ds share the first-sale date 2026-03-23: KP XXII (early) at $995M from 145 investors, Select Fund IV (growth) at $1.92B from 153, and a new Flex I at $555M from 55. For comparison, KP XXI was $825M from 106 investors in June 2024. My inference, not sourced: Flex I is the kind of pool that lets an early fund write into $200M–$1B \"seeds\" and Series As for labs without wrecking early-fund construction. Every 2026 lab bet came after this close. Who decides: Ilya Fushman and Mamoon Hamid are the only two Executive Officers on all 2026 and 2024 Form Ds. Kleiner's team page titles almost everyone \"Partner\", so the Form D is the cleaner signal of who controls the firm. Lab-access pattern (inference from portfolio overlap): Kleiner has held Together.ai since 2023, listed on the pages of Hamid, Naganath and Cochinwala. Together is the company around Chris Ré and Tri Dao's Stanford GPU-systems lineage (repo context,). Both 2026 compute-lab bets come out of that lineage. Flapping's CEO Ben Spector came from Ré's Hazy Research lab, and Sail lists Tri Dao as an angel. No source says Together introduced either deal. Constraint: Kleiner usually joins frontier labs rather than founding them. It was a participant at Flapping, Discovery Loop, Etched and Applied Compute's $350M round, and it co-led only Mirendil's seed (with a16z). It leads when the company is an AI-infrastructure or RL-platform business at Series A: Sail's A and Applied Compute's $80M round. What they'd never do (inference): run rigid ownership discipline on lab rounds. Taking a non-lead slice of a $1B Series A at a reported ~$6B is an access trade, not an ownership trade.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Kleiner closed a $3.47B 2026 vintage on one date: early fund KP XXII $995M (145 investors), Select IV $1.92B (153), and a new Flex I $555M (55). Every 2026 lab bet came after this close.", "q": "995000000", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2115014/000211501426000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Kleiner closed a $3.47B 2026 vintage on one date: early fund KP XXII $995M (145 investors), Select IV $1.92B (153), and a new Flex I $555M (55). Every 2026 lab bet came after this close.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "995000000", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2115014/000211501426000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "A new Flex I vehicle ($555M, 55 investors) sits beside the early and select funds; plausibly the pool for mega lab rounds (inference).", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Kleiner Perkins Flex I, LLC", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2114646/000211464626000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "KP and Lightspeed took board seats at the second round only nine months after the first close; the 2022 $113M round added no new director, suggesting insiders (KP/Lightspeed) carried it.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Mamoon Hamid", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1772926/000177292619000003/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "sec-filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "nomads", "name": "Nomads", "kind": "lp", "fit": "low", "crux": "Nomads behaves like Hummingbird, but for GPs. It looks for non-consensus managers whose funds are small enough to return 10x. It judges them through interviews about how they decide, rather than through track-record checklists (Generalist, 2023-12-03). The binding constraint is fund size. Gort excludes managers with more than $250M under management. Podcast show notes describe the mandate as emerging managers \"with funds sub $150M\" (Swimming with Allocators, 2023-12-06; podwise summary). Things it would no longer do: back big-name prestige funds; it dropped Tiger, Addition and Northzone after its first fund; back highly diversified portfolios; back complex decision-making structures (Generalist).", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Flexible on worldview: backed Compound despite a very different, thesis-driven approach from Hummingbird.", "q": "It didn't matter, Nomads still invested.", "u": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird"}, "items": [{"insight": "Flexible on worldview: backed Compound despite a very different, thesis-driven approach from Hummingbird.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "It didn't matter, Nomads still invested.", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Diligence is an interview about how a GP decides and who they are, not a track-record checklist.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "\"It's much more like an interview format,\" Gort said.", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Strategy pivot: Fund I backed prestige funds (Tiger, Addition, Northzone); from 2021 Nomads backs insurgent funds only.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "Starting in 2021, Nomads began to focus exclusively on insurgent funds", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "pradeep-sindhu", "name": "Pradeep Sindhu", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He has founded two silicon/systems companies from a blank sheet: Juniper (router ASICs, 1996) and Fungible (the DPU, 2015/16). Few angels have that credibility with chip founders. His name on a seed round tells later investors a systems architect has looked at the design. He is in the same small technologist-angel syndicate as Andy Bechtolsheim, Atiq Raza and Lip-Bu Tan, and that group shows up together on AI-chip cap tables (Cerebras company page, eeNews 2021-06-07). What constrains him. He writes angel-sized checks. No check amount is disclosed anywhere. He has spent most of his life as an operator, and Microsoft employment (CVP Silicon as of Nov 2024) probably limits how visible and how large his outside bets can be. His second company, Fungible, raised $300M+ (FinSMEs/press) and was sold for a reported ~$190M (Blocks & Files). His record as a company builder is therefore one huge win (Juniper) and one acqui-hire-like exit. What he'd never do. Back a \"SmartNIC\"-style, me-too architecture. He has publicly dismissed bolting general-purpose cores onto a NIC as \"not innovation\" (Grey Beards transcript). He wants architectural first principles.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Mayfield appointed Sindhu Venture Advisor in May 2026, his first formal investing role, focused on semis/systems for AI infrastructure.", "q": "scaling the foundational infrastructure powering the AI era", "u": "https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Mayfield appointed Sindhu Venture Advisor in May 2026, his first formal investing role, focused on semis/systems for AI infrastructure.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "scaling the foundational infrastructure powering the AI era", "source_url": "https://www.mayfield.com/pradeep-sindhu-joins-mayfield-as-venture-advisor/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Mayfield relationship began at Davos 2010 and ran through a decade as Fungible founding investor/board member; explains the 2026 advisor role.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I first met Pradeep at the World Economic Forum at Davos in January 2010.", "source_url": "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/celebrating-latest-milestone-our-decade-long-pradeep-sindhu-chaddha/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Same technologist angel cluster (Bechtolsheim, Raza, Sindhu) backs Analog Inference/Sagence and Cerebras; KV led Sagence, so Sindhu's chip angel checks track the Khosla network.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "co-founder of Juniper Networks", "source_url": "https://www.eenewseurope.com/en/analog-inference-startup-raises-10-6-million/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "stephanie-zhan", "name": "Stephanie Zhan", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why she wins. She commits at formation and commits fast. At Skild she met the two CMU professors on a Thursday and Sequoia was their partner by the following Tuesday. At Ricursive, Sequoia led \"their very first round from the formation\". At Reflection, Sequoia calls itself the \"first and long-term partner\". Each of these seeds was followed within months by a $105M-$300M round from Lightspeed and others at a 3-5x+ step-up. Her filter, as her own deal posts put it: researcher-founders who already built the canonical system in their field (AlphaGo/Gemini RL, AlphaChip, CMU robot learning), pitched as a \"frontier AI lab\" for a category, plus a compute-bottleneck story (\"chip design is the compute bottleneck\"). Constraint. The edge was partly Sequoia's balance sheet and brand (Arc, follow-on re-ups in Skild's A, Reflection's B and Ricursive's A). Her new firm's size is unknown. If it's a concentrated fund, she can't anchor $300M+ lab rounds alone and will look more like Striker than Sequoia. What she'd never do (inferred). Nothing in her posts suggests late entry. Every AI deal she's named on is a seed or first round. Treat this as a pattern, not a stated rule.", "score": 11.33, "n": 12, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Five-day seed decision on Skild: met the two CMU professors on a Thursday during their initial seed raise; Sequoia was partnered by the next Tuesday.", "q": "I first met Deepak and Abhinav on a Thursday afternoon", "u": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Five-day seed decision on Skild: met the two CMU professors on a Thursday during their initial seed raise; Sequoia was partnered by the next Tuesday.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "I first met Deepak and Abhinav on a Thursday afternoon", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The Skild seed closed within days of first contact (Thursday to Tuesday), and the partnership stayed private until the $300M Series A in July 2024.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we were partners by Tuesday", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Sequoia committed to Skild within five days of first meeting the founders, during the initial seed.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we were partners by Tuesday", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "wouter-gort", "name": "Wouter Gort", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: a growth-investor's network (Atomico, then Greyhound alongside Pogos Saiadian on Revolut, Brex, N26, Marqeta and Ualá) plus close friendships with Hummingbird's managing partners, which is how Nomads came to sit inside Hummingbird in 2021 (Generalist). How he decides: an interview about how a GP makes decisions and who they are, not a track-record checklist. His own test question: \"How do you get to the best decisions as quickly as possible?\" (Generalist). Constraint: size. Nomads looks for funds that can return 10x and excludes managers above $250M AUM (Generalist). What he'd never do (again): back prestige funds for the aura. Nomads' first fund held Tiger, Addition and Northzone; he moved it to insurgent funds only from 2021 (Generalist).", "score": 11.33, "n": 11, "nk": 1, "nn": 6, "key": {"t": "Nomads' anchor of Koko Xs's Fund I bought a track record built pre-fund: the Etched seed check came from Koko's personal savings, so the anchor was a bet on the person, not look-through Etched exposure.", "q": "he was able to invest out of saving $140,000 from tuition and earnings", "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc"}, "items": [{"insight": "Nomads' anchor of Koko Xs's Fund I bought a track record built pre-fund: the Etched seed check came from Koko's personal savings, so the anchor was a bet on the person, not look-through Etched exposure.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "he was able to invest out of saving $140,000 from tuition and earnings", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2024/12/13/how-21-year-old-koko-xs-became-the-new-rising-star-solo-vc", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Moved from growth equity to fund investing in 2021, first with personal money, before Nomads merged into Hummingbird.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Gort stepped back from Greyhound and began investing his own money.", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "His core diligence question for GPs is about decision speed and quality.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "How do you get to the best decisions as quickly as possible?", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/hummingbird", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "aftab-mathur", "name": "Aftab Mathur", "kind": "person", "fit": "low", "crux": "(Added 2026-11-09.) Mathur sits on both sides of Temasek's venture machine. In 2025 he described himself as running the \"innovation investments group and the emerging technologies investments group\", which back VC funds and also invest directly. The edge is scale and patience: Temasek gets Series A/B deal flow and \"break out\" signals from the tier-1 VC funds it backs, then comes in directly at Series C/D with large cheques and can hold past IPO. The binding constraint is size. He says there is a minimum cheque, below which a deal is \"harder for us\", and he looks for companies that \"require a lot of capital\". So he would not write small early cheques himself; those go through funds. He also says Temasek should not be \"the only investor\" in early-stage technologies, and frames it as crowding in private capital.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 1, "nn": 8, "key": {"t": "As an LP, Temasek concentrates fund commitments in tier-1 GPs whose picking it has come to trust. This is a poor pattern for unproven emerging managers.", "q": "we tend to concentrate our capital with them", "u": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html"}, "items": [{"insight": "As an LP, Temasek concentrates fund commitments in tier-1 GPs whose picking it has come to trust. This is a poor pattern for unproven emerging managers.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "we tend to concentrate our capital with them", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q132", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Temasek reaches Series A/B only through VC funds it backs, then invests directly at Series C/D with larger cheques and can hold past IPO (House of Lords oral evidence).", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "that is when we will come in directly and write those larger cheques", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q130", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "A minimum direct cheque size gates Temasek's direct deals; below it a deal is 'harder for us'. The number was not disclosed.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "we need to be able to write a minimum cheque size", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q131", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "aqil-pasha", "name": "Aqil Pasha", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: he thinks in systems. He is a chemical engineer and a former AWS solutions architect who supported startup customers. He writes from the infrastructure operator's side: kernels, KV-cache, GPU scheduling and fleet observability. That is rare in a fund best known for fintech and enterprise software. What he underwrites: the software layer that raises utilization of hardware that already exists, not new chips or labs. On Standard Kernel the post argues the bottleneck \"isn't a hardware problem. It's a software problem\". His agent-inference post moves the metric from tokens per second to \"time to useful work\". Constraints: he is a VP, not a partner. All four portfolio companies on his team page are co-listed with partner Saaya Pal, and every \"why we invested\" post he is on is co-bylined with Pal. No source says he sourced or led any deal. Most of his deal volume is cybersecurity (Realm, Above, Pluto), not frontier AI. What he'd never do (inferred, low confidence): back a point product that open source is likely to absorb, or \"yet another monitoring dashboard.\" He names OSS absorption as the hardest question in GPU infrastructure.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "He argues KV-cache management, historically too narrow to fund alone, may now support standalone companies because of long-horizon agents", "q": "Historically, KV-cache management has been a difficult area to justify a standalone solution.", "u": "https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/"}, "items": [{"insight": "He argues KV-cache management, historically too narrow to fund alone, may now support standalone companies because of long-horizon agents", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "Historically, KV-cache management has been a difficult area to justify a standalone solution.", "source_url": "https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The main risk he names for GPU and inference infra startups is open source absorbing the feature, not incumbents", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "The harder question will always be what gets absorbed into the OSS ecosystem", "source_url": "https://jumpcap.com/insights/three-things-im-watching-at-kubecon-and-why-they-matter/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "His solo post makes inference optimisation for agents an explicit Jump Capital focus and invites founders in kernels, runtimes, KV-cache and agent-aware scheduling", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Optimizing AI Infrastructure across the stack is a core focus for us at Jump.", "source_url": "https://jumpcap.com/insights/inference-was-built-for-requests-agents-need-something-different/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "daniel-mulet", "name": "Daniel Mulet", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: He runs the researcher-cultivation side of Radical's \"company formation with academics\" model. He talks to researchers \"[for] three months [to] a year before they're ready to actually start the company,\" and uses conferences to recruit for portfolio companies and line up professors as diligence advisers. The Logic uses he/his for him. (2026-01-06) What constrains him: His remit is climate, life sciences, materials and deep tech, not frontier labs or compute. Many of the companies on his team page were led by more senior partners (Poutanen on Crossing Minds and Genesis, Teja on Aspect, Toews on Reka, Katz on CentML), so his own lead record is thin and recent. What he'd never do (inference): back an idea that a big lab will ship as a feature. On the last day of NeurIPS he tests whether ideas are feasible and whether big tech will build them first, citing 2017–2020 dev-tool startups that model makers made obsolete.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Mulet cultivates researchers for 3-12 months before they incorporate, so Radical's sourcing starts pre-formation.", "q": "We talk to these people [for] three months [to] a year", "u": "https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Mulet cultivates researchers for 3-12 months before they incorporate, so Radical's sourcing starts pre-formation.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "We talk to these people [for] three months [to] a year", "source_url": "https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Treats NeurIPS papers as stale (preprints out 6+ months); the conference is for meeting people, not discovering research.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "None of the research is new,", "source_url": "https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Value-add is recruiting: he pitches portfolio companies to researchers and tells founders to bring top recruits to Radical dinners (Artificial Agency, Cohere, P-1, Yutori founders attended).", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "bring the most impressive person you're trying to recruit", "source_url": "https://thelogic.co/news/neurips-research-artificial-intelligence-investors/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "duquesne-family-office", "name": "Duquesne Family Office LLC", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "The rule: Druckenmiller backs people, not strategies. Every disclosed outside-manager commitment goes to someone already in his network. PointState was founded by Duquesne alumni and got $1B in 2011. Castle Hook was founded by former Soros family-office staff and was his \"second-biggest investment in a hedge fund ever\" (Business Insider via Yahoo, 2016-06-10). The venture-side links follow the same pattern. He was an Abstract management-company investor and Fund I anchor, reached through an Ovitz/Andreessen intro chain (Colossus). He is a named Nova backer next to Andreessen, Kravis and Malka (kokoxs.com). His venture heuristic is talent flow: \"where the kids are going\" (Hard Lessons transcript via A Letter a Day #320). He delegates AI judgment to a young partner (CNBC 2024-05-07). The constraint: Bandwidth and personnel on the private side. Kevin Warsh ran Duquesne's private-equity investments (Barron's via itiger), and he left in 2026 to become Fed chair, divesting Juggernaut (CBS). No successor is named. Single-family mandate, no outside capital (aggregator: altss). What it would never do (inference): Run an open emerging-manager programme or respond to cold fund pitches. No source shows a commitment that didn't come through a personal or alumni relationship.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Nova names Druckenmiller in a billionaire-individual LP cohort that also bought the Flapping Airplanes A directly.", "q": "backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka", "u": "https://kokoxs.com/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Nova names Druckenmiller in a billionaire-individual LP cohort that also bought the Flapping Airplanes A directly.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka", "source_url": "https://kokoxs.com/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Druckenmiller's Duquesne Family Office joined Q.ANT in the Series A second close with an observer (Sue Meng), three months after the first close.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Duquesne Family Office invests in Q.ANT", "source_url": "https://qant.com/press-releases/duquesne-family-office-invests-in-q-ant-to-drive-sustainable-photonic-ai-infrastructure/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "AI judgment is delegated to an unnamed young partner, the likely gatekeeper for any AI manager pitch.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "My young partner was early with Nvidia.", "source_url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "together-ai", "name": "Together AI", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why it matters to THESIS.md: it sits where compute meets pre-training research. Salesforce Ventures backed it as \"at its core, an AI research company\". Its research bench includes Tri Dao (FlashAttention), whom Lux now calls a founder. Lessons for an early investor: the seed was a professor-plus-operator team built around Stanford's Chris Ré, and it came 11 months after founding. A seed-only firm (Lux) led it, and multi-stage firms entered six months later at a reported ~$360-565M. Current relevance: it is now a growth-stage neocloud. The useful part for this repo is the talent and angel network around it (Ré, Dao, Uszkoreit, Chintala), not the company as a deal.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Chris Ré introduced Vipul Ved Prakash to Ce Zhang; Prakash decided on the company almost immediately. Ré is the connective tissue of the founding team.", "q": "when Chris introduced me to Ce", "u": "https://www.latent.space/p/together"}, "items": [{"insight": "Chris Ré introduced Vipul Ved Prakash to Ce Zhang; Prakash decided on the company almost immediately. Ré is the connective tissue of the founding team.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "when Chris introduced me to Ce", "source_url": "https://www.latent.space/p/together", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Together is a founder feeder. Sail's CEO was its first kernels hire, and Kleiner, a Together investor, led Sail's A. Watching Together alumni is a sourcing channel for inference and kernel startups.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Neil was the first kernels hire at Together AI", "source_url": "https://www.kleinerperkins.com/perspectives/sail-the-inference-platform-for-long-horizon-agents/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Together's founders describe NVIDIA as both investor and partner: the GPU-cloud check carries supply-side help", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "NVIDIA is obviously motivated to help us, both as an investor", "source_url": "https://www.latent.space/p/together", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "tess-van-stekelenburg", "name": "Tess van Stekelenburg", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: Tess is a computational neuroscientist (Oxford and UCL) who led AI methods research before investing. At Lux they work as a company builder more than a check-writer. Lux's own Valthos page lists them under Founders, next to CEO Kathleen McMahon, not under partners (, fetched 2026-11-10). Decrypt says Valthos was \"co-founded last November\" by McMahon and Tess, which puts the founding around Nov 2024. How they source: EVOLVED, an annual AI-for-biology hackathon they run. It drew 200+ ML researchers, software engineers and computational biologists. Their 2024 write-up was co-authored with NVIDIA's Vega Shah, and teams got early access to ESM3 plus about $300K in compute and APIs from Modal, Together, LatchBio, AWS and NVIDIA. By 2025 it had become \"Evolved Technology, Inc.\", with Nebius H100s and a showcase at NVIDIA HQ. What constrains them: Every non-founder at Lux holds the title \"Partner\", so the title says nothing about seniority or check authority. Tess has no Lux investment announcement bearing their name: their \"Selected Investments\" section is empty. Their book is AI-for-bio, aerospace and \"atoms\". It is not pre-training, chips or general labs. What they'd never do (inference): treat computational biology as a near-term drug-asset play. Their own essay argues that ML methods barely matter today to getting a drug to an IND filing, and still calls for funding method R&D anyway.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "The aggregator's ESM3 'investment' is better read as a partnership: EVOLVED teams got early ESM3 access; no source names her on EvolutionaryScale's $142M seed", "q": "Early access to EvolutionaryScale's ESM3 (98B-parameter protein model)", "u": "https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design"}, "items": [{"insight": "The aggregator's ESM3 'investment' is better read as a partnership: EVOLVED teams got early ESM3 access; no source names her on EvolutionaryScale's $142M seed", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Early access to EvolutionaryScale's ESM3 (98B-parameter protein model)", "source_url": "https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lux's Valthos company page lists her in the Founders field next to McMahon", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Founders Kathleen McMahon Tess van Stekelenburg", "source_url": "https://www.luxcapital.com/companies/valthos", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Her sourcing funnel is the EVOLVED AI-for-bio hackathon: 200+ ML researchers, engineers and comp biologists, doubling yearly, ~$300K compute/APIs from Modal, Together, LatchBio, AWS, NVIDIA", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "over 200 machine learning researchers, software engineers, and computational biologists", "source_url": "https://www.luxcapital.com/news/evolved-2024---code-compile-cure-where-software-engineering-meets-therapeutic-design", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "utimco", "name": "UTIMCO (The University of Texas/Texas A&M Investment Management Company)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Decision-makers. Richard Hall (President, CEO and CIO) and Patrick \"Pat\" Pace (Senior Managing Director, Private Equity) make the calls. Pace is head of private equity and leads venture capital, buyouts and private credit. Delegation. The UTIMCO board delegates manager selection to staff. Investments come to a board vote only when a director has a conflict, as with Underscore.VC in 2016 and 59 North in 2026, or when limits are exceeded. Rule they live by (2026). Scale relationships with key partners. Staff argued the old limits were \"Sizing the investment for portfolio, not for the endowment\" and \"Leaving chips on the table\". In June 2026 the board: raised the single-manager limit from 6.0% to 7.5% of Total Funds; dropped the 3% per-regime cap; changed the ownership limit from 50% of a strategy's assets to 50% of a manager's total assets, approved per draft minutes in the 2026-09-24 book. Constraint. Private equity (which includes venture) is a 30% sleeve inside the \"Global Equity\" regime, out of an endowment of about $68B. Small venture managers are reached in bulk through funds-of-funds separate accounts (Cendana Longhorns, Congress Avenue TrueBridge) rather than one by one.", "score": 11.33, "n": 10, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Deals that fit the endowment but no asset-class box motivated the new Strategic Holdings Portfolio (0% target, 10% max), a home for odd-shaped opportunities.", "q": "Good for the endowment, but not this portfolio", "u": "https://www.utimco.org/media/53kfyh0x/book-board-of-directors-june-18-2026_for-web.pdf"}, "items": [{"insight": "Deals that fit the endowment but no asset-class box motivated the new Strategic Holdings Portfolio (0% target, 10% max), a home for odd-shaped opportunities.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Good for the endowment, but not this portfolio", "source_url": "https://www.utimco.org/media/53kfyh0x/book-board-of-directors-june-18-2026_for-web.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Staff argued old concentration limits sized bets to the asset-class portfolio rather than the whole endowment, under-sizing winners; led to June 2026 limit increases.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Sizing the investment for portfolio, not for the endowment", "source_url": "https://www.utimco.org/media/53kfyh0x/book-board-of-directors-june-18-2026_for-web.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Second framing of the same problem: limits made UTIMCO under-bet on its best relationships.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Leaving chips on the table", "source_url": "https://www.utimco.org/media/53kfyh0x/book-board-of-directors-june-18-2026_for-web.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "alexandre-momeni", "name": "Alexandre Momeni", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Momeni is the GC name that turns up both on Europe's frontier labs (Mistral, Black Forest Labs) and on Paris/Stockholm pre-seeds and seeds (Stilla, Tsuga). Their advantage is GC's balance sheet. GC can write a small first cheque, then return as a co-lead or with a growth cheque (Mistral: inception, then A, then co-led the €600M B). They are usually one of several GC names on a deal, never the sole public face. Partners like Hemant Taneja, Jeannette zu Fürstenberg and Elena Viboch are quoted or take the board seats, so Momeni's personal decision rights are unclear. They don't appear as lead on any frontier-lab round. In the labs GC is a follower (a16z led BFL's A) or the inside investor that doubles down.", "score": 11.33, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "GC's team page shows no title; the only primary-source title is from a portfolio company release, which calls Momeni Partner.", "q": "Partner of General Catalyst", "u": "https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a"}, "items": [{"insight": "GC's team page shows no title; the only primary-source title is from a portfolio company release, which calls Momeni Partner.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Partner of General Catalyst", "source_url": "https://www.tsuga.com/resources/blog/pr/tsuga-raises-35-million-series-a", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "GC led Stilla's $5M pre-seed in Jan 2026 (Momeni and zu Fürstenberg on the GC page); Stilla announced it was joining Meta eight months later.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "led by General Catalyst", "source_url": "https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Stilla's pre-seed was syndicated with European AI founder-angels (ElevenLabs, Lovable, Legora, Synthesia, Hugging Face), pointing to the network the deal came through.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Mati Staniszewski (ElevenLabs)", "source_url": "https://stilla.ai/blog/announcing-our-seed-funding", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "pierre-lamond", "name": "Pierre Lamond", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.33, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Feldman went to Lamond first because Lamond had backed SeaMicro; Eclipse then wrote its Cerebras memo in March 2016.", "q": "one of the first people they called", "u": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era"}, "items": [{"insight": "Feldman went to Lamond first because Lamond had backed SeaMicro; Eclipse then wrote its Cerebras memo in March 2016.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "one of the first people they called", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lamond knew TSMC CEO Morris Chang since 1963, which got Cerebras its fab meeting.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Pierre knew Morris Chang, CEO of TSMC", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Cerebras's first institutional contact was founder-initiated: Feldman went straight to Lamond, his SeaMicro backer, before Eclipse wrote its March 2016 memo.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Then Andrew reached out to me with an idea", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "chris-re", "name": "Chris Ré", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: He is a co-founder, not just an angel. He co-founded Lattice, SambaNova, Snorkel, Together, Cartesia, Rox and Engram Labs, and says he helped other founders \"before they formed their companies\". His students become founders or professors: Alex Ratner (Snorkel), Albert Gu and Karan Goel (Cartesia), Ben Spector (Flapping Airplanes), Sabri Eyuboglu (stealth, which looks like Engram). What constrains him: He is a full-time academic with a large conflict list. His page says \"Please assume I'm conflicted\". His check size isn't public, so his capital matters less than his introductions. What he'd never do (per others): GV's Dave Munichiello writes that he \"runs away from hype\" and avoids self-promotion. That is Munichiello's description, not Ré's own words (2020-08-25). For the owner: you can't get his allocation by competing with GV, Index or Lux. You get it by being someone he trusts to back his students. The pre-formation list on his own page works like a pipeline map.", "score": 11.33, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Ré publishes a list of labs he helped before formation (Flapping, Modular, Cognition, Simile, Radical Numerics, Ricursive and others): a forward pipeline map for GV/Index/Lux deals.", "q": "Founders that trusted me to help before they formed their companies", "u": "https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php"}, "items": [{"insight": "Ré publishes a list of labs he helped before formation (Flapping, Modular, Cognition, Simile, Radical Numerics, Ricursive and others): a forward pipeline map for GV/Index/Lux deals.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Founders that trusted me to help before they formed their companies", "source_url": "https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Ré runs his own investment vehicle, Factory, funded with friends and family; GV's Modular cap table lists him as 'Chris Ré (Factory)'.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "I built and own Factory with friends and family", "source_url": "https://cs.stanford.edu/people/chrismre/startup_advising.php", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "GV formally names Ré as an advisor who has co-worked 6+ companies; the mechanism behind GV's Stanford-systems compute book.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "a GV Advisor who has teamed with us on more than a half-dozen companies", "source_url": "https://www.gv.com/news/rox-ai-sales", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "uc-investments", "name": "UC Investments (University of California, Office of the Chief Investment Officer of the Regents)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Rule they live by. Hold few, concentrated positions. The 2024-25 report says they've \"reduced external managers by 90%\" and \"saved more than $5 billion in fees\". Its ten pillars include \"Less is More\" and \"Concentrate\". Managers that won't fully disclose economics get redeemed. Where the edge comes from. UC treats its own research system as a deal source (\"What Makes UC, UC\"). Bachher co-founded the AI Futures Lab with Berkeley professors Ken Goldberg and Fernando Pérez. That is how a $198B allocator ended up co-leading a Berkeley spinout's seed (LMArena). Constraint. The team is lean. The report calls it \"one-fifth the size\" of peers (exact comparison set not quoted here). It is also mostly a buyout/growth book: the private equity table is dominated by $100M–$400M buyout commitments. New venture managers have to win scarce slots, and most small slots have gone to UC-ecosystem or diverse-owned emerging managers. What they'd never do (stated). Copy the Yale or Canadian model: \"We're not trying to be like Yale. Or Canada.\". They also won't keep a manager that isn't transparent about fees.", "score": 11.33, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "The University of California endowment office co-led both Arena seed and Series A: an LP acting as lead.", "q": "led by a16z and UC Investments (University of California)", "u": "https://pulse2.com/lmarena-100-million-seed-funding-raised-for-ai-focused-open-community-platform/amp/"}, "items": [{"insight": "The University of California endowment office co-led both Arena seed and Series A: an LP acting as lead.", "type": "round-structure", "evidence_quote": "led by a16z and UC Investments (University of California)", "source_url": "https://pulse2.com/lmarena-100-million-seed-funding-raised-for-ai-focused-open-community-platform/amp/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Filing shows a small-check ramp for UC-tied seed funds: The House Fund went $1M (2016) to $5M (2017) to $40M + $15M Opportunity (2021); Collide $11M to $15M.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "The House Fund III 2021 40,000,000", "source_url": "https://www.ucop.edu/investment-office/_files/updates/pe_irr_06-30-21.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "table row", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "UC co-led LMArena's $100M seed with a16z and framed it as monetising open UC Berkeley research, i.e. directs come from UC-lab proximity.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "open AI research translated into real-world impact", "source_url": "https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025052112/lmarena-secures-100m-in-seed-funding-to-bring-scientific-rigor-to-ai-reliability/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "sail-research", "name": "Sail Research", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "A two-founder inference and runtime company for long-running agents (cheap, latency-tolerant token APIs plus persistent \"Sailboxes\" VMs). Sequoia says it backed them \"from day 0\". A Sequoia seed and a Kleiner Series A were announced together as an $80M package on 2026-06-24.", "score": 11.33, "n": 9, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Sail does non-NVIDIA kernel work on Intel Gaudi 3 while Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan is an angel; no partnership disclosed, worth probing as hardware-access angle.", "q": "Gaudi has the raw specifications to be exceptional at LLM inference", "u": "https://sailresearch.com/blog/improving-decode-throughput-gaudi-3"}, "items": [{"insight": "Sail does non-NVIDIA kernel work on Intel Gaudi 3 while Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan is an angel; no partnership disclosed, worth probing as hardware-access angle.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Gaudi has the raw specifications to be exceptional at LLM inference", "source_url": "https://sailresearch.com/blog/improving-decode-throughput-gaudi-3", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Hardware-agnostic arbitrage: in-house tool (HTDYM) picks cheapest chip per model, including Google TPU v6e.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "which helps us assess which chips are most cost effective to deploy models on", "source_url": "https://sailresearch.com/blog/tpu-v6e-gemma", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Sequoia (Reeder, Buhler) claims first-money status: partner from day zero, i.e. before the Kleiner A; seed date itself undisclosed.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "from day 0", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-sail-agents-without-limits/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "carlo-agostinelli", "name": "Carlo Agostinelli", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: He is a recruiter and connector more than a picker. His own X posts show him cold-pitching people onto the team and celebrating founders. Koko Xs runs the investment framework (see people/koko-xs.md). Carlo brings a Stanford '23 network and the Agostinelli name: his father Robert co-founded Rhône (, secondary bio). The family office invests in AI chips (Tenstorrent Series E,) and in defense robotics (the XTEND/JFB merger,). What constrains him: The filings show almost no outside money behind his vehicles. He appears on exactly five Form Ds. Four of them show $0 sold: AV1 Fund I ($20M target, never amended), Nova Venture Fund II ($100M target), Nova Flappy and Nova Venture Fund III. The fifth, Nova Select, took $5M from one investor (EDGAR full-text search). Nova therefore leads only small rounds ($10M Midas) and joins big ones through SPVs. What he'd never do (inference from his own post): Treat a high valuation as success. In 2024 he wrote that the advice \"equating a high valuation with success, is flawed and needs to stop\" (X, 2024-10-21). Nova Flappy, which bought into Flapping Airplanes' A at a reported $6B valuation, cuts against that stance.", "score": 11.33, "n": 8, "nk": 1, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Of the five Form Ds naming Carlo (AV1 I, Nova II, Nova Flappy, Nova Select, Nova III), only Nova Select shows money sold: $5M from one investor. The capital behind him is thin or undisclosed.", "q": "5000000", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153845/000215384526000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Of the five Form Ds naming Carlo (AV1 I, Nova II, Nova Flappy, Nova Select, Nova III), only Nova Select shows money sold: $5M from one investor. The capital behind him is thin or undisclosed.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "5000000", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2153845/000215384526000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "His pre-Nova solo vehicle AV1 Fund I ($20M target, Palm Beach) was filed the month Nova started and never amended from $0 sold. Looks folded into Nova.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "20000000", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2045537/000204553725000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The Agostinelli family office invests in defense robotics through public-market deals: it is a strategic investor in the XTEND/JFB merger alongside Eric Trump. Not tied to Carlo by any source.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Unusual Machines (NYSE: UMAC), American Ventures, LLC, Protego Ventures, Aliya Capital and Agostinelli Group", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2024306/000149315226006957/ex99-1.htm", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "christina-buchanan", "name": "Christina Buchanan", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they matter: NVentures publishes no team page (nvidia.com) and every NVentures release found quotes only Siddeek (see firms/nvidia.md). Buchanan's Emerald seat is the only public trace of who below the head actually covers a company (Emerald AI company page). What constrains them: a principal at a balance-sheet corporate VC. The pool can write nine-figure lab seeds (at least $250M into Ineffable, TNW), but no check, round or investment decision is attributed to Buchanan personally. checkcapacity: growth describes the NVentures pool, not them. What they would never do (inferred): sit as a voting director or lead-and-price. At Emerald, NVentures led none of the three announced rounds and Buchanan is listed as \"(Board Observer)\", one of five observers (company page).", "score": 11.33, "n": 6, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "A principal, not Siddeek, holds the NVentures observer seat at Emerald AI: the only sourced case of a sub-head NVentures person named on a portfolio company.", "q": "Principal, NVIDIA NVentures (Board Observer)", "u": "https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan"}, "items": [{"insight": "A principal, not Siddeek, holds the NVentures observer seat at Emerald AI: the only sourced case of a sub-head NVentures person named on a portfolio company.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Principal, NVIDIA NVentures (Board Observer)", "source_url": "https://www.emeraldai.co/team-members/christina-buchanan", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "NVentures never led an Emerald round yet holds a governance seat: the Nov 2025 extension was Lowercarbon-led with NVentures following, then EIP led March 2026. NVIDIA buys observer access as a follower.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "The latest funding round was led by Lowercarbon Capital.", "source_url": "https://pulse2.com/emerald-ai-18-million-seed-extension-raised-to-advance-power-flexible-ai-infrastructure/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "NVentures re-upped in three consecutive Emerald rounds in nine months (Jul 2025, Nov 2025, Mar 2026), each under a different lead; the March round was EIP-led.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "The round was led by Energy Impact Partners.", "source_url": "https://www.fortune.com/2026/03/31/emerald-ai-nvidia-fast-pass-data-center-grid-connects/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jump-trading", "name": "Jump Trading Group (with affiliate Jump Capital)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why Jump wins access: it is one of the few investors that can credibly judge low-level GPU performance work. Its site describes an ML stack that \"powers live inference and fast iteration\". For a lab selling \"performance per chip\" (Flapping Airplanes' Series A story), a latency-obsessed trading firm is a natural strategic name on the cap table (my inference; no source states Jump's rationale). The kernel-lineage pattern (non-obvious): Flapping Airplanes CEO Ben Spector is lead author of ThunderKittens (arXiv 2410.20399, 2024-10-27), co-authored with Simran Arora and Christopher Ré. Standard Kernel's team developed KernelBench (arXiv 2502.10517). Its first author is Anne Ouyang, again with Simran Arora and Christopher Ré. Jump Capital names Ouyang and Chris Rinard as founders. So the Jump family backs two companies from one lab's kernel work, and the two compete. Constraint: Jump Trading Group does not take outside money. Jump Crypto states it \"does not operate any business lines that accept funds from external investors\". The balance-sheet capital is principal money, with no public deal team, thesis or cadence. Jump Capital is the only part with a public team, and its checks are $1–4M and $8–15M, far below a $1B round. What they'd never do (inferred): lead a frontier-lab round. Both Jump vehicles show up as followers or strategic names in big lab rounds. The only round Jump is known to have led here is a $20M seed via Jump Capital.", "score": 11.33, "n": 6, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "Jump's affiliate Jump Capital led a $20M seed in Standard Kernel, a GPU-kernel company from the same Stanford Hazy Research lineage as Flapping Airplanes; Jump is on both sides of the kernel-lab bet", "q": "Fixing the software layer that's holding AI back.", "u": "https://www.jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel"}, "items": [{"insight": "Jump's affiliate Jump Capital led a $20M seed in Standard Kernel, a GPU-kernel company from the same Stanford Hazy Research lineage as Flapping Airplanes; Jump is on both sides of the kernel-lab bet", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Fixing the software layer that's holding AI back.", "source_url": "https://www.jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Standard Kernel's founders authored KernelBench with Chris Ré and Simran Arora, the same co-authors as Ben Spector's ThunderKittens", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "KernelBench: Can LLMs Write Efficient GPU Kernels?", "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10517", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Jump Capital frames its GPU-kernel seed as recovering idle compute value rather than buying more hardware", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "It is stranded capital being put back to work.", "source_url": "https://www.jumpcap.com/insights/why-we-invested-in-standard-kernel", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "mirendil", "name": "Mirendil", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "Mirendil is a frontier lab built around automated AI R&D. It was founded by Anthropic's Discovery and autoresearch leads, raised a $200M seed co-led by a16z (Matt Bornstein) and Kleiner (Mamoon Hamid), and is the clearest 2026 example of a lab whose product is the AI researcher itself,.", "score": 11.33, "n": 6, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Mirendil says one researcher pretrained Mirendil-Base in 3.5 months for under $10M of compute, with agents writing almost all of the code. If that holds, the seed mostly funds compute, not headcount.", "q": "AI agents wrote over 99% of the code", "u": "https://mirendil.com/news/pretraining-at-scale-without-a-pretraining-team"}, "items": [{"insight": "Mirendil says one researcher pretrained Mirendil-Base in 3.5 months for under $10M of compute, with agents writing almost all of the code. If that holds, the seed mostly funds compute, not headcount.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "AI agents wrote over 99% of the code", "source_url": "https://mirendil.com/news/pretraining-at-scale-without-a-pretraining-team", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The company's own announcement says NVIDIA and unnamed other investors came in after the a16z/Kleiner seed. The post-seed cap table is not fully disclosed.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "followed by an investment from NVIDIA among others.", "source_url": "https://mirendil.com/news/announcing-mirendil", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "The only press write-up with a valuation ($1B) was published unfinished, with a placeholder where the founder quote should be. That makes the valuation a thin secondary claim.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "TO COME: founder quote on plans for the capital and why these investors.", "source_url": "https://techfundingnews.com/ex-anthropic-researchers-raise-200m-just-weeks-after-quitting-to-build-ai-that-creates-better-ai/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "peter-xiaotong-zhou", "name": "Peter (Xiaotong) Zhou", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: he controls the platform. ADV Schedule A/B lists him on the adviser from 07/2020, as CEO and a 75%+ owner of the management LP, and as an equal 25-50% member of the GP LLC alongside Huber and Hochberg, who joined in 05/2022 (ADV). He signs every fund Form D and every 13F (EDGAR full-text). A decade of Coatue public and private books (2010-2020, site) gives him a crossover investor's comfort with leading capital-heavy hardware rounds at $10-20M. What constrains him: the core fund is about $297M gross (ADV), so his lead deals are mid-size A/B rounds, not the first check. In iPronics, Amadeus led the 2022 seed before him; in Elegen, a16z, 8VC and others were already on the cap table. Public voice: none found. Huber does the podcasts and is the named lead on Coworker and Zapata. Zhou is quoted only in two portfolio press releases (Elegen, iPronics). You can't read him from content; you have to meet him. What he'd never do (inference from portfolio): sub-$5M app-layer AI seeds. Every deal he is personally named on is hardware or biology infrastructure.", "score": 11.33, "n": 6, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Coworker's Form D lists Zhou as a director even though press credits Huber with leading the $13M seed; the AI-agent deal may be Zhou's seat.", "q": "Xiaotong Zhou", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Coworker's Form D lists Zhou as a director even though press credits Huber with leading the $13M seed; the AI-agent deal may be Zhou's seat.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Xiaotong Zhou", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2067496/000206749625000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Zhou personally signs the adviser's 13Fs; the mid-2023 public book ($97.4M) held NVIDIA, AMD calls, TSMC, ACM Research and Aehr next to China internet, then shrank to $3.4M by 2024-06-30 as venture took over.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Xiaotong (Peter) Zhou", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266123003043/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Hongkou Capital was a private pre-IPO investor in AI drug-discovery company Exscientia in 2021, alongside Farallon and Laurion, not just a 13F public holder.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "HONGKOU CAPITAL MASTER FUND LP", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1865408/000110465921114491/tm2119783d6_ex10-1.htm", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jill-chase", "name": "Jill Chase", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.33, "n": 5, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Chase led Magic's $23M Series A in Feb 2023, a growth fund taking an early-stage lead on a coding-model lab before the post-ChatGPT wave; CapitalG then followed in the B and the $320M round.", "q": "his lifelong passion for AI and effusive excitement for what he was building", "u": "https://capitalg.com/insights/magic-reimagining-software-engineering-with-ai/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Chase led Magic's $23M Series A in Feb 2023, a growth fund taking an early-stage lead on a coding-model lab before the post-ChatGPT wave; CapitalG then followed in the B and the $320M round.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "his lifelong passion for AI and effusive excitement for what he was building", "source_url": "https://capitalg.com/insights/magic-reimagining-software-engineering-with-ai/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "CapitalG co-led the $56M /dev/agents (Dreamer) seed with Index: a growth fund writing seed lead checks when the team is ex-Google/Android leadership (David Singleton, Hugo Barra). The team later went to Meta Superintelligence Labs.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "We’re proud to co-lead Dreamer’s $56M Seed round alongside Index Ventures", "source_url": "https://capitalg.com/insights/dreamer-the-operating-system-for-ai-agents/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "CapitalG's Series B lead came through Stripe: its investment post says it has long watched and backed Groom, linked to Groom's years as a Stripe executive and CapitalG's Stripe position. The growth lead's relationship ran through the business cofounder, not the research CEO.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "we've had the good fortune of observing and supporting", "source_url": "https://capitalg.com/insights/physical-intelligence-bringing-general-purpose-ai-into-the-physical-world/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "striker-venture-partners", "name": "Striker Venture Partners", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.33, "n": 3, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Striker Venture Partners I raised $165M from only 12 investors (Form D amendment 2025-10-30), implying a concentrated LP base behind the ex-CRV lab team.", "q": "totalNumberAlreadyInvested 12", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "Striker Venture Partners I raised $165M from only 12 investors (Form D amendment 2025-10-30), implying a concentrated LP base behind the ex-CRV lab team.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "totalNumberAlreadyInvested 12", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2084186/000208418625000003/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Striker (Gazor's CRV spinout) writes concentrated inception checks of $5-30M into AI and cyber founders. That is a direct template for lab seeds of the size I target.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "$5-30m on day zero", "source_url": "https://www.striker.vc/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Striker was in at inception, before Menlo and Altimeter joined as co-leads; Dealroom shows a Jan 2026 Striker-only tranche at $250M.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "2026, At Inception", "source_url": "https://www.striker.vc/portfolio", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "nvidia", "name": "NVIDIA (NVentures + corporate development + Inception)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win allocation: a lab that takes NVIDIA money gets a supply and co-engineering relationship with the only vendor that matters for training. Huang's own framing is that NVIDIA pays in its company's equity instead of issuing its own: \"we get a share of their company\" (Q3 FY26 call, 2025-11-19, third-party transcript). The 10-K says the circularity out loud. Its FY2026 private investments of $17.5B \"include AI model makers that purchase our products directly or through CSPs\" (10-K). Scale and acceleration (filings): private stakes went from $1.3B (Jan 2024) to $3.4B (Jan 2025), $22.3B (Jan 2026) and $47.9B (Jul 2026). Net additions were $17.4B in FY2026 and $31.0B in just the first half of FY2027 (10-K, 10-Q). Total equity investments were $99B, plus $25B committed, by 2026-07-26 (same 10-Q). Global Venturing counts more than 125 venture rounds across the balance sheet and NVentures since the start of 2025 (GCV, 2026-06-22). The two arms have different objectives, by Siddeek's own account. NVentures has \"a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't\" (GCV interview, 2024-03-22). So corporate development (Bhagwati) is purely strategic: platform and ecosystem first, returns second. Who actually decides on big deals: The Information reports that Bhagwati runs corporate development \"but Huang often sets prices himself\" (via Dealroom summary, 2026-10-07). The pool-2 decision-maker is effectively the CEO. Constraint: optics of circular financing. GCV says NVentures \"must avoid perceptions of acting as a closed loop that finances future Nvidia customers\" (GCV 2026). The filings now carry an OpenAI-specific risk factor and a $105B lease guarantee (see adjacent structures). What they'd never do: lead and price an early lab round on a board seat. In the repo NVIDIA follows the co-leads in almost every lab round. The exceptions are compute-tied strategic tranches: Reflection AI's Series B, reported as led by NVIDIA (SiliconANGLE), and Thinking Machines' March 2026 strategic deal (TechCrunch). This is inferred from the repo pattern (patterns/co-investor-gangs.md). For the owner: NVIDIA is a near-certain follower in a $600M-class lab seed. It is a signal and a co-investor, not a person to cultivate. No individual is publicly attributable to any round, so the relationship runs company-to-company, through the lab founders and Huang.", "score": 11.0, "n": 33, "nk": 0, "nn": 9, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "About 14 of NVIDIA's 53 2026 megarounds came through NVentures; the corporate balance sheet alone would rank third", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "NVIDIA without NVentures would still rank third, with 39.", "source_url": "https://dealroom.co/charts/nvidia-megaround-investor.html", "source_type": "aggregator", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "NVIDIA is a lead, not just a strategic tag-along, in 12 of its 2026 rounds (e.g. Volta seed+A co-lead, TML strategic)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Dealroom lists NVIDIA as a lead investor on 12 of its 2026 rounds.", "source_url": "https://dealroom.co/charts/nvidia-megaround-investor.html", "source_type": "aggregator", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Siddeek separates the arms: NVentures is returns-minded, while corporate development under Vishal Bhagwati is not, so big strategic checks are priced for ecosystem, not IRR.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "we have a returns focus as well, which Vishal doesn't", "source_url": "https://globalventuring.com/corporate/healthcare/nventures-sid-siddeek-interview/?format=md", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "zach-fredericks", "name": "Zach Fredericks", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he matters: He is an ex-operator who led product at Loadsmart from seed through Series D. He reads the AI buildout as a supply-chain optimization problem. The flexibility post says it plainly: \"That’s our background, so it’s what we know\" (, 2026-07-22). How he works: He writes a newsletter backed by expert calls, sharpens a thesis in public, and publishes a list of startup archetypes before the deals exist. Constraint: He is a principal at a seed and pre-seed firm whose energy lane only opened in 2025: \"In 2025, we’re expanding our focus to include energy\". His bio says \"He gravitates toward asset-light models\". I found no energy or data-center-power deal on any card yet. What he'd never do: Back an asset-heavy power developer for venture returns. In his words, \"most will earn infrastructure returns\", and the venture outcomes go to the software layer.", "score": 11.0, "n": 23, "nk": 0, "nn": 5, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "He acknowledges hardware efficiency work is done by internal colleagues (likely the compute practice) while he focuses elsewhere.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "by many smart individuals (including our colleagues)", "source_url": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-energy-intelligence-hypothesis", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Expert-call sourcing network includes a Microsoft data-center buildout executive.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "an executive responsible for the data center buildout at Microsoft asks", "source_url": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Hidden: a hyperscaler chief strategy officer signs nuclear/geothermal LOIs as options, expecting most never to operate.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "signing these LOIs freely, while assuming most will never reach operation", "source_url": "https://www.primary.vc/articles/the-gas-turbine-bottleneck-reshaping-energy-infrastructure-ex8qe", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "mercor", "name": "Mercor", "kind": "company", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Mercor is lab-adjacent rather than a lab. It supplies the human-expert data that post-training runs on, and General Catalyst's page describes it as \"organizing human intelligence to power the AI economy\". Its customers are named as Amazon, Google, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI and Nvidia, with an outsized share from a few of them, including OpenAI. For THESIS.md it matters in three ways: Who got in early. The answer is a Thiel-network, young-founder cohort (Thiel, the GC seed, Koko Xs). Benchmark followed with the A, then Felicis took the growth rounds. Talent supply. Mercor's alumni and investors sit in the same young-founder graph as Etched, Cursor and Decart (see Koko Xs, Prod and Nikhil Suresh below). Price discipline. The founder publicly attacked Sequoia's tranche pricing, a practice the same article ties to Serval's rounds. It is far past my entry point now.", "score": 11.0, "n": 22, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Foody publicly attacked Sequoia's two-tranche pricing, claiming many recent cases. Shaun Maguire admitted roughly five in seven years and called Mercor a miss. The same article ties the pattern to Serval's rounds, a company the owner likes, which is a price-transparency flag for co-investing alongside Sequoia.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "a half dozen rounds", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/08/mercors-brendan-foody-calls-out-sequoia-over-dual-pricing-valuation-tricks/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Sequoia's own partner quantified the tranche practice instead of denying it. This is a useful datapoint on how Sequoia prices hot AI rounds.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "approximately five times", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/08/mercors-brendan-foody-calls-out-sequoia-over-dual-pricing-valuation-tricks/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Sequoia passed. Shaun Maguire publicly called Mercor a miss amid Brendan Foody's 2026 attack on Sequoia's tranche-pricing practice, so a Sequoia entry looks unlikely.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "it was a miss for us", "source_url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/08/mercors-brendan-foody-calls-out-sequoia-over-dual-pricing-valuation-tricks/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "brown-endowment", "name": "Brown University Endowment (Brown Investment Office)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Leadership (named in full). Current CIO: Jane Dietze. She joined Brown in December 2013 as managing director and has been VP and CIO since 2018. She leaves at the end of 2026 to become the first CIO of the Fund for Science and Technology (FFST), a $3.1B Paul G. Allen-ecosystem foundation, starting 2027-01-04;. Incoming CIO: Joshua Kennedy (Brown '97). He joined in 2016 and has been deputy CIO since 2022, focused on \"selecting investment managers, constructing Brown's investment portfolio and managing direct investments\". Incoming deputy CIO: Peter Levine (Brown '95), managing director since 2019. Former CIOs: the outgoing CIO is Dietze. An earlier CIO was Joe Dowling; the source does not say whether he directly preceded her (likely, given the timeline, but unconfirmed). Kennedy says the program was in early transformation in 2016 \"under the leadership of former chief investment officer Joe Dowling\" and Dietze. The source gives no dates for Dowling's tenure or where he went next; those are unconfirmed. Rule they live by. They state three tenets: 1. Build a dedicated professional team. 2. \"Partner over the long term with investment managers of exceptional ability and high character.\" 3. Strong governance. The FY25 report says these \"remain unchanging\". Edge they claim. Patient capital. The FY21 report calls \"long-duration capital\" one of the rare competitive advantages, and venture is the asset class that rewards it. Constraints. Payout is 4.5–5.5%, and the Corporation has temporarily set it at the top of that range. The federal excise tax rises from 1.4% to 4% in FY2027. Private equity has been slow to return capital. Managers are screened for integrity and ESG. At $9.5B, Brown is roughly half the size of WashU or Michigan, so its fund tickets are probably smaller in absolute terms. This is our inference: no ticket size is disclosed. What they'd never do. Nothing explicit found. Reports stress manager \"integrity\" and \"high character\" as a gate, beyond returns;.", "score": 11.0, "n": 18, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Venture now exceeds buyouts: the FY26 audited statements show venture at $2.35B (from $1.81B) vs buyouts at $1.74B (from $1.83B). Venture grew ~30% in a 19.6% year.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Private equity funds employ buyout and venture capital strategies", "source_url": "https://controller.brown.edu/sites/default/files/A_744198F-1A_BrownUniversity_FS.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "AI exposure comes via real assets: the real assets book is increasingly digital infrastructure, a picks-and-shovels play on generative AI.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "Real Assets portfolio is dominated by ownership in digital infrastructure", "source_url": "https://investment.brown.edu/sites/default/files/Reports/INV_Endowment-Report-FY25_MP-4415.pdf", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The office worries that generative AI may disrupt the endowment investment model itself, not just its holdings, and is preparing to adapt.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "the investment model itself may be exposed to similar disruption", "source_url": "https://investment.brown.edu/sites/default/files/Reports/INV_Endowment-Report-FY25_MP-4415.pdf", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "periodic-labs", "name": "Periodic Labs", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "This is the THESIS archetype: a very large seed into a new lab run by frontier-lab talent. The valuation went from a reported $1.3B at seed to a reported $7.5B about eight months later. The lesson: access came through personal ties to the founders (an ex-OpenAI colleague at Felicis, and Midha via \"mutual friends\"), not through process.", "score": 11.0, "n": 17, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "The company names five firms plus four angels, yet at least six more claim a seat: BCV (own post), CRV, A.Capital, SV Angel, Sabrina Hahn, plus Khosla and Radical per The Logic. A mega-seed carries a long unlisted tail of small allocations, so a ~$20M check can get in without being named.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "BCV is investing in Periodic", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/we-must-know-we-will-know", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Founding staff has a Montreal/Mila lineage: Fedus did his PhD under Bengio and Larochelle, and Bahdanau and Agarwal joined. Toronto-based Radical's otherwise unexplained presence may track this, but that link is my inference.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Several founding staff have Montreal ties.", "source_url": "https://thelogic.co/briefing/radical-ventures-backs-periodic-labs-in-us300m-round-to-build-ai-scientists/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Founder-side signal that the reported AMP-led 2026 round closed before late September 2026 (Forbes in May only had advanced talks); terms remain reported, not announced.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "you just raised a new round", "source_url": "https://www.generalist.com/p/a-chatbot-walks-into-a-laboratory", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "tom-hulme", "name": "Tom Hulme", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: a 12-year London network (GV Europe founding partner, 2014) that gets them inception-round, first-money positions with proven or repeat founders. Examples: Q.ai: GV co-led the 2022 inception seed, and Apple bought the company in 2026. Tessl: GV co-led the 2024 inception round with boldstart. Its founder Guy Podjarny previously built Snyk, which is also on Hulme's list. Their frontier-lab view changed over two years: In 2024 they described funding a model as \"investing a few hundred million into a power station\". They expected value to go to incumbents with data and distribution, and said they'd struggle to invest in OpenAI at $90B. In 2026 they said \"80 or 90% of what we're doing is AI\" and pointed to the London DeepMind spinout wave. In May 2026 they co-led Recursive. I found no source that explains the change. A plausible read is that they distinguish commodity scaling labs from RL and self-improvement bets. That is my inference. What constrains them: GV has one LP, Alphabet, and Hulme shares the frontier-lab lane with Munichiello (Flapping, Modular). Hulme has no AI-chip or compute deal on any source. They co-lead rather than lead alone in large lab rounds (Recursive with Greycroft). What they'd never do (inferred): take diligence on trust from a big co-investor's name. They call that their angel-era mistake: \"Outsource discipline and due diligence to them\".", "score": 11.0, "n": 17, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "In 2024 Hulme compared funding a foundation model to building a power station: capital-heavy, quick to commoditize. In the same episode they said they'd struggle to pay $90B for OpenAI. Two years later they co-led a $650M round into a months-old lab.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "investing a few hundred million into a power station", "source_url": "https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/tom-hulme-lessons-from-a-24x-angel-track-record-275x-on-robinhood-making-billion", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "They see Google DeepMind alumni as the source of the new London labs, naming Recursive, David Silver's Ineffable and AMI Labs. The transcript does not say GV invested in Ineffable or AMI.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "these are all coming out of GDM.", "source_url": "https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "They place Recursive as an RL lab with a London node (Tim Rocktäschel, garbled in the auto-transcript) and a West Coast node (Richard Socher). That fits sourcing through the London DeepMind and UCL network.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "reinforcement learning companies like Recursive Super Intelligence.", "source_url": "https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-03-19/gvs-tom-hulme-35-of-the-worlds-ai-researchers-are-in-europe-and-80-90-of-gvs-deals-are-ai/transcript", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "jason-kelly", "name": "Jason Kelly", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he matters: in February 2026 Ginkgo plugged an OpenAI reasoning model into its robotic lab and beat a published biochemistry benchmark by 40%. Kelly took the conclusion that experiment throughput, not model IQ, wins science: \"What really let it break through wasn't that it was so smart\" (Sequoia Training Data, 2026-03-24). That puts him at the junction of frontier labs, AI-for-science and lab robotics. Few people in those categories have his operating data, about 50 robots and 50 scientists dogfooding them, scaling to 100. Constraint: time and conflict. Compound itself says \"99.9% of Jason's time is continuing to build Ginkgo\". Ginkgo sells autonomous labs and cloud-lab services, so any autonomous-lab startup is a potential competitor or customer. No deal names him personally. What he'd never do: back humanoids for the lab or \"digital twin\" simulation as a substitute for real experiments. He says no to humanoids in the lab and \"Oh, make a digital twin. No.\" (Sequoia). He also says he dropped the science half of the problem, design models, to focus on the engineering half, the lab (same source).", "score": 11.0, "n": 17, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "AI scientists with robot labs win on economics even if less capable: <5% of research spend goes to reagents today, ~90% in an autonomous lab, about 10x data per dollar.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "They can even be dumber than the scientists. I think they win", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Admitted mistake: Ginkgo pursued both AI design (science) and lab automation (engineering); he dropped design and now treats protein/genome models (Chai, Boltz, Arc Evo 2) as tools.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "I went after both initially, and I kind of took my licks for that", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Flat no on humanoids in labs; prefers track-plus-arm carts (RACs). Notable since Compound holds a stealth humanoid robotics company.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "There are much better ways to do that than, like, walk them bipedally", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/how-autonomous-labs-will-transform-scientific-research-ginkgo-bioworks-jason-kelly/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "mitimco", "name": "MIT Investment Management Company (MITIMCo)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "How they decide: Since 2014 MITIMCo has run a bottom-up, manager-centric model rather than top-down asset allocation. Asset class matters less than finding the exceptional person: \"it doesn't really matter what asset class it is\" (Seth Alexander, FCLT Global podcast, 2022-04-03). Why they win: They are willing to be first and sometimes the only institution in a manager. Half of their emerging-manager hires had no other institutional investors. Constraint: Venture is a tiered book of about 6–8 core, ~20 middle and ~3 experimental managers. Venture deployment fell from a peak of ~$3B a year to ~$1B (Ryan Akkina, 20VC, 2024-01-29; auto-generated transcript). What they'd avoid: Akkina's own advice is that one LP probably shouldn't exceed 10–20% of a fund. He prefers new venture managers who have at least some angel track record. Leadership. Current: Seth Alexander, President. His appointment was announced 2006-03-22 and took effect 2006-05-15. He came from the Yale Investments Office. Former: Allan Bufferd, treasurer of the MIT Corporation from 1999. He was MIT's chief investment officer for 19 years and became MITIMCo's first president in July 2004. The AI-CIO list says \"10 years at fund\", which conflicts with the 2006 start, so I did not use that figure. Venture/directs lead on record: Ryan Akkina, Global Investment Team (20VC E1109). Akkina is still listed as \"Global Investment Staff\" on the team directory (fetched 2026-11-09). MITIMCo removed investment-team titles in 2013.", "score": 11.0, "n": 17, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Venture book has three ticket tiers: ~6-8 core managers at $50-150M per fund, ~20 middle at $10-20M, ~3 experimental at ~$1M for very early managers (auto-generated transcript).", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "maybe six to eight core relationships we would refer to them as", "source_url": "https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/ryan-akkina-how-mit-builds-their-venture-fund-portfolio-how-mit-approach-direct-", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "Portfolio Allocation chapter", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The experimental tier writes ~$1M checks into managers who are very early; this is the realistic entry point for a new fund (auto-generated transcript).", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "we'll write a million doll check because maybe it's extremely early", "source_url": "https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/ryan-akkina-how-mit-builds-their-venture-fund-portfolio-how-mit-approach-direct-", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "Portfolio Allocation chapter", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "MIT passed on investing in OpenAI around when Sam Altman left YC; Akkina names it a mistake he thinks about often.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "we also had an opportunity to invest in open Ai and we didn't do it", "source_url": "https://rosetta.to/u/20vc/ryan-akkina-how-mit-builds-their-venture-fund-portfolio-how-mit-approach-direct-", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "Evolution of Ryan's Investing Style chapter", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "compound", "name": "Compound (compound.vc; formerly Metamorphic Ventures)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why it wins: it writes public, detailed theses before a category is consensus, which pulls in technical founders. The writing page says research is published to \"connect with the founders we want to back\". Its stated AI strategy is to back \"early-movers to fundamental breakthroughs\" or final movers and leave the crowded middle to multistage funds (2023 letter). The track record fits that strategy: Deepgram (deep-learning speech), Runway (AI creativity) and Wayve (end-to-end driving) are the letter's own examples. Constraint: fund size. Dempsey puts Compound among \"boutique early-stage firms with <$150M vehicles\". They also say their bar went up because portfolio companies depend on downstream capital (letter). The firm follows on (Orbital Series B), but its name disappears from later rounds at Prime Intellect ($15M Founders Fund round, $130M Radical-led Series A). What they'd never do: chase a consensus AI-application seed priced as a megafund call option. The letter says such seeds \"get bid up to very high prices\" and that their edge is \"n-of-1\" companies, not picking winners in crowded spaces. Dempsey is openly skeptical of the \"$1B on $4B round for a neolab that everyone believes is downside protected\" (, 2026-03-04). That is the owner's own market, described from the outside. Hidden details: 1. New funds in 2026 (Form D). Compound 2026, L.P. registered a $115M offering on 2026-03-11, about 1.6x Fund II's $73M. A new side vehicle, Compound Explorer I, L.P., registered $35M on 2026-08-10. Both reported $0 sold at filing, and both list only Dempsey and Hirsch as directors. The purpose of \"Explorer\" isn't disclosed. It came out the same month as the Reverie micro-grants and the Jason Kelly venture-partner announcement, so it may be an incubation or out-of-distribution sleeve (inference). 2. The firm admits its mistakes in writing. On 2023: \"we perhaps were too disciplined during 2023\". Fund II invested under $10M from Sept 2021 to Jan 2023 on purpose. It plans for 50–80% of each fund's investments to fail (letter). 3. Stealth AI positions. The portfolio page lists unnamed companies: \"Local Compute Co.\", \"Robotics Lab Co.\", \"Industrial/Military Humanoid Robotics Co.\", \"Retail Robotics Co.\", \"Minibinders Co.\" and \"Chemical Synthesis Co.\". A humanoid position stands out given the 2024 letter's skepticism of humanoids. 4. Possible path into Prime Intellect. Co-founder Vincent Weisser was previously Chief of Ecosystem and AI at Molecule (The Block). Molecule is a Compound DeSci portfolio company (letter). That the deal came through Molecule is an inference; no source states it. 5. The Zavain Dar thread. Dempsey and Dar (then at Lux) are both named on Runway's 2020 Series A. Dar later co-founded Achira through Dimension, and Compound joined Achira's $33M seed.", "score": 11.0, "n": 16, "nk": 0, "nn": 12, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Compound II was a $73M fund; first investments Sept 2021.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "of our $73M fund", "source_url": "https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Deployed under $10M of Fund II from Sept 2021 to Jan 2023, deliberately slow into the 2021 peak.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "invested under $10M from then until January 2023", "source_url": "https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Admits it under-deployed in the 2023 correction.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "we perhaps were too disciplined during 2023", "source_url": "https://www.compound.vc/writing/compound-annual-letter-2023", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "guru-chahal", "name": "Guru Chahal", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He spent about five years as fourth co-founder of Avi Networks before returning to Lightspeed in 2019. That gives him operator trust with infrastructure founders. Nexthop came from a relationship he says Lightspeed held \"for over a decade\". He also has Lightspeed's balance sheet behind him: he can lead a $110M A, a $300M A or a $500M B. What constrains him. On the frontier-lab deals he is usually not first and not alone. Ricursive: Sequoia led the seed, and Lightspeed came in at the A under two months later at $4B. Anthropic: Lightspeed's entry was the 2024 Series D. Almost every big AI memo is co-signed with Ravi Mhatre, Lightspeed's co-founder. Nexthop is the one memo he signs alone. What he would not do. Nothing stated outright. His pattern suggests he won't skip an energy- or efficiency-first thesis for a pure scaling bet: on Unconventional AI he wrote \"We’re scaling on power and cost, not efficiency.\". This is my inference.", "score": 11.0, "n": 16, "nk": 1, "nn": 3, "key": {"t": "Writes pre-research memos, not just deal posts: argues multi-agent RL in training may be the next scaling axis, with reward signal as the blocker.", "q": "The unsolved juggernaut appears to be signal.", "u": "https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Writes pre-research memos, not just deal posts: argues multi-agent RL in training may be the next scaling axis, with reward signal as the blocker.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "The unsolved juggernaut appears to be signal.", "source_url": "https://lsvp.com/stories/collective-model-intelligence-the-next-scaling-axis/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Networking thesis predates Nexthop by ~5 years: a 2020 post argued cheap merchant silicon, 5G and multi-cloud reopened network infra for venture.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "the network is the lifeblood of our economy", "source_url": "https://medium.com/feed/@guruchahal", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "At Avi Networks he was customer-facing, speaking with 150+ customers; his diligence style leans on buyer pain.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "I've had a chance to speak with over 150 customers about our technology", "source_url": "https://medium.com/feed/@guruchahal", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "stanley-druckenmiller", "name": "Stanley Druckenmiller (also \"Stan Druckenmiller\")", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins allocation: His name is a cap-table asset. Founders list him as an \"angel & advisor\" (si.inc), and Etched puts him on its public angel wall next to Karpathy and Hinton (etched.com). He openly says he outsources AI judgment to younger people. On Morgan Stanley's Hard Lessons (early 2026), he said he has \"young superstars in my firm,\" and that in venture \"we've always looked at ... where the kids are going\" (transcribed by A Letter a Day #320). The pattern in the deals: He is late and never leads. The only company where a source calls him the first investor is Toggle AI (now Reflexivity), and its co-founder Jan Szilagyi used to work for him at Duquesne (CNBC 2023-06-06). Everywhere else he arrives as a listed participant after institutional leads have priced the round: Perplexity: a \"new investor\" in the April 2024 B1, about 19 months after the 2022 seed (perplexity.ai). Etched: an unannounced 2024–26 financing, not the 2023 seed (TechCrunch). Standard Intelligence and Flapping Airplanes: the Series A in both. What constrains him: Bandwidth on the private side, which just got worse. Per Barron's, \"Warsh runs the firm's private-equity investments, according to Druckenmiller\" (Barron's via itiger, 2026-02-02). Kevin Warsh was nominated Fed chair on 2026-01-30 and confirmed in May 2026. He filed to stop consulting for Duquesne and to divest Juggernaut (CBS). The person who ran Duquesne's private investing has left, and no successor is named in any source I found. Public-market discipline over loyalty: He does trim AI winners and says so on air. He cut Nvidia because it \"tripled in a year.\" By early 2026 he said AI was \"not driving the engine anymore\" and called the cycle \"Maybe 8th inning\" (NBC/CNBC; A Letter a Day #320). What he would never do (inference): Underwrite a lab on his own technical judgment, or take a board seat. His own word for his edge on AI is \"luck,\" and he credits his \"young partner.\"", "score": 11.0, "n": 16, "nk": 1, "nn": 7, "key": {"t": "He is an LP in Koko Xs's Nova; Nova (seed, aggregator-listed) and Duquesne (Series A) are both in Flapping Airplanes, so his first exposure to that lab may have come via an LP stake.", "q": "backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka", "u": "https://kokoxs.com/"}, "items": [{"insight": "He is an LP in Koko Xs's Nova; Nova (seed, aggregator-listed) and Duquesne (Series A) are both in Flapping Airplanes, so his first exposure to that lab may have come via an LP stake.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka", "source_url": "https://kokoxs.com/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Druckenmiller and Warsh are co-named on Hebbia's round (and both bought into Abstract's management company in 2017, where Abstract took a large Hebbia check): inferred Abstract-to-Hebbia allocation path.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh, Julius Genachowski, Alexandr Wang", "source_url": "https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "His stated venture heuristic is talent flow: back where young engineers are going, not a technology view.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "that's something we've always looked at in venture is where the kids are going.", "source_url": "https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "vidu-shanmugarajah", "name": "Vidu Shanmugarajah", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: he compounds conviction by holding positions over years and doubling down. He calls developing \"unreasonable conviction\" the holy grail of the job, and says what earns it is watching a team execute over several years. Synthesia: GV joined at the 2021 Series B and led the Series E in 2026. Lawhive: GV led the 2024 seed, co-led the Series A and followed in the Series B. The Synthesia CEO's endorsement in GV's announcement: \"I know he'll always go to bat for us.\" What he backs: applied AI with enterprise-grade economics. On Synthesia, what impressed him was founders who were \"completely unromantic\" about where the AI actually works. His Buena post states the AI-native automation thesis outright: automate labor-intensive industries \"to achieve software margins\". What constrains him: he is a single European GP inside a single-LP corporate fund. His deals are application-layer (video, legal, hiring, property, security, NLP tooling), and none are labs or silicon. He says GV is happy with low AI gross margins but wants a path to better economics. GV's London frontier-lab deal (Recursive) sits with Tom Hulme. What he'd never do (inferred): back a founder who seems more interested in the success than in the problem. He tests for this with a \"murder detective\" line of questioning on detail. He also says he would not pin Europe's opportunity to one layer, model or application.", "score": 11.0, "n": 16, "nk": 0, "nn": 10, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Frames the job as building unreasonable conviction, earned by watching a team execute over years; explains doubling down from Synthesia B to leading the E.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "the holy grail of our job as VCs is to develop unreasonable conviction", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:00", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "What sold him on Synthesia at the 2021 Series B: founders realised AI should replace text (corporate training), not video, learned from hundreds of hours with customers.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "the best use of the AI wasn't where you were trying to replace video", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:04", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Wants tier-one AI teams that are unsentimental about where the tech actually delivers value today.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "completely unromantic about how you deliver that value in the real world", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EMhhrvpzTQ", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:05", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bain-capital-ventures", "name": "Bain Capital Ventures (BCV)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: they partner early and then follow on at growth with the larger fund. Merritt Hummer, at the 2023 close: \"Our early stage exposure is a tremendous asset for identifying winners\". In 2023 Kevin Zhang described Fund X as split roughly evenly between seed and growth (Fortune paraphrase). Their edge in AI infrastructure is relationships and research community, not lab-building. Crusoe came through Stefan Cohen, who now leads Bain Capital Crypto. BCV co-led the 2019 seed with Founders Fund, when AI was only a \"call option\". BCV Labs, Rak Garg's research and incubation community, produced Contextual AI and other seeds. Constraint: at the frontier-lab layer BCV is a participant, not a price-setter. It was not in Flapping's seed: Index, GV and Sequoia co-led it, and Lux and Index co-led the A. The Etched and Chai entries are growth-stage tickets. What they would not do: I found no sourced statement. No BCV-led frontier-lab mega-seed was found (absence of evidence only). The LP angle: BCV runs a fund of funds for emerging managers (Seed Fund Manager Program / \"Emerge\"). It has backed more than 100 managers. BCV Emerge Fund V filed a Form D on 2026-03-27 and amended it on 2026-10-14. For the owner, this is the most relevant thing about BCV.", "score": 11.0, "n": 15, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "A new flagship (Fund XI) and BCV Emerge Fund V both filed Form Ds in March 2026; Emerge amended in Oct 2026: both raising/deploying now.", "q": "Bain Capital Venture Fund XI, L.P.", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0002089217"}, "items": [{"insight": "A new flagship (Fund XI) and BCV Emerge Fund V both filed Form Ds in March 2026; Emerge amended in Oct 2026: both raising/deploying now.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "Bain Capital Venture Fund XI, L.P.", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0002089217", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "BCV entered Flapping at the $1B Series A, not the seed: its CMS portfolio record was created 2026-10-22, one day after the A announcement, ~9 months after the Index/GV/Sequoia seed.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Flapping Airplanes", "source_url": "https://0ystv7v4.apicdn.sanity.io/v2021-10-21/data/query/production?query=*%5B_type%3D%3D%22portfolio%22%26%26slug.current%3D%3D%22flapping-airplanes%22%5D", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "On Simile (Stanford generative-agents research spinout) BCV entered at the A and then doubled down, the same entry pattern as Flapping.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "We backed Simile at the Series A", "source_url": "https://baincapitalventures.com/insight/the-human-layer-of-ai-why-we-re-continuing-to-invest-in-simile/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "brian-zhan", "name": "Brian Zhan", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He is a technical investor whom researcher-founders vouch for. Reflection CEO Misha Laskin is quoted saying founders need investors who really understand the technical details, and that Zhan does. He shows up before the company exists. CRV dates Reflection as founded and partnered in the same month (Feb 2024), and Dyna as partnered (Aug 2024) before it was founded (Sep 2024). His self-told origin. At Facebook he watched strong engineers struggle to raise because they had no business metrics, so he started angel investing in former colleagues' projects. What constrains him. Striker I is $165M from 12 investors. It plans only about 10 companies, at up to $30M each. Three consequences: He can't lead a $300M-$1B lab seed alone. He co-invests at inception or comes in as a large participant. Every slot is precious. The CRV-era board seats were observer roles under Gazor's lead. What he'd never do (inference, not stated). Spray small checks or wait for revenue. Striker reports 100% of its investments were pre-revenue.", "score": 11.0, "n": 15, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Zhan backed Reflection AI's seed at a reported $200M valuation after most firms balked. That seed is now an $8B company, and it is the contrarian call his reputation rests on.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "多数机构望而却步，但Zhan力排众议出手", "source_url": "https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Striker's fund design: about 10 companies and single checks up to $30M, sized like Series B/C checks but written at seed. This is the closest analog to my $20M lab-seed model.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "该基金每轮仅投10家公司，但单笔投资最高可达3000万美元", "source_url": "https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Reflection CEO Misha Laskin publicly vouches that founders need technically fluent investors like Zhan. A lab CEO endorsing a then-junior investor is rare and is his sourcing moat.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "创始人需要能真正理解技术细节的投资人", "source_url": "https://news.pedaily.cn/202512/558249.shtml", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sarah-wang", "name": "Sarah Wang", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why she wins: a16z Growth gives her a balance sheet that can fund labs whose day-one needs are, in her words, \"growth scale\". She also sits close to Casado's infra team, so she reaches founders before the round is priced. One example: Cursor's CEO pitched the team in a16z's office in June 2024, before the August 2024 Series A. How she picks: she backs a \"very specific thesis\" about each lab's founder and team, not the whole category. She has said a16z is \"not invested in all of them\". What constrains her: she can't own many of these deals alone. Most lab and infra posts are co-signed by Casado, Jennifer Li or the firm's founders (Mind Robotics is the solo exception), and many of the bio deals have no byline from her at all. What she wouldn't do: in February 2026 she was publicly skeptical of robotics valuations. A month later she wrote the post on Mind Robotics, a founder-driven exception (RJ Scaringe of Rivian), so the skepticism looks aimed at the category, not at proven founders.", "score": 11.0, "n": 14, "nk": 0, "nn": 6, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Frames mega-rounds for new labs as hybrid: growth-scale capital from day one, but still an early-stage founder bet.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "it's still a tech founder bet, which obviously is inherently early stage", "source_url": "https://www.latent.space/p/a16z", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:01:20", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Says lab day-one resource needs justify growth-fund participation in seeds.", "type": "check-size", "evidence_quote": "the resources that they need day one are kind of growth scale.", "source_url": "https://www.latent.space/p/a16z", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:01:20", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Compute contracts worth hundreds of millions are negotiated by months-old labs; equity-for-compute is a live term.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "do you get equity for the compute?", "source_url": "https://www.latent.space/p/a16z", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:02:40", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "lee-fixel", "name": "Lee Fixel", "kind": "person", "fit": "low", "crux": "Why he wins: pricing judgement applied across stages. Scott Wilson, WashU's CIO, sums up the edge as \"disciplined around price, but will pay up for super high-quality assets\", plus underwriting that dissects business models and management teams. He also says Fixel steps away \"when things get too f- frothy\" (20VC, ~00:51–52 and ~00:36; Wilson speaking, not Fixel). The track record behind that reputation: the early Flipkart bet and Spotify at Tiger (ai-cio, 2019), and Tiger's lead of Peloton's 2014 Series B (TechCrunch, 2019-03-14). What constrains him: Addition raises $1.3–1.5B per vintage, with about $7B AUM by 2022 (Wikipedia, secondary)). Wikipedia describes one partner with final authority, so deal flow is bottlenecked on him. He has almost no public voice: I found no podcast, Substack, X account or talk of his own. What he'd never do: by Wilson's account, chase rounds he thinks are frothy. No source has Fixel saying this himself. Treat it as a second-hand trait, not a stated rule.", "score": 11.0, "n": 13, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Credited, as a WashU IMC director with the board and 'Adam', for WashU's SpaceX win: SpaceX once exceeded 20% of the portfolio and was held through the IPO via continuation vehicles. Governance credit; no source says Addition invested in SpaceX.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Lee and the board, and Adam should get a lot of credit", "source_url": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:13:00", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Harry Stebbings says Fixel, a mutual friend, told him to ask WashU's CIO about concentration limits, so Fixel helped shape the interview (Stebbings speaking).", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "he told me that I had to ask about concentration limits.", "source_url": "https://20vc.substack.com/p/20vc-newsletter-8th-november-2026", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~00:13:00", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Render founder Anurag Goel says Fixel opened their February 2020 meeting with two questions: what customers pay, and what a DevOps engineer earns. That prices the product against the salary it replaces, so unit economics come first. Founder writing; Fixel is identified via a link to his Forbes profile.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "what a typical customer pays for Render", "source_url": "https://render.com/blog/render-series-a", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "andrew-feldman", "name": "Andrew Feldman", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: a repeat team. Five of the six SeaMicro founding executives came back to build Cerebras. That track record took the risk out of the 2016 pitch for Benchmark and Eclipse. He then lands one anchor customer and learns from it, first G42 and then OpenAI. What constrains him: customer concentration. G42 was 87% of revenue in the 2024 S-1 and still 24% in 2025. The January 2026 OpenAI deal (750 MW through 2028, ~$10B) temporarily bars sales to Anthropic. He is now a public-company CEO facing short sellers: Culper Research attacked wafer lifespan, and Feldman answered in a 2026-11-05 post. What he'd never do (by history): give up wafer-scale. He kept the bet through ~18 months without a working chip while burning ~$8M a month.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 0, "nn": 5, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Board meetings in the pre-working-wafer years were a run of no-progress updates", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "Not yet. Not yet. Not yet.", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Win big customers by landing one anchor and learning from it, as with G42 and then OpenAI (20VC, quoted by Eclipse)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "first catch one and learn", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/how-an-unreasonable-pitch-became-the-backbone-of-the-reasoning-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Answers the Culper short report: wafers are retired for economics, not wear; <0.5% of CS-3 wafers decommissioned since 2024", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "Most silicon is retired for economic reasons", "source_url": "https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/what-it-took-to-build-wafer-scale-and-why-it-lasts", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bastian-hasslinger", "name": "Bastian Hasslinger", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win: Hasslinger comes to deals through operator networks. Index already backed two founder groups before their new companies: Nord Security's founders (nexos.ai) and Rohlik's Tomas Čupr (Duvo). The other cluster is ex-Palantir forward-deployed engineers (Ankar, Conduct, and Arondite's CEO). Index then leads a large first institutional round ($8M to $15M) and follows on. Their stated filter is founders who \"execute intensively and attract exceptional people\" (Index team page), plus a stated preference for ambition over category. Constraint: Hasslinger rarely signs alone. Six of the eight AI posts carry a second, usually more senior, Index name (Hannah Seal, Jan Hammer, Stephane Kurgan, Sahir Azam). On Oratomic they are only tagged, under Martin Mignot's byline. In two deals they were not the first institutional money: Creandum backed Conduct before them, and Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First backed Scope before them. Once Index has seeded a company, other funds have led the next round (Atomico led Ankar's A, Balderton led Duvo's A). What they'd never do (inferred): Lead a pre-training lab or chip company. Every AI deal on their page is the application or orchestration layer. Their only deep-tech exposure (Oratomic, Cowboy Space) comes through rounds led by other Index partners.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Three of Hasslinger's seven AI companies have ex-Palantir founders (Ankar, Conduct, Arondite CEO); Palantir alumni look like a repeat sourcing channel.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "previously built mission-critical software at Palantir", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/ankar-raises-20m-series-a-to-accelerate-and-protect-innovation/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Nomos came from a roughly 10-year relationship; byline is Hasslinger and Hammer, so which one is speaking is unconfirmed.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "I first met co-founder Stefan almost a decade ago", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-a-new-energy-model-for-europe-our-investment-in-nomos/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Hasslinger's book is a founder loop: Index re-backed Rohlik's Cupr (Duvo), and nexos.ai co-founder Okmanas then angel-invested in Hasslinger's Nomos deal.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "We backed Tomas Čupr when he built Rohlik", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/duvo-raises-15m-to-give-retail-teams-an-ai-workforce-that-goes-live-in-weeks-cutting-manual-work-by-40/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bryan-gertzog", "name": "Bryan Gertzog", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins. He is a growth underwriter with one repeatable instrument: lots of customer reference calls. On Orca (2021) it was \"CISOs we spoke with love Orca.\" On Island (2022) it was customer feedback \"some of the best and most consistent we've ever heard.\" On Etched (2026) it was customers calling it \"the first system that could truly transform their inference economics.\" He pairs that with a stage-in-then-lead playbook: take a small position, watch, then lead the step-up round (Snyk B to C; Island A to B). What constrains him. Ken Fox, the founder, sits beside him on the big-ticket deals (Etched, Cognition, Snyk, Orca, Island, Axonius) and holds the Snyk board seat. He works out of a ~$1B flagship (Stripes VII) and leans on SPVs (see firms/stripes.md). He has no public voice: no podcast, X account or bylined essays outside the firm's deal memos. What he'd never do: seed. Every one of his deals is labeled Growth or Early Growth on stripes.co. How far his remit has moved. His 2019 bio says he focused on \"software and restaurants\" (Snooze is the pancake chain). Six years later he co-led a pre-volume-production AI chip company at $5B. That is the firm's style drift, carried by one person.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Written stage-in-then-lead playbook: Stripes took a Snyk Series B position to watch, then led the $150M C. A Stripes check in round N signals interest in pricing N+1.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "monitoring its progress to later take on a larger role", "source_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240813193529/https://www.stripes.co/articles/developer-first-security-our-investment-in-snyk", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Same pattern at Island: Series A in 2021, then a larger Series B position in 2022 (memo co-authored by Gertzog).", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "deepen our partnership with Island after investing in their Series A", "source_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20241003234809/https://www.stripes.co/articles/the-first-enterprise-browser-our-investment-in-island", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "His diligence instrument is customer references (CISOs at Orca), the same instrument Stripes cites for Etched's inference economics.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "CISOs we spoke with love Orca", "source_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240525163314/https://www.stripes.co/articles/securing-the-next-big-attack-surface-our-investment-in-orca", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "seth-winterroth", "name": "Seth Winterroth", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins. He holds positions for a long time and sits on many boards. MarketScreener (aggregator) lists about eight director seats. He started in robotics early. Kiva was his origin story, and 6 River Systems was his first Eclipse deal. He stays in his best positions through very large later rounds: Oxide from seed to D over seven years, Wayve A and B, True Anomaly A through D. His network is prior colleagues in robotics. Kevin Peterson's route into Eclipse's Bedrock Robotics ran through him. Constraint. The Eclipse mandate is the physical economy. That excludes pure-software frontier labs, so his AI exposure comes through hardware. Compute hardware: Oxide, plus a board seat at the photonic-quantum company Quantum Source (aggregator). Embodied AI: Wayve, Foxglove, Bedrock. What he'd never do. Commercialize before the system design works. He warns founders against exactly that.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Not the Cerebras partner: his compute deal is Oxide, which Eclipse led from seed/A (2019) to the $445M Series D.", "q": "we led their seed and Series A rounds", "u": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era"}, "items": [{"insight": "Not the Cerebras partner: his compute deal is Oxide, which Eclipse led from seed/A (2019) to the $445M Series D.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "we led their seed and Series A rounds", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Bedrock Robotics co-founder Kevin Peterson reached Eclipse via a prior working tie to Winterroth.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Kevin had previously worked with Eclipse Partner Seth Winterroth", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/breaking-ground-with-bedrock", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Oxide D thesis: AI users want owned infrastructure rather than cloud for sensitive data.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "don't want to pour their most valuable data into infrastructure someone else controls", "source_url": "https://eclipse.capital/blog/built-for-the-compute-ownership-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "industry-ventures", "name": "Industry Ventures (now part of Goldman Sachs Asset Management, External Investing Group)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "IV wins by being the specialist wrapper for funds that are too small for pensions to back directly. Its deck tells LPs that a $125M average underlying fund \"is too small\" for many of them. It earns the edge three ways at once: primaries into sub-$250M seed funds (~40%), discounted early secondaries in those same funds (~20%), and directs/SPVs that use the small managers' pro-rata to concentrate in mid-stage winners (~40%). Sourcing is proactive and database-driven (\"Eighty to ninety percent of what we do is proactive\", Swildens, 2014). What it would never do is anchor a large, concentrated fund: the fund-size ceiling is structural. The new constraint is ownership. Goldman paid $665M plus up to $300M of earn-out to 2030, so future emerging-manager pools will be raised and governed inside XIG.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "IV pitches itself to pensions as the wrapper for funds too small for them to back directly; average underlying fund is about $125M.", "q": "For many Limited Partners, $125M average underlying fund size is too small", "u": "https://data.treasury.ri.gov/dataset/40fb544c-65c5-464f-ba49-e3f65d1adc36/resource/9405d2dc-20f3-43cd-8c52-9be772b6e985/download/2c-industry-ventures-partnership-holdings-viiersripublic.pdf"}, "items": [{"insight": "IV pitches itself to pensions as the wrapper for funds too small for them to back directly; average underlying fund is about $125M.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "For many Limited Partners, $125M average underlying fund size is too small", "source_url": "https://data.treasury.ri.gov/dataset/40fb544c-65c5-464f-ba49-e3f65d1adc36/resource/9405d2dc-20f3-43cd-8c52-9be772b6e985/download/2c-industry-ventures-partnership-holdings-viiersripublic.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "slide 12", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Primary fund commitments carry only 5% carry to IV LPs (10% early secondaries, 20% directs), so economics push IV toward directs and secondaries, not bigger primary tickets.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "5% primary commitments; 10% early secondaries; 10% SPVs; 20% direct investments", "source_url": "https://data.treasury.ri.gov/dataset/40fb544c-65c5-464f-ba49-e3f65d1adc36/resource/9405d2dc-20f3-43cd-8c52-9be772b6e985/download/2c-industry-ventures-partnership-holdings-viiersripublic.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "slide 14", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Hard ceiling on underlying fund size: primary commitments go only to seed/early-stage funds under $250M, which excludes a manager writing ~$20M checks into $600M lab seeds.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "Commitments to seed and early-stage funds <$250M in size", "source_url": "https://data.treasury.ri.gov/dataset/40fb544c-65c5-464f-ba49-e3f65d1adc36/resource/9405d2dc-20f3-43cd-8c52-9be772b6e985/download/2c-industry-ventures-partnership-holdings-viiersripublic.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "slide 8", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "jennifer-li", "name": "Jennifer Li", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why she wins. She is a product operator, not a researcher. She led product at Solvvy (acquired by Zoom) and ran self-service and analytics products at AppDynamics (acquired by Cisco for $3.7B). Her stated filter is that the tech is \"only like half or maybe 60% of the equation\" and founders still have to package it (GTMnow, 2026-04-28). She gets in years before the round and then backs the winner repeatedly. She met fal's founders \"three years ago\" before the 2025 Series B, and met ElevenLabs when it \"was two people in London\". What constrains her. Board load and scope: She sits on 9 boards (ElevenLabs, fal, Mintlify, Mux, Pylon, Rasa, Reducto, Stainless, Stacker) and observes on 5 more (Fivetran, Ideogram, MotherDuck, NX, Vantage). Her own deals are devtools, data infra and creative models, not pre-training labs. Lab-scale and compute deals on the Infra team sit with Casado, Midha and others. When she names OpenAI, Thinking Machines and SSI, she is describing the firm's book, not hers (GTMnow). She co-signs most big deals with Casado, Sarah Wang or Yoko Li. What she would never do. Nothing sourced. By her own framing, she would not back a research-only team with no product instinct.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 1, "nn": 5, "key": {"t": "Solo-bylined Preference Model (RL environments for frontier labs; founder Jennifer Zhou ex-Anthropic pretraining). Her most lab-adjacent bet; Mascorro's site claims the seed lead, so she likely inherited it after he left (inference).", "q": "As an early member of Anthropic’s team, she helped build", "u": "https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Solo-bylined Preference Model (RL environments for frontier labs; founder Jennifer Zhou ex-Anthropic pretraining). Her most lab-adjacent bet; Mascorro's site claims the seed lead, so she likely inherited it after he left (inference).", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "As an early member of Anthropic’s team, she helped build", "source_url": "https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-preference-model/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "fal round shape: a16z led the seed, let Kindred Ventures lead the Series A (a16z only participating), then co-led the B and took the board. She keeps the company rather than fight on price.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "The Seed round was led by Andreessen Horowitz", "source_url": "https://blog.fal.ai/generative-media-needs-speed-fal-has-raised-23m-to-accelerate/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Founder filter: tech is only ~60% of winning; the rest is packaging into a consumable product. Favors product-minded technical teams over pure research teams.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "that's only like half or maybe 60% of the equation", "source_url": "https://www.buzzsprout.com/2090897/episodes/19088734-vc-inside-a16z-s-1-7b-infrastructure-bet-jennifer-li-general-partner", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~28:37", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "pat-grady", "name": "Pat Grady", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: Two assets. (1) A 19-year SaaS growth book (HubSpot 2011, ServiceNow 2009, Okta, Qualtrics, Snowflake, Zoom) produced an operator alumni network that now founds AI-native apps and comes back to them for seed: Day.ai (HubSpot CPO), Agency (Drift founder), Paid (Outreach founder), Blockit (ex-Sequoia partner). (2) A thesis platform co-written with Sonya Huang: the GPT-3, Act Two, Act o1 and \"2026: This is AGI\" essays plus the Training Data podcast, which they host. What constrains him: They are co-steward of a firm that just cut back to seven GPs and closed Arc and Scouts. Two of their own seeds (Day.ai, Agency) came via Arc, so that funnel is gone. On AI their own publicly stated view puts the application layer ahead of models for venture returns. What they'd never do (on the evidence): Be the first check into a pre-training lab or AI chip company. No lab, chip or compute seed carries their name. OpenAI (2021 share sale) and Hugging Face (2022 Series C, Lux led) were late, non-lead entries. Trajectory (post-training tooling, Series A led 2026-11) is the closest they get to the research stack.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "On Training Data they press founders on whether general-purpose foundation models are commoditizing, a question that frames their app-layer bias.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "Broad domain, general purpose foundation models, they are commoditizing. Yes or no?", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-daniel-nadler/", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Blockit seed is a bet on a former Sequoia partner (Kais Khimji, partner from 2019) who pitched the idea internally first; Grady led the $5M seed and forecasts $1Bn+ revenue.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "This was his calling, and we were delighted to see him pursue it.", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/the-most-precious-resource/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Day.ai seed came out of the HubSpot network: the Sequoia author met both founders at HubSpot, visited when the company was a week old, and led the seed via Arc. Grady is the sole partner listed.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "But we knew Christopher and Michael were a team we could believe in", "source_url": "https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-day-ai-customer-obsession-productized/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "sarah-zeng", "name": "Sarah Zeng", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: their deal flow comes from their day job. Braintrust, the AI evals and observability company where Zeng is chief of staff, sits inside many AI teams' build stacks. Zeng invests in some of the same companies Braintrust buys from or backs. Braintrust is a Crosby customer. Zeng was the customer reference who described moving all of Braintrust's contracts to Crosby, and Snowy Leopard holds Crosby. Braintrust (the company) is listed as a Trajectory backer, and Snowy Leopard also lists Trajectory,. Network layers: an earlier investing career (firm unnamed), then a seat among Character AI's first 50 employees. What constrains them: Size: Fund I targets $6M and had sold $2.48M to 18 LPs at filing. Time: Zeng operates full time, so investing is part-time. Access, not ownership: checks into rounds like Flapping's $1B Series A are signal and access, with no meaningful ownership. What they'd never do (on the evidence): lead or price a round. No source shows a lead. The firm's list mixes \"Fund & Angel\" checks.", "score": 11.0, "n": 12, "nk": 0, "nn": 7, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "In Feb 2025 the (probable) author argued pre-training your own model was a 2021-era moat that no longer holds; by Oct 2026 Snowy Leopard is in Flapping Airplanes' A, suggesting they back efficiency-pitch labs rather than scale races (attribution: anonymous Substack matched by Braintrust CoS role)", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "In 2021, pre-training your own large language model provided a significant competitive advantage.", "source_url": "https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-primer-on-ai-agents", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Likes vertical, services-heavy data businesses that need ground operations to beat horizontal incumbents; frames Mercor as vertical search. Same shape as their later Crosby (AI-native legal) holding", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Acquiring this data may require on-the-ground operations.", "source_url": "https://thezeroshot.substack.com/p/a-new-opportunity-for-search", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Customer-to-investor path: Braintrust moved all its contracts to Crosby on Zeng's account; Snowy Leopard holds Crosby", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "migrate all of Braintrust's contracts over", "source_url": "https://www.new-ontologies.com/posts/Crosby", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bella-garcia-camargo", "name": "Bella Garcia-Camargo", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Added 2026-11-09. She wins on a narrow, network-sourced lane: Conviction hired her (start ~March 2025) to cover AI in \"cybersecurity or finance\", and her one public deal, Corridor, came out of founders she had already short-listed inside the Alex Stamos / Krebs Stamos Group / SIO security network that also referred her to the firm. The constraint: she is one of four investors at an eight-person firm, with no public sign that she leads lab, compute or chip deals; Conviction's hottest 2026 seed (Instinct) was Pranav Reddy's. No source says what she would never do.", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 0, "nn": 6, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Her path into Conviction ran through Alex Stamos: Mike Vernal's welcome post says Stamos (her Stanford professor) recruited her to his cybersecurity firm and referred her to Conviction. Pins her start to ~March 2025.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "recruited by her professor (@alexstamos – ty for the referral!)", "source_url": "https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Conviction hired her with an explicit sourcing lane: AI companies in cybersecurity or finance (her Krebs Stamos and Bridgewater history), not labs or compute.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "If you are building an AI company – especially in cybersecurity or finance", "source_url": "https://x.com/mvernal/status/1897751959970750796", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Pre-Bridgewater, as a 2022 Susa Venture Fellow, her stated interests were cybersecurity, industrials and defense, consistent with the later Conviction mandate.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "Isabella is excited about all things cybersecurity, industrials, and defense.", "source_url": "https://susaventures.substack.com/p/introducing-the-2022-susa-venture-fellows-class-4b3ddfe9d191", "source_type": "substack", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "igor-babuschkin", "name": "Igor Babuschkin", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "A lapsed investor who went back to building. After xAI he angel-invested in AI safety startups, got impatient waiting on other founders, and started River AI instead. He wins on credibility as a lab builder (AlphaStar, OpenAI reasoning, Colossus) and on his belief that labs are won by a critical mass of talent. He is constrained by a commercial gap: River needs its open-weights training API to pay its way before it can train frontier models. He'd never back a \"rent intelligence by the token\" model; he frames centralised closed labs as the problem.", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "After leaving xAI he spent the gap angel-investing in AI safety startups; that was the real activity behind the announced Babuschkin Ventures, not a raised fund.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "decided to do a bit of angel investment in AI safety companies", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:10:43", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "He quit allocating because backing others felt too passive; impatience waiting on founders is what pushed him to found River AI.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "I wanted to start something new myself so I can help push", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:11:19", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "xAI's origin was a chance meeting: he ran into Musk while visiting the Tesla office; Musk later reached out when starting the lab.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "randomly happened to be at the Tesla office", "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aBTpoCX_A8", "source_type": "youtube", "timestamp_in_source": "00:44:38", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "leigh-marie-braswell", "name": "Leigh Marie Braswell", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: they have engineering credibility from Scale AI, where they were an early engineer and the first PM on 3D annotation. Fortune describes them as having \"a reputation for backing technical winners\". Their usual move is to lead the Series A or an up-round once a technical team has early traction: Avoca A, Applied Compute $80M at $1.3B, Reevo co-lead, Nooks B. Constraint: they sit inside a multi-stage firm where Mamoon Hamid and Aditya Naganath own the lab and compute lane (Flapping Airplanes, Mirendil, Etched, Sail; see firms/kleiner-perkins.md). Braswell's page lists no labs, chips or compute. Partner-page credit is also shared. Bun's seed is credited to \"Bucky & Leigh Marie\", and on Reevo the Kleiner quote in the press is Hamid's. What they'd never do (inference): write the first check into a pre-product lab, or claim a moat that doesn't exist. They prefer \"head start\" to \"first-mover advantage\" because \"It's more intellectually honest\".", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Bun is not a clean Braswell win: the founder credits the Kleiner-led $7M seed to two people, and the partner page dates Bun to 2022, before Braswell's stated 2023 Kleiner start.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "We raised a $7 million seed round led by Kleiner Perkins", "source_url": "https://bun.com/blog/bun-joins-anthropic", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Braswell rejects 'first-mover advantage' language and prefers 'head start', a hedge on how durable an AI application lead is.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "I like the phrase 'head start' better", "source_url": "https://fortune.com/2026/04/27/avoca-ai-agents-missed-calls-hvac-plumbing-roofing-kleiner-perkins-chen-shrivastava-braswell/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Partner-page attribution overstates individual sourcing: on Reevo, which Kleiner co-led and which sits on Braswell's page, the Kleiner voice in coverage is Mamoon Hamid.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "They've flipped traditional CRM on its head", "source_url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/reevo-debuts-80-million-funding-171500872.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "vin-sachidananda", "name": "Vin Sachidananda", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins: He is a technical lateral hire. He spent about a decade as an AI researcher (Stanford EE PhD and MS) and as an engineer at Google, Apple, Amazon and two acquired startups. That background lets him write hardware and architecture theses most VCs can't, and it gives Radical, a seed-heavy firm, a compute and growth-stage partner just as its growth and Breakouts money arrived. What constrains him: He is new (since October 2025) and joined a partnership whose famous deals belong to Jacobs, Toews, Welch and Rosenberg. No Radical round is yet announced with him as lead or board member. His one named Radical deal, Etched, is a late entry at $5B or more that came through the firm's growth and Breakouts vehicles, not a first-in seed. What he'd never do (inference from his writing): He would not bet against transformers on generality grounds. He thinks the only reason to leave them is efficiency.", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "He names Etched's chip competitors in Radical's own memo and calls them far from shipping, a rare explicit competitive call from a VC.", "q": "MatX and Fractile remain a long way from delivering hardware to the market.", "u": "https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/"}, "items": [{"insight": "He names Etched's chip competitors in Radical's own memo and calls them far from shipping, a rare explicit competitive call from a VC.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "MatX and Fractile remain a long way from delivering hardware to the market.", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Two Sigma was in Etched's 2024 Series A while he was its AI/deeptech partner; after he joined Radical (Oct 2025), Radical entered Etched in 2026 at $5B or more and he wrote its memo. That he sourced Radical's entry is inference.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Etched has charted the fastest path to gigawatt-scale production", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/the-future-of-ai-inference-2/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Sachidananda's under-covered research bet is discrete diffusion LMs (consistent with his Inception Labs deal at Two Sigma).", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "An area that is still early but shows promise is discrete diffusion models.", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/vin-sachidananda-radicals-newest-partner-on-the-ai-opportunity/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "sam-teller", "name": "Sam Teller", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Teller is a channel, not a thesis-driven picker. As Musk's chief of staff he was the conduit between Musk and the OpenAI founders during the 2017 for-profit negotiations. 2026 trial exhibits show him relaying Musk's offer of a Tesla board seat to Altman. Shivon Zilis testified that she fed him information on what was happening inside OpenAI. His edge is trust and access inside the Musk network (Tesla, SpaceX, Valor, the people Musk's office hired), and now Thrive's capital. His constraint is the flip side: he has no public writing, no podcasts and no attributed investing thesis. Over roughly six years of investing (Valor, angel, Thrive), no deal is publicly credited to him as lead. No source says what he would never do. His 2012 interview stresses backing teams that have already built something, not ideas. (Added 2026-11-10.) A second constraint is structural. Thrive insists \"no individual name gets attributed to any deal\", so his Thrive-era record may never become visible. His access also predates OpenAI: he sat in on Musk's 2017–2018 meetings with PIF governor Yasir al-Rumayyan.", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Nov 2017 trial exhibit: Teller relayed Musk's offer of a Tesla board seat to Altman, and wrote that Musk hoped Altman, Brockman and Sutskever would help build Tesla AI; Teller was inside the OpenAI founding-era negotiations", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "remains very open to the idea of you joining the Tesla board", "source_url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/05/12/openai-trial-updates-sam-altman-set-to-testify-in-musk-suit.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Shivon Zilis testified she fed Musk and Teller information about what was happening inside OpenAI in 2017–2018; Teller was a conduit between OpenAI and Musk", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "sometimes providing information to Musk and Teller about what was happening at the company", "source_url": "https://abc7news.com/live-updates/elon-musk-sam-altman-live-updates-trial-enters-2nd-week/19036397/entry/19058213/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Teller hired Elissa Butterfield into Musk's office; she now drives Island Green Capital's (LA, ~$88M) xAI and SpaceX positions, showing how Musk-office alumni become access routes into Musk-company rounds", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "quien fue jefe de gabinete de Musk entre 2014 y 2019", "source_url": "https://www.forbesargentina.com/millonarios/de-asistente-elon-musk-inversora-spacex-historia-elissa-butterfield-n71483", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "stepstone-group", "name": "StepStone Group (incl. StepStone VC, ex-Greenspring Associates, and StepStone Private Venture & Growth Fund / SPRING)", "kind": "lp", "fit": "high", "crux": "One team, three products. StepStone VC does direct, fund and secondary investing on one platform. SPRING describes itself as combining those three \"under a single platform\" N-2. How the platform feeds itself. Primary relationships with managers produce co-invest and SPV allocations and secondary deal flow. The secondaries business ($3.3B Fund VI in 2024; Fund VII had raised $1.77B by 2026-10) gives managers' LPs liquidity, Form D. Constraint. The book is huge, so most of its dollars go to large franchises: Altimeter, Thrive, Glade Brook, 8VC, Vy, DST and Valor Equity. Where emerging managers fit. They enter through dedicated small-fund vehicles (VC Micro, Early Stage, Diversity) and through co-invest SPVs.", "score": 11.0, "n": 10, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "LP demand is shifting from large funds toward $50-300M vehicles, which is the band a $20M-check seed manager would sit in (auto-transcript labels every speaker \"Host\"; attribution to Somerville likely but unconfirmed)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "fund sizes that are probably more in the 50 to 300 million range", "source_url": "https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "1:04", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Spinout GPs get tracked before they launch and are underwritten selectively; a frontier-lab or big-fund spinout should expect pre-existing views on them (auto-transcript labels every speaker \"Host\"; attribution to Somerville likely but unconfirmed)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "you have to be very picky among the spinouts", "source_url": "https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "3:08", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "They pre-build views on likely spinouts so they can move quickly when one launches (auto-transcript labels every speaker \"Host\"; attribution to Somerville likely but unconfirmed)", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "So if a spinout happens, we come in with a prepared mind.", "source_url": "https://audioscrape.com/podcast/how-i-invest-with-david-weisburd/episode/e167-the-hardest-questions-limited-partners-ask-gps-wstepstones-hunter-somerville", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "3:08", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "austin-liang", "name": "Austin Liang", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: they sit on the deal team under Matt Jacobson, the partner who co-signs almost every post they are on. Through that they are on the most important AI growth positions ICONIQ holds: Anthropic (lead on F, co-lead on G and H), Braintrust (lead on B) and Omni (lead on B and C). They are also on the firm's rare seed bet, Whirl AI. What constrains them: they are never the senior name. Every post lists them after two to five partners or GPs, and there is no first-person writing, podcast or X presence from them. The capital is late-stage: ICONIQ met Anthropic in early 2023 but entered only at the $183B Series F in Sep 2025. On Sierra C and Databricks J, ICONIQ joined rounds others were already in. First in? Only once: Whirl AI, where ICONIQ led the seed (2026-03-31). On every other AI deal they are named on, ICONIQ came in at Series B or later, after a16z, Greylock and others. What they'd never do: nothing sourced. There is no first-person material.", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Only post crediting Liang personally is a seed: Jacobson names Liang as working partner on ICONIQ's Whirl AI seed lead, the only deal where ICONIQ was first in", "q": "with a special thanks to my partner on this investment", "u": "https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai"}, "items": [{"insight": "Only post crediting Liang personally is a seed: Jacobson names Liang as working partner on ICONIQ's Whirl AI seed lead, the only deal where ICONIQ was first in", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "with a special thanks to my partner on this investment", "source_url": "https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/unlocking-its-transformation-moment-our-partnership-with-whirlai", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "ICONIQ bylines only use GP/Partner/Investor; Liang's real rank (Principal) appears only in a DX photo caption, and later bylines still say Investor", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "ICONIQ Partner, Murali Joshi, and Principal, Austin Liang", "source_url": "https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/dx-joins-atlassian-powering-developer-intelligence-for-the-ai-era", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Post Liang co-signed: ICONIQ courted Anthropic ~2.5 years (early 2023) before leading the Series F at $183B; late entry, not first in", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Since we first met Anthropic in early 2023", "source_url": "https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/partnering-with-anthropic", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "david-katz", "name": "David Katz", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win: Radical turned GPU access into part of the product it offers founders, and Katz runs that function. Radical Reads (2026-10-13) says Katz's team tracks GPU availability, pricing and contract terms and connects portfolio companies with compute providers. That gives them a live, buyer's-side view of the compute market that few VCs have, and it is the obvious source of their infra deals (CentML optimises scarce GPUs; Crusoe builds AI data centres; Prime Intellect sells compute plus RL tooling). Firm power beyond deals: Katz is one of only four named people (with Jordan Jacobs, John Megrue and CFO Blair Bernholtz) on the Form Ds for both Fund IV and Growth Fund I, listed as \"Executive Officer\" Fund IV Form D Growth Fund I Form D/A. Together with \"leads the Investment Team\" and \"oversees strategic initiatives and operations\" on the team page, this points to an operating-partner-turned-COO-of-investing role rather than a pure deal partner. What constrains them: They came up as an operator and GTM partner, not as a researcher or a deal sourcer. Two of their three named deals came after other firms (Crusoe was first backed by BCV and Founders Fund in 2019; Prime Intellect raised a seed in 2024 and a Founders Fund round in 2025 before Radical led). Their public writing is thin and mostly about policy and the market rather than personal theses. What they'd never do (inference from their writing): bet on a single closed model stack. The Prime Intellect post frames lock-in to one closed system as \"business risk\".", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Katz runs Radical's compute desk (tracks GPU availability, pricing, contract terms; connects portfolio to providers) and per Radical's WSJ summary it has negotiated billions in compute deals since ~2021.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "has negotiated billions of dollars in compute deals over the past five years", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/how-agentic-activity-changes-the-risk-equation/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Katz is one of four related persons (with Jacobs, Megrue, CFO Bernholtz) named Executive Officer on Fund IV and Growth Fund I Form Ds; no other investing partner appears - he is an operating/fund officer, not only a deal partner.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Executive Officer", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2065455/000095010325005206/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "In Radical's Canadian compute-consultation submission Katz argued for subsidising compute anywhere (including foreign) and directing money to AI companies, not chipmakers or hyperscalers.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "Short-term subsidies for compute, regardless of its physical location", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/building-ai-infrastructure/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "gavin-uberti", "name": "Gavin Uberti", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why he wins. He is a kernels engineer, not a chip-industry insider. He started writing kernels at Xnor.ai at 17, then did the same at OctoML, and concluded that fast decode is about data movement, not math (EP.480, ~24:14). From there he made one narrow bet early: \"In 2022, we made a bet that transformers would take over the world\" (TechCrunch). He then hired \"legends\" (Mark Ross from Cypress, Brian Loiler from Nvidia's HGX/DGX team, Ajat Hukkoo from Broadcom and Intel) to cover his lack of tape-out experience (etched.com). Constraint. The model is concentrated and capital-hungry. It only works if transformer-family models stay dominant (\"under 100 models that actually mattered\", per a founder on EP.480, ~51:31), and it needed about $100M before a chip existed. Every major Valley investor passed on that ask in late 2023 (EP.480, ~01:02:43). What he'd never do. He won't build a general graph compiler or support arbitrary PyTorch/ONNX graphs; Etched chose kernels first instead (founder, EP.480, ~51:31). He also won't do a cautious test-chip-first plan. The founders' line is that they would \"go all the way\" rather than \"half-ass it\" (Wachen-voiced block, EP.480, ~01:04:22).", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Mark Ross (ex-Cypress CTO) sized the start-up cost before joining; recruited as chief hardware architect after the seed.", "q": "you're going to need two to three million dollars", "u": "https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356"}, "items": [{"insight": "Mark Ross (ex-Cypress CTO) sized the start-up cost before joining; recruited as chief hardware architect after the seed.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "you're going to need two to three million dollars", "source_url": "https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/gavin-uberti-real-time-ai-the-future-of-ai-hardware-invest-like-the-best-ep356", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~01:09:08", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Background is kernels and competition engineering: two-person FTC robotics team that beat 20-person teams.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "We were rated by OPR third in the world for software development", "source_url": "https://podscripts.co/podcasts/invest-like-the-best-with-patrick-oshaughnessy/etched-building-ai-hardware-to-make-inference-faster-and-cheaper-invest-like-the-best-ep480", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "~26:05", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Thiel Fellowship (2024 class) came about a year after Primary's March 2023 seed, so it did not legitimize the first raise.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Gavin J. Uberti ’25", "source_url": "https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2024/3/27/thiel-fellowship-startups-2024/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "kathi-wilhelm", "name": "Kathi (Katharina) Wilhelm", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why she wins: she does hands-on technical diligence with research founders. For Machenta she brought her own props into the lab to test one-shot robot learning. Track record: she pairs that with a DACH/industrial network from Index, where she led the EthonAI Series A (ETH Zurich PhDs) and was named alongside Danny Rimer on DeepL's $300M round. Constraints: she is new at Creandum, a European early-stage firm. Her public deals so far are $11M and $40M seeds, so she is a co-lead partner, not a $600M-lab-seed anchor. What she'd never do: nothing sourced. Her stated taste favors \"boring spaces\" and applied science (manufacturing, drug trials, robots) over model-layer consumer plays.", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "She did hands-on diligence on Machenta by visiting the lab and testing one-shot robot learning with her own props before co-leading the $40M seed.", "q": "We showed the robot the task once, with one object.", "u": "https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta"}, "items": [{"insight": "She did hands-on diligence on Machenta by visiting the lab and testing one-shot robot learning with her own props before co-leading the $40M seed.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "We showed the robot the task once, with one object.", "source_url": "https://creandum.com/stories/backing-machenta", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "At Index she led EthonAI's Series A as a later entrant after General Catalyst, Earlybird and Founderful, yet Index's company page credits only Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas, hiding her origination.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Katharina Wilhelm, a partner at Index who led the investment", "source_url": "https://sg.news.yahoo.com/index-ventures-backs-ai-manufacturing-080626804.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Her DeepL role is visible only in a standfirst; the byline and company page credit Danny Rimer alone, so she likely did DACH legwork under a senior partner.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Index Partners Danny and Kathi share why Index is leading a $300m round", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/fluency-is-currency-deepl/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "low", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "mike-volpi", "name": "Mike Volpi", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: A Cisco M&A and infrastructure operator background, plus fifteen years of Index board seats in open-source and data infrastructure (Confluent, Elastic, ClickHouse, Kong, Cockroach) (wiki). That gave them a founder and co-investor network that now lets Hanabi get into crowded rounds (Applied Compute, Traversal) as a name-brand small check rather than as a lead. What constrains them: Hanabi is never named as lead in any sourced round. The Index-era lead capacity has gone, and their time is split with legacy Index boards (\"conservó sus puestos en el consejo\") and corporate boards (Ferrari, Stanford Health Care). What they'd never do (inferred): Run a lead process for a $600M new-lab seed. Their Index pattern was model and infra companies at Series A, not pre-product research labs, and the one frontier-architecture bet on record (Cartesia) was a $22M seed.", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 1, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Volpi is personally named with both Hanabi co-founders on Traversal's seed+A (Sequoia/Kleiner led), the only Hanabi deal where any partner is named; Offutt/Thakur likely the deal-doers with Volpi as senior name (pairing inferred).", "q": "Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi", "u": "https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement"}, "items": [{"insight": "Volpi is personally named with both Hanabi co-founders on Traversal's seed+A (Sequoia/Kleiner led), the only Hanabi deal where any partner is named; Offutt/Thakur likely the deal-doers with Volpi as senior name (pairing inferred).", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi", "source_url": "https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Stanley Druckenmiller was a direct investor in the Hebbia Series A that Volpi led, alongside Kevin Warsh and Alphabet board members; Druckenmiller's direct AI exposure extends beyond Etched.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "another Alphabet board member, Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh", "source_url": "https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "On Cartesia's seed, Volpi publicly bet on state-space models as a successor architecture to transformers.", "type": "contrarian-view", "evidence_quote": "We believe Cartesia's SSMs can be that new architecture", "source_url": "https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-next-generation-of-real-time-ai-models-our-investment-in-cartesia/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "sonali-singh", "name": "Sonali Singh", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "A former Morgan Stanley banker who was new to VC as of Jan 2026 (\"I recently entered the world of venture capital\"), now working as the second or third name next to senior Sequoia growth partners (Pat Grady, Sonya Huang, Anas Biad, Andrew Reed) on fast-moving AI rounds. Her advantage is access and the firm's platform, not her own sourcing. No source shows her originating or leading a deal, and every listed company is a growth-stage or late-priced round ($1B to $7.5B valuations),. Her constraint: no check authority or own check size is public, and her writing is joint work with Huang and Grady, so her personal view is hard to separate from theirs. What she would not do, as far as the sources show: nothing found. She has stated no exclusions.", "score": 11.0, "n": 9, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Her X handle is @w1tness1ngh, confirmed by the byline of Sequoia's Aug 2026 'Own Your Intelligence' article she co-wrote with Sonya Huang and Pat Grady. The account was created 2024-11-15, with bio 'i work @sequoia'.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "By @sonyatweetybird, @gradypb, and @w1tness1ngh", "source_url": "https://x.com/sonyatweetybird/status/2089390902162895350", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "On Chai, her first diligence question was team composition: AI-bio needs chemists, biologists and AI researchers in one team.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "you need to assemble a kind of quadrilingual group of people", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "She framed biology as hard to verify. Founder Joshua Meier corrected her on air, saying wet-lab readouts make it one of the more verifiable domains. She also admitted Sequoia knows very little about biology. This shows a generalist growth investor relying on founders for technical judgment.", "type": "mistake", "evidence_quote": "biology is one of those inherently not so verifiable domains", "source_url": "https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/chai-discoverys-bitter-lesson-drug-design-is-another-scaling-problem", "source_type": "podcast-transcript", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "hof-capital", "name": "HOF Capital", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win: LP breadth sold to founders as a commercial asset. The partners page claims 240 \"industry-leading investors in our funds\" across 37 countries. Co-founder Onsi Sawiris's bio says he \"helps founders secure commercial traction by identifying synergies within the firm's global LP network\". In a crowded late round, that is the pitch for an allocation. The capital machine: Morgan Stanley Smith Barney is the placement agent on the flagship funds. GGI Fund II raised $300M from 236 investors (first sale 2021-07-09). Strategic Opportunities Fund I sold $500M to 204 investors (first sale 2024-12-17). Strategic Opportunities Fund II targets $1.5B and had sold $708.5M to 39 investors by 2026-08-31. On top of the funds, EDGAR shows about 60 single-purpose \"Growth Opportunity\" and \"XX Growth\" LLCs filed 2020 to 2024. That pattern (my inference) is deal-by-deal SPVs into named late-stage rounds. Constraint: they are a buyer of allocation, not a creator of it. The Anthropic position started as a secondary bought from the FTX estate in March 2024, not a primary round. In Flapping Airplanes they were one of ~20 Series A participants, not in the seed. What they'd never do (inferred): run rigid ownership discipline. A wealth-channel LP base wants logos, and the firm structure (SPVs, crossover team, \"Strategic Opportunities\" funds) is built for marking up brand names, not for owning 15% of a seed.", "score": 11.0, "n": 8, "nk": 1, "nn": 4, "key": {"t": "A dedicated compute vehicle exists: HOF Capital Compute Infrastructure L.P. sold $40M to just 4 investors (first sale 2025-11-13), with Elhaddad managing the GP. This is HOF's most lens-relevant pool.", "q": "HOF Capital Compute Infrastructure, L.P.", "u": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2096453/000209645325000001/primary_doc.xml"}, "items": [{"insight": "A dedicated compute vehicle exists: HOF Capital Compute Infrastructure L.P. sold $40M to just 4 investors (first sale 2025-11-13), with Elhaddad managing the GP. This is HOF's most lens-relevant pool.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "HOF Capital Compute Infrastructure, L.P.", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2096453/000209645325000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Strategic Opportunities Fund II targets $1.5B and had $708.5M from 39 investors at filing. First sale was 2026-08-14, nine weeks before the Flapping Airplanes A closed (adjacency, not proof of source).", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "HOF Capital Strategic Opportunities Fund II, L.P.", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2148780/000214878026000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "HOF Capital is a confirmed Flapping Airplanes Series A investor; Genesis partner Adam F. was a HOF investor to Sep 2025 (aggregator), a possible allocation channel (unconfirmed).", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "proud to back", "source_url": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-shmeizer-0543a12a/", "source_type": "x-post", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "max-rimpel", "name": "Max Rimpel", "kind": "person", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why they win. They are the working investor on GC's AI infrastructure and research-spinout deals. GC publishes their name as a co-author of the investment case on Engram (, 2026-06-23), Modal (, 2026-05-21) and Spiral (, 2025-09-11). GC is lead or co-lead on all three. Engram was a first round, which means they were in at inception on a Chris Ré lab spinout. What constrains them. On every deal the owner cares about, they appear next to an MD or the CEO. Quentin Clark is on 8 of them, Marc Bhargava on 5 and Hemant Taneja on 3 (GC portfolio pages, see Portfolio). The deals where they are the only GC name are smaller and further from the lens: Highlight, Sapien, Accordance, Anagram and Bandana. Independent lead authority on large AI rounds is not shown by any source. Correction to the premise (River AI). River AI's own announcement names only Hemant Taneja and Marc Bhargava for GC, and GC's River story is a Taneja interview. Rimpel's River credit is a portfolio-page tag only. Treat it as \"on the deal team\", not \"sourced it\". What they'd never do. Nothing sourced. They have no public writing beyond co-authored firm memos, and no podcasts were found.", "score": 11.0, "n": 8, "nk": 2, "nn": 1, "key": {"t": "Strongest personal credit: co-bylined GC's Engram memo. GC co-led the first round with Modern Capital, so Rimpel was in at inception on a Chris Re lab spinout working on memory in model weights. This is a firm statement, not personal speech.", "q": "We were lucky enough to co-lead the first round of funding with Modern Capital", "u": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram"}, "items": [{"insight": "Strongest personal credit: co-bylined GC's Engram memo. GC co-led the first round with Modern Capital, so Rimpel was in at inception on a Chris Re lab spinout working on memory in model weights. This is a firm statement, not personal speech.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "We were lucky enough to co-lead the first round of funding with Modern Capital", "source_url": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/our-investment-in-engram", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Correction to the premise: River AI's own announcement names only Taneja and Bhargava for GC. Rimpel's River credit rests only on a GC portfolio-page tag.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "General Catalyst's Hemant Taneja sat down with Babuschkin", "source_url": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/own-your-ai-experience", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Mercor: GC's page lists Rimpel first, ahead of MD Marc Bhargava, with backed since 2023, the year GC led the $3.6M seed. That makes them a plausible seed sponsor of a now $10B company. This is an inference from listing order and is unconfirmed.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "organizing human intelligence to power the AI economy", "source_url": "https://www.generalcatalyst.com/portfolio/mercor", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "low", "score": 9, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "parasvil-patel", "name": "Parasvil Patel", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins: He was one of Radical's first investment-team hires after Fund 2 launched in 2019, and he helped build the team's process and the go-to-market work for the Velocity program. He made Partner in about two years. His deals are Series A leads that come with board seats (ClimateAi, V7, Enriched Ag). He came from KKR and BCG, so he brings operating and value-creation skills rather than research credentials. What constrains him: He is not a researcher, and none of his named deals is a lab or a compute company. At Radical, the researcher-founder and frontier deals go to Jacobs, Toews, Rosenberg and Katz (see firms/radical-ventures.md). After the 2022 run (Pixxel, Enriched Ag, V7) and Graft in 2023, the only new deal publicly tied to him is Vizcom in 2025. What he'd never do (inference): Lead a pre-product mega-seed for a research lab. His pattern is to come in once there is a product and an industrial customer, and to help with operations from the board.", "score": 11.0, "n": 8, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "He spoke publicly with CentML's CEO Gennady Pekhimenko on 2023-04-24, about two months before Radical's CentML seed post (credited to Katz); a Toronto research-founder relationship, not a deal credit.", "q": "Starting a Company as an AI Researcher", "u": "https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko"}, "items": [{"insight": "He spoke publicly with CentML's CEO Gennady Pekhimenko on 2023-04-24, about two months before Radical's CentML seed post (credited to Katz); a Toronto research-founder relationship, not a deal credit.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Starting a Company as an AI Researcher", "source_url": "https://web.cs.toronto.edu/news-events/events/mscac-talks-april-2023-parasvil-patel-gennady-pekhimenko", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "On Vizcom, Radical came in third: it led the $27M B after Unusual's seed and Index's $20M A, so Patel's pattern is a later-stage lead, not a first check.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Vizcom aims to become the Figma for the physical world", "source_url": "https://radical.vc/articles/building-the-future-of-industrial-design/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "As a Principal in 2020 he wrote on coaching founder teams through crisis, a sign of the operator, coaching-style board role he plays.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "start the discussion by being vulnerable and sharing what they are feeling", "source_url": "https://betakit.com/not-fine-and-thats-ok-a-model-for-managing-teams-through-crisis/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "low", "confidence": "high", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "temasek", "name": "Temasek", "kind": "lp", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Temasek splits venture by stage. It reaches Series A/B through VC funds, and invests directly only at Series C/D and later, \"when we will come in directly and write those larger cheques\". A minimum cheque size gates every direct deal (House of Lords, Q130–Q131). As an LP it concentrates capital with a few tier-1 GPs and uses them as a pipeline that shows which companies \"are starting to break out\". The pressure that now creates new paths in is a target to lift AI exposure from 6% to 15% of the portfolio by 2031 (Malay Mail, 2026-07-08). Since 2026-04-01 fund allocation sits in a new entity, Temasek Partnership Solutions (TPS), which works with the asset manager Seviora (fintechnews.sg).", "score": 11.0, "n": 8, "nk": 0, "nn": 5, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Temasek reaches Series A/B through VC funds and only invests directly at Series C/D+, so funds are its early-stage access layer.", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "when we will come in directly and write those larger cheques", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q130", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "As an LP Temasek concentrates capital in a few tier-1 VC managers rather than spreading across many.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "we tend to concentrate our capital with them", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q132", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Its emerging-manager behaviour is seeding and anchoring local Singapore funds; the count drifted from 5-6 to 7-8 within one session.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "we have seeded and anchored about five or six VC funds", "source_url": "https://committees.parliament.uk/oralevidence/16060/html", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Q130;Q139", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "seven-stars", "name": "Seven Stars (Seven Stars Ventures)", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win. Lee has been first money in AI applications since his SV Angel years. The firm says that in \"more than 60% of our portfolio companies\" it was the founders' first investor or committed before a lead. Founder testimonials back this up for Longeye, Dialogue AI and Pomo (firm-site marketing, not independent proof). Corgi and Sage Care show him co-investing with SV Angel, so that network is still active,. What constrains them. Fund size. Fund I is $40.9M gross. The Fund II target is $65M,. At 35 to 45 companies a year, checks must be small. Implied average: under $1M (my inference). LP base. 66% of Fund I is held by funds of funds, across 31 beneficial owners. That matches StepStone and Sapphire as the named LPs. Key-person risk. Lee is the only individual named on both Form Ds and the only control person on the ADV. What they'd never do. Lead a $100M+ lab round. The stated thesis is AI applications for \"the next 8 billion\", across consumer and enterprise. Frontier labs, pre-training and chips are not the thesis. What Flapping Airplanes means for them (my inference). A frontier-lab logo for a fund that was raising Fund II during 2026 (Form D filed 2026-04-06). It is not a pre-training thesis bet.", "score": 11.0, "n": 7, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Two-thirds of Fund I is owned by funds of funds (StepStone and Sapphire named at launch), across only 31 beneficial owners with a $10k minimum. Fund-of-funds programmes, not endowments directly, anchored this first-time AI manager.", "q": "66%", "u": "https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/336685/PDF/336685.pdf"}, "items": [{"insight": "Two-thirds of Fund I is owned by funds of funds (StepStone and Sapphire named at launch), across only 31 beneficial owners with a $10k minimum. Fund-of-funds programmes, not endowments directly, anchored this first-time AI manager.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "66%", "source_url": "https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/336685/PDF/336685.pdf", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "Schedule D 7.B.(1) Q15(a)", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Fund II Form D filed for $65M (vs $40M Fund I) with nothing sold at filing, about six months before the Flapping Airplanes Series A closed. The frontier-lab logo lands mid Fund II raise (timing inference).", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Seven Stars Ventures GP II", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2127148/000212714826000001/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The firm site describes Flapping Airplanes with its seed-era data-efficiency framing, not the Series A megakernel/compute story. This suggests a light, late touch on the deal rather than deep diligence (inference).", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Frontier data efficiency lab", "source_url": "https://www.sevenstars.vc/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "bryan-offutt", "name": "Bryan Offutt", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he wins. He is a technical, infra-native evaluator. Index's own story of the Lightning deal has him judging the founders on the \"elegance\" of their code. He also has the Index/Volpi network behind him. As a result, Hanabi gets allocation in oversubscribed AI rounds (pre-launch Applied Compute, Index-led Simile and LiveKit) without leading. What constrains him. Hanabi is a new fund (2025, aggregator) that has never been seen leading. Its check sizes and fund size are undisclosed. He has almost no public voice: no firm-site posts or podcast transcripts were found. What he'd never do (inferred, not sourced). Price or lead a mega-round. Every Hanabi appearance found is \"with participation from\".", "score": 11.0, "n": 6, "nk": 1, "nn": 2, "key": {"t": "Offutt, not Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas (whom Index's page tags), led Index's $20M Adaptive ML seed months before leaving Index.", "q": "the Index Ventures partner who led the investment into Adaptive", "u": "https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/"}, "items": [{"insight": "Offutt, not Carlos Gonzalez-Cadenas (whom Index's page tags), led Index's $20M Adaptive ML seed months before leaving Index.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "the Index Ventures partner who led the investment into Adaptive", "source_url": "https://fortune.com/2024/03/11/adaptive-startup-funding-falcon-ai-models-team-rlhf-rlaif/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Hanabi Capital, a recurring unidentified co-investor across Applied Compute/Cognition/Simile/Netic in this repo, is run by ex-Index partners Offutt and Mike Volpi plus Ishani Thakur.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "Bryan Offutt, Ishani Thakur & Mike Volpi of Hanabi", "source_url": "https://traversal.com/blog/launch-announcement", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Hanabi participates in rounds led by Offutt's and Volpi's old firm, Index (LiveKit C; also Simile A), suggesting the Index relationship persists post-departure.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "joined by Salesforce Ventures, Hanabi Capital, and our longtime supporters", "source_url": "https://livekit.com/blog/livekit-series-c/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "medium", "score": 10, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "crv", "name": "CRV (formerly Charles River Ventures; older SEC entities are \"Charles River Partnership\")", "kind": "firm", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why they win. CRV is a brand-name seed and Series A lead. Its homepage headline is \"Leading Seed and Series A Rounds Since 1970\". It goes in early: on lab deals CRV often \"partners\" at or before inception. Its portfolio page dates Dyna Robotics as partnered Aug 2024, before its Sep 2024 founding, and Reflection AI as founded and partnered in the same month (Feb 2024). The hidden fact: the AI-lab engine left. The crv.com profiles of Max Gazor and Jon Auerbach both say they \"Retired from making new investments for us in 2025\",. Gazor founded Striker Venture Partners (Form D: $165M sold to 12 investors, amended 2025-10-30). Striker's site lists Reflection AI and Dyna Robotics among his board relationships. Brian Zhan, described there as a former CRV Principal and board observer, lists Reflection, Skild, Periodic Labs, Dyna, Lepton and Voyage as his founder relationships \"include[ing] companies he partnered with at CRV\" (paraphrase). Matan Lamdan, CRV's Israel cyber investor (Trail, Vega, Kela), went with them. crv.com/team/brian-zhan now returns 404. The partnership shrank. The Form D signatories tell the story: | Filing | Date | Signatories | |---|---|---| | CRV XIX ($1B offering) | 2022-01-24 | Auerbach, Bicer, Garratt, Gazor, Gur | | CRV Select II-V | 2024-05-17 | Auerbach, Bicer, Gazor, Gur, Christian | | CRV XX ($750M offering) | 2025-08-01 | Bicer, Gur and Reid Christian only | The flagship target fell 25% from XIX to XX. What constrains them. A $750M early-stage fund cannot anchor a $500M+ lab seed. CRV's lab exposure now means participating, not leading (Flapping's A, Reflection's B). The partner who backed Flapping is most plausibly Reid Christian. His Form D board seats are all dev-tools and infra SaaS, with no frontier lab. What they'd never do (inferred). Lead a large late-stage growth round. CRV has positioned itself as an early-stage-only firm since 1970 (homepage). The Select II growth vehicle had a $500M offering in 2022, but I found no successor Select fund on EDGAR (filings checked 2026-11-10).", "score": 11.0, "n": 5, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "CRV's lab-sourcing GPs stopped new CRV investing in 2025: Gazor and Auerbach are marked retired from new investments, and Gazor now runs Striker. The firm behind Reflection, Skild and Dyna is not the one in today's lab rounds.", "type": "crux", "evidence_quote": "Retired from making new investments for us in 2025", "source_url": "https://www.crv.com/team/max-gazor", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "The CRV XX flagship ($750M offering, first sale 2025-07-18, 80 investors at filing) has only three GP signatories (Bicer, Gur, Christian), down from five on CRV XIX's $1B offering in 2022.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "totalOfferingAmount 750000000", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2077305/000101297525000415/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "CRV runs small single-purpose vehicles alongside its main funds: Select II-V ($20M, 4 investors, 2024) and XIX-C ($6.5M, 2 investors, $1.5M minimum, 2026-07-22). Neither names its target company.", "type": "lp-mechanics", "evidence_quote": "minimumInvestmentAccepted 1500000", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2145391/000101297526000699/primary_doc.xml", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ribbit-capital", "name": "Ribbit Capital", "kind": "firm", "fit": "high", "crux": "Why it wins: Founder trust and speed. In January 2021 it wired $500M to Robinhood overnight on an SVB overdraft ILB EP.401 transcript. It also has a dense alumni graph from Stripe, Robinhood, Revolut and Figure that now produces AI founders. Constraints: Its sourcing edge is fintech. In frontier AI it usually comes in after Sequoia, Kleiner, Founders Fund or Index have set the price. The fund base is also smaller than in 2021 (see Funds). What it would never do: Diversify for its own sake. Malka: \"If you don't have conviction, you end up diversifying\" (same transcript). It also wouldn't take LPs who lead with track-record questions (see people/micky-malka.md).", "score": 11.0, "n": 5, "nk": 0, "nn": 3, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Ribbit's flagship fund size peaked in 2021 and has roughly halved since. Form D offering amounts: I $90M (2012), II $125M, III $220M, IV $300M, V $420M, VI $420M, VII $1.155B (2021, 298 investors, fully sold), X $800M (2022, $798.8M sold), Y $500M (2025, no sale reported), Z $565M ($561.9M sold to 266 investors by the 2026-05-13 amendment). No VIII or IX was found in EDGAR. Its AI push is being funded from smaller vehicles than its 2021 peak.", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "totalOfferingAmount 565000000 ... totalNumberAlreadyInvested 266", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=0002107959&type=D", "source_type": "filing", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Ribbit's AI portfolio is built on its own fintech founder graph. Harmonic is co-founded by Robinhood's Vlad Tenev. Navra is Mike Cagney's (Figure). Campfire's founder came from Invoice2go. CoPlane, Multiplier and SafetyKit are ex-Stripe, and Nevis and Outpost are ex-Revolut. Ribbit's own portfolio page lists 'Vlad T.' under both Robinhood and Harmonic.", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "Harmonic Tudor A., Vlad T.", "source_url": "https://ribbitcap.com/rebels", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Ribbit sent LPs a dedicated note on Anthropic's Opus 4.5 in Feb 2026, separate from its quarterly Pulse. It treated a model release as a portfolio-level event. The note itself is not public.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Ribbit Pulse: Opus 4.5 Moment - Feb '26", "source_url": "https://ribbitcap.com/perspective", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "ineffable-intelligence", "name": "Ineffable Intelligence", "kind": "company", "fit": "high", "crux": "It's the archetype of the founder-led mega-seed: pre-product, priced on one researcher's track record (AlphaGo, AlphaZero, AlphaStar), with strategic and sovereign money in the same round as the top-tier leads. For THESIS.md, the useful signal is how it was assembled: a small fund got the first check through two years of relationship-building, then the mega-leads priced the round.", "score": 11.0, "n": 5, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "First money came from a sub-$250M Seattle fund, not the mega-round leads: Flying Fish wrote Ineffable's first check after partner Heather Gorham approached Silver in early 2024, roughly two years before the company existed. Gorham later joined Ineffable as a cofounder (compute, fundraising, operations).", "q": "allowing Flying Fish to write the first check for Silver's new company", "u": "https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html"}, "items": [{"insight": "First money came from a sub-$250M Seattle fund, not the mega-round leads: Flying Fish wrote Ineffable's first check after partner Heather Gorham approached Silver in early 2024, roughly two years before the company existed. Gorham later joined Ineffable as a cofounder (compute, fundraising, operations).", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "allowing Flying Fish to write the first check for Silver's new company", "source_url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Gorham's approach to Silver came before he had left DeepMind and was framed as positioning Flying Fish to invest if he spun out; Fortune credits it with getting Flying Fish in on the ground floor.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "had approached Silver in early 2024", "source_url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/exclusive-ineffable-intelligence-adds-six-103555324.html", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Lead structure changed during the raise: in February the FT reported Sequoia alone as lead at a ~$4B valuation; by close (2026-04-27) Lightspeed had joined as co-lead and the valuation rose to $5.1B.", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "is leading the round", "source_url": "https://siliconangle.com/?p=731373", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "medium", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 9, "sig": 0}]}, {"slug": "willem-van-den-bosch", "name": "Willem van den Bosch", "kind": "person", "fit": "medium", "crux": "Why he matters: Triatomic is small (about $346M regulatory AUM) and partner-heavy. Its team page lists three GPs, one Principal (him) and one Associate (triatomic.ai). Below the GPs, he is the investor most likely to run diligence and syndicate calls on the compute cluster: MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis and iPronics (see firms/triatomic.md). What he brings: A quantitative, operating-finance background rather than research or engineering. At a Los Angeles long/short fund, black-and-white Capital, he used alternative data across consumer goods, trucking and logistics, mobile gaming and internet. At DoorDash he did driver-supply and logistics modeling before the IPO (Soma bio, archived). Nothing found shows a technical background in chips or ML research. Constraints: He has no public deal attribution, no public writing and no X presence. Public Triatomic announcements name the GPs (Zhou, Huber, Hochberg), never him, so his personal sourcing record can't be checked. He was hired directly as Principal (2024), and no promotion has been announced. What he'd never do (inferred from the firm): sub-$5M apps-layer seeds. Triatomic's entry checks are $10–20M.", "score": 11.0, "n": 5, "nk": 0, "nn": 4, "key": null, "items": [{"insight": "Soma's own (now 404, archived) bio described his Soma role as growth-stage, while Triatomic's bio recasts the same tenure as leading early-stage deals", "type": "constraint", "evidence_quote": "joined Soma Capital in 2021 and focuses on growth-stage investments", "source_url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Pre-venture edge is hedge-fund alternative-data analysis (black-and-white Capital, LA long/short), not technical AI/chip expertise", "type": "how-they-decide", "evidence_quote": "used alternative data to inform investment decisions", "source_url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20221201043541/https://www.somacap.com/member/willem/", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "medium", "confidence": "high", "score": 11, "sig": 1}, {"insight": "Coworker (AI agent, Triatomic portfolio) had Soma Capital, his prior firm, in its pre-seed before Triatomic (Huber) led the $13M seed in May 2025; his personal role is undocumented", "type": "deal-origin", "evidence_quote": "They join pre-seed investors Soma Capital, Focal VC, Mischief, and Karman Ventures", "source_url": "https://pulse2.com/coworker-ai-13-million-raised-for-ai-agent-research-company/amp/", "source_type": "press", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "medium", "score": 11, "sig": 1}]}, {"slug": "mikowai-ashwill", "name": "Mikowai Ashwill", "kind": "other", "fit": "high", "crux": "", "score": 11.0, "n": 4, "nk": 2, "nn": 0, "key": {"t": "The investor who led Ribbit's Decagon position (a Series C in 2025) has left for Spark Capital's early-stage team. Ribbit's best AI-apps sourcing came from a junior who then left.", "q": "joining from Ribbit, where he led the firm's investment in Decagon", "u": "https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/mikowai-ashwill"}, "items": [{"insight": "The investor who led Ribbit's Decagon position (a Series C in 2025) has left for Spark Capital's early-stage team. Ribbit's best AI-apps sourcing came from a junior who then left.", "type": "relationship", "evidence_quote": "joining from Ribbit, where he led the firm's investment in Decagon", "source_url": "https://www.sparkcapital.com/team-members/mikowai-ashwill", "source_type": "firm-site", "timestamp_in_source": "", "non_obvious": "high", "lens_fit": "high", "confidence": "high", "score": 12, "sig": 2}, {"insight": "Ashwill was 'Investor, San Francisco' in Spark's Feb 2026 LP deck and is titled General Partner on the site by Nov 2026: promoted within ~9 months, around the Standard Intelligence A he co-led (Apr 2026).", "type": "timing", "evidence_quote": "Miko Ashwill Investor, San Francisco", "source_url": "https://nic.nebraska.gov/sites/default/files/doc/4c%20Spark%20Capital%20Overview%20-%20Public%20Version